__d(function(e,t,a,i,n,s,o){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(s,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(s,"default",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return l}});var r=t(o[0]);var l=[{num:1,title:'What Is Corporate Finance?',xp:30,steps:[{type:'hookOpener',title:'The $118 billion question',fact:'Apple\'s business generated $118 billion of cash from operations in fiscal 2024. Deciding what to do with that pile is one executive\'s entire job.',outcome:'By the end you\'ll be able to name the three decisions every company makes with its cash, spot them in real headlines, and make the call yourself as a CFO.',visualDescription:'A single headline metric showing the size of Apple\'s yearly cash generation',visual:{type:'metric',title:'Apple\'s yearly cash machine',value:'$118B',detail:'cash from operations, fiscal 2024',footer:'That is roughly $324 million every single day',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'stakesCard',without:{label:'Allocate cash badly',detail:'Toys R Us was loaded with $7.5 billion of buyout debt and had no plan to reinvest in its stores. In 2018 it closed about 800 US locations and roughly 30,000 workers lost their jobs.'},withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Toys R Us, 2018',value:'800',detail:'US stores closed after debt payments left nothing to reinvest',sentiment:'negative'},with:{label:'Allocate cash well',detail:'Microsoft poured its profits into Azure cloud computing. The same company, making smarter cash decisions, grew about 10x in a decade.'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Microsoft market value',points:[{label:'2014',value:'$300B'},{label:'2018',value:'$780B'},{label:'2021',value:'$2.5T'},{label:'2024',value:'$3.1T'}],trend:'up',footer:'Profits reinvested into Azure cloud',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The core idea',title:'Every company makes the same three decisions',paragraphs:['Corporate finance sounds complicated, but it boils down to three questions about cash. First, the investment decision: what should we build or buy? A new factory, a research lab, another company.','Second, the financing decision: how do we pay for it? Borrow money (debt) or sell ownership pieces (equity). Third, the dividend decision: when cash piles up, do we reinvest it or hand it back to shareholders through dividends and buybacks?','That\'s the whole field. Every CFO on earth is just answering these three questions, over and over, with real money.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'debt',definition:'Borrowed money the company must pay back with interest.',example:'Disney selling bonds to raise $6 billion.'},{term:'equity',definition:'Ownership pieces of the company, sold as shares of stock.',example:'Rivian selling new shares to fund its truck factories.'},{term:'buyback',definition:'When a company buys its own shares back from investors, returning cash and shrinking the share count.',example:'Apple announced a $110 billion buyback in 2024.'}],diagramDescription:'Cash flows out of daily operations and into the three decision buckets: invest, finance, return',diagramVisual:{type:'flow',title:'Where the cash goes',nodes:[{label:'Operations',value:'$118B in'},{label:'Invest',value:'build or buy'},{label:'Finance',value:'borrow or repay'},{label:'Return',value:'dividends, buybacks'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'microCheck',question:'A company raises money by selling bonds to investors. Which of the three decisions is that?',options:['The financing decision','The investment decision'],correct:0,explanationCorrect:'Yes. Selling bonds is borrowing, and choosing between debt and equity is exactly what the financing decision covers.',explanationWrong:'Not quite. The investment decision is about what to build or buy. Selling bonds is about where the money comes from, which is the financing decision.'},{type:'keyTermsCards',terms:[{term:'capital allocation',definition:'Deciding where a company\'s cash goes across the three decisions.',example:'Apple splitting its cash between chip research and stock buybacks.',sentence:'Warren Buffett says capital allocation is the CEO\'s most important job.'},{term:'CFO',definition:'Chief financial officer, the executive who manages the company\'s money and leads the three decisions.',example:'Apple\'s CFO decides how much of the $118 billion goes to buybacks each year.',sentence:'The CFO presented three options for the spare cash to the board.'},{term:'shareholder value',definition:'The total worth a company delivers to its owners through a rising stock price and cash payouts.',example:'Microsoft\'s decade of Azure investment multiplied shareholder value about tenfold.',sentence:'A buyback only builds shareholder value if the stock is worth buying.'},{term:'cost of capital',definition:'The return a company must earn on its cash to satisfy the lenders and investors who supplied it.',example:'If a company borrows at 6%, a new project must earn more than 6% to be worth doing.',sentence:'The plant was rejected because its returns fell below the cost of capital.'}]},{type:'quiz',question:'Netflix spends $17 billion in a year producing new shows and films. Which decision is that?',options:['The dividend decision','The financing decision','The investment decision'],correct:2,explanation:'Content is what Netflix builds. Spending cash to create shows is choosing what to build or buy, which is the investment decision. It would be a financing decision if Netflix were borrowing the money, and a dividend decision if it were handing cash back to shareholders.'},{type:'concept',emoji:'\ud83d\udca1',title:'Every headline is one of three moves',body:'A new Tesla factory, an Apple buyback, a Disney bond sale, a Coca-Cola dividend check: strip away the press release and each one is just the investment, financing, or dividend decision in action.'},{type:'scenario',situation:'You are the CFO of a snack company sitting on $10 billion of spare cash. The business runs fine, but it still carries old loans charging 9% interest. Your team pitches three uses for the money. The board wants your call.',choices:[{label:'Build a $10 billion new plant',outcome:'The plant is projected to earn about 6% a year in a market that is barely growing. That is below the 9% you are paying lenders, so every year the project runs, it destroys value. The board is not impressed.',correct:!1},{label:'Pay down the 9% loans',outcome:'Every dollar of debt you erase stops costing you 9 cents a year, guaranteed. A risk-free 9% beats the uncertain returns of the other two options, so this is the highest-value move on the table. The board approves.',correct:!0},{label:'Buy back $10 billion of stock',outcome:'Shareholders get cash today, but the company keeps paying 9% interest on its loans forever. You returned money while an expensive debt kept draining profits. Analysts call it backwards.',correct:!1}]},{type:'misconceptions',title:'Common myths',subtitle:'What people get wrong about company money',items:[{myth:'Debt is always bad for a company.',truth:'Cheap debt used well creates value.',explanation:'In the 2010s Apple borrowed billions at interest rates around 3% and put the money to work in a business earning far more than that. Debt only kills when it costs more than the cash can earn, like the Toys R Us buyout.'},{myth:'Buybacks are money thrown away, since the company gets nothing back.',truth:'A buyback returns cash to owners by shrinking the share count.',explanation:'With fewer shares outstanding, each remaining share owns a bigger slice of the same company. It is the dividend decision wearing a different outfit.'},{myth:'The CFO is just the head accountant.',truth:'The CFO makes the allocation calls that decide the company\'s future.',explanation:'Accountants record where the money went. The CFO decides where it goes next: which projects get funded, how they are paid for, and how much goes back to shareholders.'}]},{type:'trueFalse',statements:[{s:'The dividend decision includes stock buybacks, not just dividend checks.',a:!0},{s:'A project earning 6% is a good use of cash for a company whose money costs it 9%.',a:!1},{s:'The investment decision is about which stocks the company should buy in the stock market.',a:!1},{s:'Microsoft grew about 10x in a decade largely by reinvesting profits into cloud computing.',a:!0}]},{type:'summaryCards',lessonTitle:'What Is Corporate Finance?',cards:[{takeaway:'Corporate finance is three decisions',detail:'Invest (what to build or buy), finance (debt or equity to pay for it), and dividend (reinvest or return the cash). Every headline a company makes is one of these moves.'},{takeaway:'Cash has to earn its keep',detail:'Money always has a cost of capital. A project only creates value if it earns more than the money costs, which is why paying off a 9% loan can beat building a 6% plant.'},{takeaway:'Allocation is life or death',detail:'Toys R Us drowned under $7.5 billion of debt with no reinvestment plan. Microsoft reinvested into Azure and grew from about $300 billion to over $3 trillion. Same game, opposite calls.'}]}],challenge:[{question:'A company raises $500 million by issuing new shares of stock to investors. Which of the three decisions is that?',options:['The investment decision','The financing decision','The dividend decision'],correct:1,explanation:'Issuing shares is raising money from equity investors, and choosing where the money comes from is the financing decision. It would be an investment decision if the company were spending that cash on projects, and a dividend decision if it were returning cash to shareholders.'},{question:'Amazon spends $48 billion in a year building new warehouses and data centers. Which decision is that?',options:['The financing decision','The investment decision','The dividend decision'],correct:1,explanation:'Warehouses and data centers are what Amazon builds. Spending cash on assets the business will use is choosing what to build or buy, which is the investment decision. It would be a financing decision if Amazon were borrowing the money, and a dividend decision if it were handing cash back to shareholders.'},{question:'Apple pays out $15 billion in cash to its shareholders during the year. Which decision is that?',options:['The dividend decision','The investment decision','The financing decision'],correct:0,explanation:'Handing cash back to shareholders is exactly what the dividend decision covers. It would be an investment decision if Apple were spending the cash on products or facilities, and a financing decision if it were raising money rather than paying it out.'},{question:'A startup takes out a $20 million bank loan to fund its operations. Which decision is that?',options:['The investment decision','The dividend decision','The financing decision'],correct:2,explanation:'Taking a loan is borrowing, and choosing between debt and equity to raise money is the financing decision. What the startup later spends the money on would be the investment decision, and any cash paid back to owners would be the dividend decision.'}]},{num:2,title:'Financial Statements: The Language of Business',xp:35,steps:[{type:'hookOpener',title:'Profitable until the day it died',fact:'82% of small businesses that fail blame cash flow problems, and many of them were reporting a profit right up until the day they closed.',outcome:'By the end you\'ll be able to name what each of the three financial statements shows, spot a company that is profitable on paper but starving for cash, and calculate a profit margin.',visualDescription:'A comparison of one company\'s reported profit against the cash it could actually spend',visual:{type:'comparison',title:'Same company, same month',rows:[{label:'Profit on paper',value:'$10M',percent:100},{label:'Cash it can spend',value:'$0.5M',percent:5}],footer:'Payroll gets paid with cash, not with paper profit',sentiment:'negative'}},{type:'stakesCard',without:{label:'Watching only profit',detail:'The income statement says everything is fine, so nobody checks the bank account. When rent, payroll, and suppliers all come due in the same week, the money is not there.'},with:{label:'Watching cash flow too',detail:'You see cash leaving faster than it arrives months before it becomes a crisis. That is enough warning to collect what customers owe, cut spending, or borrow on good terms.'},withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'The blind spot',value:'$0',detail:'cash left when the bills come due',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'The early warning',value:'6 months',detail:'of runway spotted before trouble hits',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The three statements',title:'One company, three questions',paragraphs:['Every public company tells its story with three documents. The income statement answers: did we make a profit over this period? The balance sheet answers: what do we own and owe at this exact moment? The cash flow statement answers: how much cash actually came in and went out?','Each one can look healthy while another looks sick. That is why investors read all three together instead of trusting any single number.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'income statement',definition:'A report of revenue minus expenses over a period, ending in profit or loss.',example:'Apple\'s 2023 income statement showed about $383 billion in revenue and $97 billion in profit.'},{term:'balance sheet',definition:'A snapshot of what a company owns and owes at one moment in time.',example:'A balance sheet dated December 31 lists that day\'s cash, inventory, and debts.'},{term:'cash flow statement',definition:'A record of actual cash moving in and out during a period.',example:'A company that collected $80M and spent $95M shows negative $15M in cash flow.'}],diagramDescription:'The three statements side by side, each labeled with the question it answers',diagramVisual:{type:'grid',title:'The three statements',items:[{label:'Income statement',value:'Profit over a period',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Balance sheet',value:'Owns and owes right now',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Cash flow statement',value:'Cash in, cash out',color:'#10B981'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'match',title:'Match the statement to its job',pairs:[['Income statement','Profit or loss over a period'],['Balance sheet','What it owns and owes at one moment'],['Cash flow statement','Actual cash moving in and out']]},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The aha',title:'Profitable and broke at the same time',paragraphs:['Meet TechFit, a fitness app company. It billed customers $100M this year and spent $90M, so its income statement shows a $10M profit. Sounds great.','But here is the catch: $120M of what it billed is still sitting in unpaid invoices. Customers owe the money, TechFit just cannot spend it yet. The income statement counts a sale when it is earned, not when the cash lands.','So TechFit is profitable on paper and cannot cover next month\'s payroll. Profit is an opinion shaped by accounting rules. Cash is a fact.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'receivables',definition:'Money customers owe a company for sales already made.',example:'TechFit\'s $120M in unpaid invoices are receivables: real sales, no cash yet.'},{term:'profit margin',definition:'Profit divided by revenue, shown as a percent.',example:'$10M profit on $100M revenue is a 10% profit margin.'}],diagramDescription:'TechFit\'s revenue, expenses, and paper profit dwarfed by the pile of unpaid invoices',diagramVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'TechFit this year',rows:[{label:'Revenue billed',value:'$100M',percent:83},{label:'Expenses',value:'$90M',percent:75},{label:'Profit on paper',value:'$10M',percent:8},{label:'Stuck in unpaid invoices',value:'$120M',percent:100}],footer:'The profit is real on paper, but the cash is trapped in receivables',sentiment:'negative'}},{type:'microCheck',question:'TechFit shows a $10M profit, but $120M of its sales sit in unpaid invoices. Can it count on that profit to make payroll this month?',options:['Yes, a reported profit means the cash is in the bank','No, the profit can be stuck in invoices customers have not paid'],correct:1,explanationCorrect:'Right. The income statement counts sales when they are earned, not when cash arrives. Payroll needs actual cash, which is why the cash flow statement matters most.',explanationWrong:'Not quite. Profit is an accounting result, not money in the bank. TechFit\'s $10M profit exists on paper while the cash sits in customers\' hands as unpaid invoices.'},{type:'calculator',title:'Compute TechFit\'s profit margin',formula:'profit = revenue - expenses, then margin = profit / revenue x 100. Revenue is $100M and expenses are $90M.',blanks:[{label:'Profit in millions of dollars',answer:10,tolerance:0},{label:'Profit margin in percent',answer:10,tolerance:.5}]},{type:'misconceptions',title:'Common myths',subtitle:'What people get wrong about profit and cash',items:[{myth:'Profit and cash are the same thing.',truth:'Profit counts sales when they are earned, even if the cash arrives months later or never.',explanation:'A $500 sale on credit boosts profit today, but the bank account does not move until the customer actually pays.'},{myth:'A profitable company cannot go broke.',truth:'It absolutely can, and it happens constantly.',explanation:'Bills, payroll, and rent are paid with cash. If cash runs out while profit sits in receivables, the company fails anyway. That is the story behind most cash flow failures.'},{myth:'The balance sheet shows how much a company earned this year.',truth:'The balance sheet is a snapshot of one moment, not a video of the year.',explanation:'Earnings over a period live on the income statement. The balance sheet only shows what the company owns and owes on a single date.'}]},{type:'keyTermsCards',terms:[{term:'Income statement',definition:'The report of revenue minus expenses over a period, ending in profit or loss.',example:'Revenue of $100M minus $90M of expenses gives a $10M profit.',sentence:'The income statement says TechFit made $10M this year.'},{term:'Balance sheet',definition:'A snapshot of everything a company owns and owes at one moment.',example:'On December 31 it lists cash, inventory, buildings, and every debt.',sentence:'The balance sheet showed more debt than the company owned in assets.'},{term:'Cash flow statement',definition:'The record of actual cash that came in and went out during a period.',example:'Collected $80M, spent $95M, so cash flow was negative $15M.',sentence:'The cash flow statement revealed the profit was not turning into money.'},{term:'Receivables',definition:'Money customers owe for sales the company has already made.',example:'TechFit\'s $120M in unpaid customer invoices.',sentence:'Rising receivables meant sales were growing but cash was not.'}]},{type:'scenario',situation:'You run a sneaker resale page. This month you sold $600 worth of pairs, but $400 of that is IOUs from friends who will pay you next month. You have $200 of actual cash. On Friday a rare pair drops for $350, cash up front.',choices:[{label:'Buy it Friday, you made $600 in sales this month',outcome:'Your $200 does not cover the $350. You are short at the register because $400 of your sales is still in your friends\' pockets, not yours.',correct:!1},{label:'Check your actual cash first and skip this drop',outcome:'Smart. Your income statement says $600, but your cash flow says $200. You wait, collect the IOUs next month, and buy the next drop without borrowing.',correct:!0},{label:'Promise the seller you will pay next week when the IOUs come in',outcome:'The seller wants cash up front and sells to someone else. Counting money you have not collected as money you can spend is exactly how businesses get burned.',correct:!1}]},{type:'trueFalse',statements:[{s:'A company can report a profit and still run out of cash.',a:!0},{s:'The balance sheet covers a period of time, like a full year.',a:!1},{s:'A sale can count as revenue before the money reaches the bank account.',a:!0},{s:'If cash flow is negative for one period, the company must be unprofitable.',a:!1}]},{type:'summaryCards',lessonTitle:'Reading a Company\'s Financials',cards:[{takeaway:'Three statements, three questions',detail:'The income statement shows profit over a period, the balance sheet shows what is owned and owed at one moment, and the cash flow statement shows cash actually moving.'},{takeaway:'Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact',detail:'Accounting rules count sales when earned. A company like TechFit can post a $10M profit while $120M sits in unpaid invoices it cannot spend.'},{takeaway:'The cash flow statement is the lie detector',detail:'If profit keeps rising but cash keeps falling, something is wrong. 82% of failed small businesses blame cash flow, not lack of profit.'},{takeaway:'Profit margin measures efficiency',detail:'Profit divided by revenue: $10M on $100M of revenue is a 10% margin, meaning the company keeps 10 cents of every sales dollar.'}]}],challenge:[{question:'GreenGrid reports a $25M profit this quarter, but $200M of its sales are sitting in unpaid customer invoices. Can it rely on that profit to cover this month\'s payroll?',options:['Yes, a reported profit means the cash is already in the bank','No, the profit can be stuck in invoices customers have not paid','Yes, as long as the profit margin is above 10 percent','No, because profit can only be spent at the end of the fiscal year'],correct:1,explanation:'The income statement records sales when they are earned, not when cash arrives. GreenGrid\'s $25M profit exists on paper while the cash sits in customers\' hands as unpaid invoices. Payroll needs actual cash, which is why the cash flow statement matters most.'},{question:'SwiftCart shows a $4M profit, yet $75M of its sales remain in unpaid invoices. Which statement should its CFO check to know if the company can make payroll this week?',options:['The income statement, since it shows the profit','The cash flow statement, since payroll requires actual cash on hand','The balance sheet footnotes, since they list employee salaries','No statement is needed, since profit guarantees cash'],correct:1,explanation:'Profit is an accounting result, not money in the bank. Sales are counted when earned, so SwiftCart\'s $4M profit can exist entirely on paper while cash is tied up in unpaid invoices. Only the cash flow statement reveals whether real cash is available for payroll.'},{question:'Nordic Fitness earned revenue of $80M with expenses of $68M last year. What is its profit margin?',options:['12 percent','15 percent','18 percent','85 percent'],correct:1,explanation:'Profit equals revenue minus expenses: $80M minus $68M gives $12M. Margin equals profit divided by revenue times 100: $12M divided by $80M gives 15 percent. A common mistake is stopping at the $12M profit figure and reading it as 12 percent.'},{question:'PulseWear posted revenue of $250M and expenses of $230M. What are its profit and profit margin?',options:['$20M profit and an 8 percent margin','$20M profit and a 20 percent margin','$30M profit and a 12 percent margin','$20M profit and a 92 percent margin'],correct:0,explanation:'Profit equals revenue minus expenses: $250M minus $230M gives $20M. Margin equals profit divided by revenue times 100: $20M divided by $250M gives 8 percent. The margin is measured against revenue, not assumed to equal the dollar profit figure.'}]},{num:3,title:'Time Value of Money',xp:35,steps:[{type:'hookOpener',title:'The $24 island',fact:'In 1626, Peter Minuit bought Manhattan from the Lenape for goods worth about $24. Invested at 7% a year, that $24 would be worth over $12 trillion today, more than all the real estate in Manhattan combined.',outcome:'By the end you\'ll be able to turn any future dollar amount into what it\'s worth today, and use that to judge whether a deal is worth taking.',visualDescription:'A line showing $24 in 1626 compounding at 7% into trillions by 2026',visual:{type:'line',title:'$24 invested at 7% since 1626',points:[{label:'1626',value:'$24'},{label:'1726',value:'$21K'},{label:'1826',value:'$18M'},{label:'1926',value:'$16B'},{label:'2026',value:'$13T'}],trend:'up',footer:'Time turns small money into huge money. That power is why timing matters.',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'stakesCard',without:{label:'Ignore the time cost of money',detail:'A company pays $1,000 today for cash arriving in 3 years that is only worth $751 in today\'s terms. It overpays for project after project and slowly bleeds value.'},with:{label:'Discount before deciding',detail:'A company shrinks every future payout back to today\'s dollars first. It only funds projects that beat its cost of capital, so each dollar invested creates value.'},withoutVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'Paying $1,000 for $751',rows:[{label:'Price paid today',value:'$1,000',percent:100},{label:'Today\'s value received',value:'$751',percent:75}],footer:'Every deal like this destroys $249',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'Only fund what beats the hurdle',rows:[{label:'Project return',value:'20%',percent:20},{label:'Cost of capital',value:'10%',percent:10}],footer:'Return clears the hurdle, so the project adds value',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The core idea',title:'Why today\'s dollar wins',paragraphs:['A dollar in your hand today can be invested right now. At 7%, $100 today becomes $107 in a year. So $100 promised a year from now is worth less than $100 today, because you skip a year of growth.','That is the time value of money: cash has a time cost. To compare money arriving at different times, you first drag future dollars back to today. That move is called discounting.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'time value of money',definition:'The idea that money today is worth more than the same amount later, because today\'s money can be invested and grow.',example:'$100 today beats $100 next year, since today\'s $100 can become $107.'},{term:'discounting',definition:'Shrinking a future amount back to its value today.',example:'$107 arriving in one year, discounted at 7%, is worth $100 today.'}],diagramDescription:'Money flowing from today into an investment and coming out bigger a year later',diagramVisual:{type:'flow',title:'One year at 7%',nodes:[{label:'Today',value:'$100'},{label:'Invested at 7%',value:'+$7'},{label:'In 1 year',value:'$107'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'microCheck',question:'You can take $100 today or $100 in one year. Your money can earn 7% in an index fund. Which is worth more?',options:['$100 today','$100 in one year'],correct:0,explanationCorrect:'Yes. Today\'s $100 can be invested and grow to $107 by next year, so it beats $100 arriving later.',explanationWrong:'Not quite. If you take the $100 today and invest it at 7%, you have $107 next year. Waiting for $100 means giving up that $7 of growth.'},{type:'explorable',widget:'compoundGrowthLab',title:'Replay the Manhattan trade',prompt:'Start with $24 at 7% and drag the years slider up. Notice how the curve barely moves for decades, then explodes. That explosion is exactly why future money must be discounted.',insight:'Each extra year multiplies everything that came before, so the last stretch of the curve dwarfs the first. Run it backward and the same force shrinks future dollars: money promised years from now is worth far less today.',params:{startingAmount:24,annualReturn:7,years:100,monthlyContribution:0}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The tool',title:'Present value: dragging money back to today',paragraphs:['To find what future money is worth now, divide by (1 + r) once for every year of waiting, where r is the discount rate. The answer is the present value.','Take $1,000 arriving in 3 years with a 10% discount rate. Dividing by 1.10 three times gives $909, then $826, then $751. So that $1,000 is worth about $751 today. Put $751 in an account earning 10% and it grows right back to $1,000 in 3 years.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'present value',definition:'What a future amount of money is worth today after discounting.',example:'$1,000 in 3 years at a 10% discount rate has a present value of about $751.'},{term:'discount rate',definition:'The yearly rate used to shrink future money back to today, usually the return you could earn elsewhere.',example:'If your money could earn 10% invested, you discount future cash at 10%.'}],diagramDescription:'Bars showing $1,000 shrinking each year it is discounted back toward today',diagramVisual:{type:'bars',title:'$1,000 discounted at 10% per year',items:[{label:'In 3 years',value:1e3,displayValue:'$1,000'},{label:'In 2 years',value:909,displayValue:'$909'},{label:'In 1 year',value:826,displayValue:'$826'},{label:'Today',value:751,displayValue:'$751'}],footer:'Each step back divides by 1.10',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'calculator',title:'Discount $1,000 back to today',formula:'PV = FV / (1 + r)^n, with FV = $1,000, r = 10%, n = 3 years',blanks:[{label:'First compute (1.10)^3',answer:1.331,tolerance:.01},{label:'Now PV = 1,000 / 1.331, in dollars',answer:751,tolerance:5}]},{type:'fillBlank',sentence:'To compare future money with money today, you shrink it using a ____ rate.',options:['growth','discount','tax'],correct:1},{type:'keyTermsCards',terms:[{term:'present value',definition:'What a future amount is worth today after discounting.',example:'$1,000 in 3 years at 10% has a present value of about $751.',sentence:'She computed the present value of the payout before agreeing to wait for it.'},{term:'future value',definition:'What money today grows into after earning a return for some years.',example:'$751 at 10% grows to a future value of about $1,000 in 3 years.',sentence:'The future value of his $500 summer savings at 8% surprised him.'},{term:'discount rate',definition:'The yearly rate used to pull future cash back to today, based on what your money could earn elsewhere.',example:'An investor who can earn 10% in the market discounts future cash at 10%.',sentence:'Raising the discount rate made the faraway payout look much smaller.'},{term:'cost of capital',definition:'The return a company must earn to satisfy the people funding it. Projects must beat it to be worth doing.',example:'A firm with a 10% cost of capital rejects projects returning 8%.',sentence:'The startup used its cost of capital as the hurdle for every new project.'}]},{type:'scenario',situation:'You run a sneaker resale side hustle. A supplier offers a deal: pay $1,000 today, and when the shipment sells in one year you get $1,200 back. Your other option is parking the $1,000 in an index fund you expect to earn 10%. What do you do?',choices:[{label:'Reject it, $1,200 later feels too far away',outcome:'You passed on value. The present value of $1,200 in one year at 10% is $1,200 / 1.10, about $1,091. That beats the $1,000 cost by $91 in today\'s dollars.',correct:!1},{label:'Take the deal after discounting the $1,200',outcome:'Right call. $1,200 / 1.10 is about $1,091 today, which is more than the $1,000 it costs. The deal beats your 10% alternative, so it creates about $91 of value.',correct:!0},{label:'Take it because $1,200 is simply bigger than $1,000',outcome:'Right answer, wrong reason. Comparing raw numbers across time is how companies overpay. If the payout were $1,050, it would still be bigger than $1,000 but worth only $955 today, a losing deal.',correct:!1}]},{type:'misconceptions',title:'Time Value of Money',subtitle:'What people get wrong about future money',items:[{myth:'A $1 million payout in 10 years is worth $1 million today.',truth:'At a 7% discount rate it is worth about $508,000 today.',explanation:'Dividing $1,000,000 by 1.07 ten times gives roughly $508,000. That is the amount which, invested at 7%, grows back to $1 million in 10 years. Nearly half the headline number is just the time cost of waiting.'},{myth:'If a project\'s payout is bigger than its cost, it is worth doing.',truth:'The payout only counts after it is discounted at the cost of capital.',explanation:'A project costing $1,000 that pays $1,050 in a year looks profitable. But at a 10% cost of capital that $1,050 is worth only about $955 today, so the project destroys $45 of value.'},{myth:'Discounting is only for Wall Street analysts.',truth:'Every choice between money now and money later is a discounting problem.',explanation:'Choosing a payment plan, waiting for a sale, or lending a friend $50 until next summer all involve comparing dollars across time. The math is one division per year of waiting.'}]},{type:'trueFalse',statements:[{s:'A dollar today is worth more than a dollar next year because today\'s dollar can be invested and grow.',a:!0},{s:'When the discount rate is positive, the present value of a future payment is bigger than the payment itself.',a:!1},{s:'A company\'s cost of capital is the return its projects must beat to be worth funding.',a:!0},{s:'Raising the discount rate makes future cash flows worth more today.',a:!1}]},{type:'summaryCards',lessonTitle:'Time Value of Money',cards:[{takeaway:'Money has a time cost',detail:'A dollar today beats a dollar next year because today\'s dollar can be invested. $24 compounding at 7% since 1626 would top $12 trillion.'},{takeaway:'Discount to compare across time',detail:'Divide future cash by (1 + r) for each year of waiting. $1,000 in 3 years at 10% is worth about $751 today.'},{takeaway:'Present value drives decisions',detail:'A deal is worth doing when the present value of what you get beats what you pay. $1,200 in a year at 10% is $1,091 today, so it justifies a $1,000 cost.'},{takeaway:'Beat the cost of capital',detail:'Companies discount at their cost of capital and only fund projects that clear it. Skipping this step is how businesses overpay.'}]}],challenge:[{question:'You can take $500 today or $500 in one year. Your money can earn 5% in an index fund. Which is worth more?',options:['$500 today','$500 in one year','They are worth exactly the same','It cannot be determined'],correct:0,explanation:'The $500 today can be invested at 5% and grow to $525 by next year. Waiting for $500 means giving up that $25 of growth, so money today beats the same amount later.'},{question:'You will receive $2,000 in 2 years, and your discount rate is 8%. Using PV = FV / (1 + r)^n, what is that money worth today (rounded to the nearest dollar)?',options:['$2,333','$2,000','$1,852','$1,715'],correct:3,explanation:'First compute (1.08)^2 = 1.1664. Then PV = 2,000 / 1.1664, which is about $1,715. Choosing $2,000 ignores discounting, $1,852 only discounts one year, and $2,333 compounds forward instead of discounting back.'},{question:'A payment of $1,500 arrives in 3 years and the discount rate is 6%. Using PV = FV / (1 + r)^n, what is its present value (rounded to the nearest dollar)?',options:['$1,259','$1,335','$1,500','$1,787'],correct:0,explanation:'Compute (1.06)^3 = 1.191. Then PV = 1,500 / 1.191, which is about $1,259. The answer $1,335 only discounts 2 years, $1,500 skips discounting entirely, and $1,787 grows the money forward instead of shrinking it back.'},{question:'To compare a future cash flow with money you have today, which rate do you use to shrink the future amount back to its present value?',options:['The growth rate','The discount rate','The tax rate','The exchange rate'],correct:1,explanation:'The discount rate is what you use to shrink future money into today\'s dollars. Growth rates describe how money compounds forward, and tax or exchange rates have nothing to do with converting future value to present value.'}]},{num:4,title:'Capital Structure: Debt vs. Equity',xp:35,steps:[{type:'hookOpener',title:'How Companies Raise Money',fact:'In August 2017, Amazon borrowed $16 billion in a single bond sale to help buy Whole Foods. It could have sold more of its own stock instead, but chose to go into debt on purpose.',outcome:'By the end you\'ll be able to explain the two ways companies raise money, why cheap debt can be dangerous, and why selling ownership is never free.',visualDescription:'A stacked bar showing a sample company funded 40% by debt and 60% by equity',visual:{type:'stack',title:'One company\'s money, two sources',segments:[{label:'Debt (borrowed)',percent:40,color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Equity (ownership sold)',percent:60,color:'#A451E6'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'stakesCard',without:{label:'Too much debt',detail:'Lehman Brothers borrowed about $30 for every $1 it owned. When lenders stopped rolling over its loans in 2008, it collapsed with $613 billion in debts, the largest bankruptcy in US history.'},with:{label:'The smart mix',detail:'Apple sat on $145 billion in cash in 2013 and still sold $17 billion of bonds at roughly 2% interest, because borrowing that cheaply was smarter than the alternatives.'},withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Lehman Brothers, 2008',value:'$613B',detail:'in debts when it went bankrupt',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Apple, 2013',value:'~2%',detail:'interest rate on its $17 billion bond sale',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The two doors',title:'Borrow it, or sell a piece',paragraphs:['Every dollar a company raises comes through one of two doors. Door one is debt: borrow money, usually by selling bonds, and promise to pay it back with interest no matter what.','Door two is equity: sell shares, which are small slices of ownership. There is nothing to pay back, but the new shareholders now own part of everything the company earns forever.','The blend of the two is called the capital structure. A company that is 40% debt and 60% equity built itself with $40 of loans for every $60 of ownership sold.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'capital structure',definition:'The mix of debt and equity a company uses to fund itself.',example:'A firm funded with $4M of loans and $6M of shareholder money has a 40/60 capital structure.'},{term:'bond',definition:'An IOU a company sells to investors. The company pays interest and repays the full amount on a set date.',example:'Amazon sold $16 billion of bonds in 2017 to help buy Whole Foods.'}],diagramDescription:'Money flowing into a company from two sources: lenders on one side, shareholders on the other',diagramVisual:{type:'grid',title:'Two ways in',items:[{label:'Debt',value:'Must repay, with interest',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Equity',value:'No repayment, but you sold a slice',color:'#A451E6'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'microCheck',question:'A company raises $10 million and never has to pay a cent of it back. Which door did it use?',options:['Debt','Equity'],correct:1,explanationCorrect:'Yes. Equity is sold, not borrowed. There is no repayment, because investors got ownership instead of an IOU.',explanationWrong:'Not quite. Debt always comes with a repayment promise. Money with no repayment attached means the company sold ownership, which is equity.'},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The aha',title:'Debt is cheap but dangerous. Equity is safe but expensive.',paragraphs:['Debt looks scary but is actually the cheap option. Interest rates are lower than the returns shareholders demand, and the tax code sweetens it: interest payments are tax deductible, so the government effectively pays part of the bill.','The catch is that interest is due even in a terrible year. Miss payments and lenders can push you into bankruptcy. Equity flips this: shareholders can never demand their money back, but they expect big returns and they own a slice of every future profit. Loading up on debt to boost returns is called leverage, and selling new shares that shrink everyone\'s slice is called dilution.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'leverage',definition:'Using borrowed money to fund a company, which magnifies both gains and losses.',example:'Lehman Brothers used about 30 to 1 leverage before it collapsed in 2008.'},{term:'dilution',definition:'When new shares are sold, every existing owner\'s percentage of the company shrinks.',example:'If a founder owns 50% and the company sells new shares equal to a quarter of the total, the founder\'s stake drops to 40%.'}],diagramDescription:'A comparison of what debt costs a company versus what equity investors expect to earn',diagramVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'The price of each door',rows:[{label:'Typical after tax cost of debt',value:'~5%',percent:25},{label:'Return equity investors expect',value:'~15%',percent:75}],footer:'Debt is roughly 3x cheaper, but it must be paid even in a bad year',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'quiz',question:'A profitable company can raise money either way. Why might it pick debt over selling shares?',options:['Debt is free because it never has to be repaid','Lenders become part owners and share the losses','Interest is tax deductible and no ownership is given up','Banks do not charge interest to profitable companies'],correct:2,explanation:'Debt has two big perks: the tax code lets companies deduct interest payments, and lenders get zero ownership. The company keeps every slice of its future profits, as long as it can make the payments.'},{type:'caseStudy',title:'Amazon used both doors',events:[{date:'1997',event:'Amazon\'s IPO raises $54 million by selling shares at $18 each.',context:'A money-losing online bookstore could never get a big loan, so it sold equity. Early shareholders took the risk in exchange for ownership.'},{date:'1999',event:'Amazon sells $1.25 billion of convertible bonds.',context:'Still unprofitable, Amazon borrowed huge sums to survive the dot-com era and keep building infrastructure.'},{date:'2006',event:'Amazon launches AWS, its cloud computing business.',context:'The servers and data centers behind AWS were built by a company kept alive with both equity and debt money.'},{date:'2017',event:'Amazon sells $16 billion of bonds to help buy Whole Foods.',context:'Now a giant with steady cash flow, Amazon could borrow billions cheaply instead of diluting its shareholders.'}],lesson:'Young and risky, Amazon sold ownership because no one would lend to it cheaply. Big and stable, it borrowed because debt was the cheaper door. The right capital structure changes as the company does.'},{type:'misconceptions',title:'Common myths',subtitle:'What people get wrong about raising money',items:[{myth:'Equity is free money because you never pay it back.',truth:'Equity is usually the most expensive money a company can take.',explanation:'Sell 30% of your startup for $5 million and later sell the company for $50 million. That 30% stake is now worth $15 million. Your "free" money cost triple what a loan\'s interest would have.'},{myth:'Debt is always bad for a company.',truth:'Reasonable debt is often the smartest money available.',explanation:'Interest is tax deductible and lenders take no ownership. That is why Apple, holding $145 billion in cash, still chose to sell $17 billion of bonds in 2013.'},{myth:'Selling new shares does not cost existing owners anything.',truth:'Every new share shrinks every existing owner\'s slice.',explanation:'This is dilution. A founder with 40% of the company before a big share sale might hold 30% after it, giving up a tenth of every future dollar of profit.'}]},{type:'fillBlank',sentence:'When a company sells new shares and every existing owner\'s slice of the pie shrinks, that is called ____.',options:['dilution','leverage','a coupon'],correct:0},{type:'scenario',situation:'You founded a sneaker resale platform that now earns a steady $2 million a year in profit. You need $5 million to expand. A bank offers a $5 million loan at 9% interest. A venture capital firm offers $5 million for 30% of your company.',choices:[{label:'Take the 9% loan',outcome:'You pay about $450,000 a year in interest, which your steady profits easily cover and which is tax deductible. The bank gets paid even in a bad year, but you keep 100% of the company you built.',correct:!0},{label:'Sell 30% to the VC',outcome:'No repayments ever, and the VC brings connections. But if you sell the company for $50 million someday, their 30% is worth $15 million. You paid $15 million for $5 million because you gave up ownership you did not need to.',correct:!1}]},{type:'trueFalse',statements:[{s:'Interest payments on company debt are tax deductible.',a:!0},{s:'A company that sells shares must repay those investors on a set date.',a:!1},{s:'If a company misses its bond payments, lenders can force it into bankruptcy.',a:!0},{s:'A company with zero debt is always making the smartest financial choice.',a:!1}]},{type:'keyTermsCards',terms:[{term:'capital structure',definition:'The mix of debt and equity a company uses to fund itself.',example:'40% debt and 60% equity is a common blend for a stable company.',sentence:'Amazon\'s capital structure shifted toward debt as its cash flow became predictable.'},{term:'leverage',definition:'Using borrowed money to amplify a company\'s returns, and its risks.',example:'Lehman Brothers ran at roughly 30 to 1 leverage before 2008.',sentence:'A little leverage boosts returns, but too much can sink a company in one bad year.'},{term:'dilution',definition:'The shrinking of existing owners\' stakes when new shares are sold.',example:'A founder\'s 50% stake can drop to 40% after a new funding round.',sentence:'Founders often accept debt specifically to avoid dilution.'},{term:'coupon',definition:'The fixed interest payment a bond makes to whoever holds it.',example:'A $1,000 bond with a 5% coupon pays $50 a year.',sentence:'Apple\'s 2013 bonds carried coupons near 2%, remarkably cheap money.'}]},{type:'summaryCards',lessonTitle:'How Companies Raise Money',cards:[{takeaway:'Companies raise money through two doors',detail:'Debt means borrowing, usually via bonds, and repaying with interest. Equity means selling shares of ownership with nothing to repay. The blend is the capital structure.'},{takeaway:'Debt is cheap but unforgiving',detail:'Interest is tax deductible and lenders get no ownership, but payments are due even in terrible years. Over-levered companies like the 2008 banks collapsed when they could not roll over their debt.'},{takeaway:'Equity is safe but expensive',detail:'Shareholders can never demand their money back, but they dilute existing owners and take a slice of every future profit. A 30% stake in a $50 million exit costs $15 million.'},{takeaway:'The right mix changes with the company',detail:'Risky young companies sell equity because no one lends to them cheaply. Stable giants like Amazon and Apple borrow billions because debt is the cheaper door.'}]}],challenge:[{question:'A startup raises $25 million and is never required to repay any of it. Which financing door did it walk through?',options:['Debt','Equity'],correct:1,explanation:'Money that never has to be repaid means the company sold ownership stakes rather than borrowing. Equity investors receive shares instead of a repayment promise, while debt always carries an obligation to pay the money back.'},{question:'A profitable company borrows $40 million instead of selling new shares. What is the main appeal of choosing debt here?',options:['The loan principal never has to be repaid','The lenders will absorb any future losses as part owners','Interest payments are tax deductible and the founders keep full ownership','Banks waive interest charges for companies with strong profits'],correct:2,explanation:'Debt offers two key advantages: the tax code allows the company to deduct interest payments from taxable income, and lenders receive no ownership. As long as the company can cover its payments, every slice of future profit stays with the existing owners.'},{question:'A company with 8 million shares outstanding issues 2 million new shares to investors. Each existing shareholder\'s percentage of the company shrinks. What is this effect called?',options:['Dilution','Leverage','A coupon'],correct:0,explanation:'When new shares are created, the ownership pie is cut into more slices, so every existing holder\'s percentage stake gets smaller. This is dilution. Leverage refers to using borrowed money, and a coupon is the interest payment on a bond.'},{question:'A firm receives $15 million from investors who get shares, and separately borrows $5 million from a bank. Which portion comes with a repayment promise?',options:['The $15 million, because equity must eventually be returned','The $5 million, because debt is borrowed and must be repaid','Both amounts, since all outside money must be paid back','Neither amount, since companies only repay when profitable'],correct:1,explanation:'Only the borrowed $5 million must be repaid, because debt is a loan with a repayment obligation plus interest. The $15 million was exchanged for ownership, so those investors hold shares rather than an IOU and the company owes them no repayment.'}]},{num:5,title:'Valuing a Company',xp:30,steps:[{type:'hookOpener',title:'One company, two wildly different price tags',fact:'In 2022, professional analysts published price targets for Tesla ranging from about $67 a share to over $1,500 a share. Same company, same public numbers, a 22x gap.',outcome:'By the end you\'ll be able to explain what a company is really worth, discount a future cash flow back to today, and read a P/E ratio like an analyst.',visualDescription:'Bars showing the huge spread between the lowest analyst target, the average, and the highest for the same stock',visual:{type:'bars',title:'Analyst price targets, same stock',items:[{label:'Lowest',value:67,displayValue:'$67'},{label:'Average',value:300,displayValue:'$300'},{label:'Highest',value:1500,displayValue:'$1,500'}],footer:'Valuation depends on assumptions about the future',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'stakesCard',without:{label:'Without valuation skills',detail:'You buy the hype at any price. Investors who bought Peloton near its $171 peak in January 2021 watched it fall about 95% to under $9 by late 2022 when growth assumptions collapsed.'},withoutVisual:{type:'line',title:'Peloton share price',points:[{label:'Jan 2021',value:'$171'},{label:'Dec 2021',value:'$36'},{label:'Jun 2022',value:'$10'},{label:'Dec 2022',value:'$8'}],trend:'down',footer:'About a 95% drop from the peak',sentiment:'negative'},with:{label:'With valuation skills',detail:'You compare price to what the business actually earns. A $40 stock earning $2 per share costs 20x earnings, and you can judge whether that is fair before you buy.'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'The habit that protects you',value:'20x',detail:'price compared to earnings, checked before buying',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The big idea',title:'A company is worth its future cash, counted today',paragraphs:['A company\'s value is not its stock price. It is the sum of all the cash the business will generate in the future, adjusted for the fact that money later is worth less than money now.','That adjustment is called discounting. $100 arriving next year is worth about $91 today at a 10% discount rate, because $91 invested today at 10% grows into $100 in a year.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'valuation',definition:'Estimating what a business is actually worth, separate from its current price.',example:'An analyst values a sneaker brand at $50 per share while it trades at $70.'},{term:'discounted cash flow',definition:'A method that values a company as the sum of its future cash flows, each shrunk back to today\'s dollars.',example:'$100 arriving in 10 years at a 10% rate counts as only about $39 today.'}],diagramDescription:'Bars showing the same future $100 shrinking the further away it is',diagramVisual:{type:'bars',title:'What a future $100 is worth today',items:[{label:'Next year',value:91,displayValue:'$91'},{label:'In 5 years',value:62,displayValue:'$62'},{label:'In 10 years',value:39,displayValue:'$39'}],footer:'At a 10% discount rate',sentiment:'negative'}},{type:'microCheck',question:'According to valuation theory, what makes a company valuable?',options:['The cash it will generate in the future','Its current stock price'],correct:0,explanationCorrect:'Yes. Price is just what people are paying today. Value is the discounted sum of all future cash flows.',explanationWrong:'Not quite. The stock price is an opinion that changes daily. Value comes from the cash the business will produce over its life.'},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The method',title:'DCF in three moves',paragraphs:['A discounted cash flow, or DCF, works in three steps. First, project the cash the business will produce each year, usually 5 to 10 years out, plus a terminal value for everything after that.','Second, discount each year\'s cash back to today by dividing by (1 + r)^n. Third, add them all up. That total is your estimate of value.','Here is the catch: tiny changes in your growth or discount assumptions compound over decades, so two careful analysts can land on very different numbers. That is exactly how Tesla got targets of $67 and $1,500 at the same time.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'terminal value',definition:'One lump number standing in for all the cash flows beyond your forecast years.',example:'Years 11 and onward might be captured as one $80 billion terminal value.'}],diagramDescription:'A left to right flow from projecting cash flows, to discounting them, to summing into a value',diagramVisual:{type:'flow',title:'The DCF pipeline',nodes:[{label:'Project cash flows',value:'years 1 to 10'},{label:'Discount each one',value:'divide by (1 + r)^n'},{label:'Sum them up',value:'= company value'}],sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'calculator',title:'Discount it yourself',formula:'PV = FV / (1 + r)^n',blanks:[{label:'A company will hand you $121 in 2 years. At a 10% discount rate, what is that worth today? ($121 divided by 1.1, twice)',answer:100,tolerance:2}]},{type:'teachingSlide',sectionLabel:'The shortcut',title:'P/E: valuation in one number',paragraphs:['A full DCF takes a spreadsheet. The price to earnings ratio is the quick version: divide the share price by earnings per share.','A company earning $2 per share and trading at $40 has a P/E of 20. You are paying $20 for every $1 of yearly profit. Higher P/E means the market expects faster growth, and expects it to last.'],highlightedTerms:[{term:'price to earnings',definition:'Share price divided by earnings per share. It tells you how many dollars you pay per dollar of annual profit.',example:'$40 price with $2 of earnings per share is a P/E of 20.'}],diagramDescription:'Comparison bars showing typical P/E levels from a slow utility to a hot growth stock',diagramVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'What different P/E levels look like',rows:[{label:'Sleepy utility',value:'12x',percent:24},{label:'Market average',value:'20x',percent:40},{label:'Hot growth stock',value:'50x',percent:100}],footer:'Higher multiples bake in bigger growth expectations',sentiment:'positive'}},{type:'fillBlank',sentence:'A company earning $2 per share and trading at $40 has a P/E of ____.',options:['20','80'],correct:0},{type:'quiz',question:'An analyst raises her long term growth assumption from 3% to 5%. What happens to her DCF value?',options:['It rises by exactly 2 percentage points','It rises sharply, growth compounds for years','It stays the same until profits change','It falls because faster growth is riskier'],correct:1,explanation:'Growth compounds over every forecast year and inflates the terminal value, so a 2 point change in the assumption can swing the final number by 50% or more. This sensitivity is why honest analysts disagree so much.'},{type:'keyTermsCards',terms:[{term:'discounted cash flow',definition:'Valuing a company as the sum of its future cash flows, each shrunk back to today\'s dollars.',example:'$121 arriving in 2 years counts as $100 today at a 10% rate.',sentence:'Her discounted cash flow model said the stock was worth $45, well below its $70 price.'},{term:'price to earnings',definition:'Share price divided by earnings per share, the quick check on how expensive a stock is.',example:'A $90 stock earning $3 per share has a P/E of 30.',sentence:'The price to earnings ratio of 50 made him ask whether growth could really justify it.'},{term:'terminal value',definition:'A single number that stands in for all cash flows beyond the years you explicitly forecast.',example:'Years 11 and beyond rolled into one lump sum at the end of the model.',sentence:'Most of her DCF\'s answer came from the terminal value, so she double checked that assumption.'},{term:'margin of safety',definition:'Buying well below your estimate of value so that being somewhat wrong does not wreck you.',example:'Valuing a stock at $50 but only buying under $35.',sentence:'Because DCF outputs are just estimates, he demanded a margin of safety before buying.'}]},{type:'scenario',situation:'A stock you like trades at a P/E of 50 while the market average is 20. Your friend says sell it immediately. What do you do?',choices:[{label:'Buy more, a high P/E means everyone loves it',outcome:'Popularity is not value. Peloton was loved at its peak too, right before growth stalled and the stock lost about 95%. Paying any price for hype is how you overpay.',correct:!1},{label:'Check its earnings growth before judging',outcome:'Smart. You find profits growing 40% a year, which would nearly triple earnings in 3 years and pull the P/E down fast if the price stayed flat. A 50 P/E can be earned, but only by real, durable growth. Now you can decide with evidence.',correct:!0},{label:'Sell now, a P/E of 50 always means overvalued',outcome:'Not always. Amazon traded above a P/E of 50 for most of the 2010s while the stock rose more than 10x, because earnings kept catching up. A high multiple is a question to investigate, not an automatic verdict.',correct:!1}]},{type:'misconceptions',title:'Cheap and expensive are not what they look like',subtitle:'Three myths that cost real money',items:[{myth:'A $5 stock is cheaper than a $500 stock.',truth:'Share price alone tells you nothing about value.',explanation:'Price per share depends on how many slices the company is cut into. One share of Berkshire Hathaway Class A costs over $600,000 and has been a better deal than plenty of $3 stocks. What matters is price compared to earnings and cash flow.'},{myth:'A stock that fell 80% must be a bargain now.',truth:'A falling price can simply mean the old assumptions were wrong.',explanation:'Peloton was down about 80% from its peak by early 2022. It then kept falling. If future cash flows shrink, the value drops with them, and the old high price stops being relevant.'},{myth:'A DCF spits out the one true answer.',truth:'A DCF is only as good as its assumptions.',explanation:'Nudge growth from 3% to 5% or the discount rate from 10% to 8% and the output can swing by half. That is why investors demand a margin of safety instead of trusting one precise number.'}]},{type:'summaryCards',lessonTitle:'Valuing a Company',cards:[{takeaway:'Value is future cash, discounted to today',detail:'A company is worth the sum of the cash it will generate, with each year shrunk back to present dollars. Price is an opinion; value is a calculation.'},{takeaway:'Small assumptions, huge swings',detail:'Growth and discount rate changes compound over decades, which is how the same company can be valued at $67 and $1,500 at once.'},{takeaway:'P/E is the quick check',detail:'Share price divided by earnings per share. $40 on $2 of earnings is a P/E of 20, and higher multiples demand faster growth to be justified.'},{takeaway:'Leave a margin of safety',detail:'Since every valuation is an estimate, buy well below your number so being somewhat wrong does not wreck the outcome.'}]}],challenge:[{question:'According to valuation theory, what makes a company valuable?',options:['Its share price on today\'s ticker','The cash it will generate in the future','The number of employees it has','How often its stock is traded'],correct:1,explanation:'Price is just what people are paying today, an opinion that changes daily. Value is the discounted sum of all the cash the business will produce over its life.'},{question:'A company will hand you $242 in 2 years. At a 10% discount rate, what is that worth today?',options:['$200','$220','$242','$196'],correct:0,explanation:'Divide $242 by 1.1 twice: 242 / 1.1 = 220, then 220 / 1.1 = 200. Money arriving in the future is worth less today, and each year of waiting shaves off another 10%.'},{question:'A company earning $3 per share and trading at $75 has a P/E ratio of what?',options:['25','78','3','225'],correct:0,explanation:'P/E is price divided by earnings per share: 75 / 3 = 25. It tells you how many dollars investors are paying for each dollar of annual earnings.'},{question:'An analyst raises her long term growth assumption from 2% to 4%. What happens to her DCF value?',options:['It rises by exactly 2 percentage points','It rises sharply, because growth compounds for years','It stays the same until profits actually change','It falls because faster growth is riskier'],correct:1,explanation:'Growth compounds over every forecast year and inflates the terminal value, so a 2 point change in the assumption can swing the final number by 50% or more. This sensitivity is why honest analysts disagree so much.'}]},(0,r.L)(6,'Working Capital Management',30,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Working Capital Management','Dell revolutionized manufacturing by collecting payment from customers BEFORE paying suppliers, creating negative working capital that funded its explosive growth without external financing. At its peak, Dell had \u2212$7 billion in working capital, meaning customers funded the entire operation.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand the cash conversion cycle, how to optimize receivables, inventory, and payables, and why working capital efficiency can make or break a company.','Cash conversion cycle diagram showing days inventory \u2192 days receivable \u2192 days payable',{type:'metric',title:'Dell: Negative Working Capital',value:'\u2212$7 billion',detail:'Customers pay BEFORE suppliers, the entire operation self-funded',footer:'Negative working capital funded explosive growth',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Poor working capital management',detail:'Sears had $8B in inventory but only $1B in cash. Slow inventory turns tied up capital that could\'ve funded its digital transformation. The company filed bankruptcy in 2018 after 130 years.'},{label:'Efficient working capital',detail:'Amazon\'s negative working capital cycle means it collects cash from customers ~20 days before paying suppliers. This $30B+ "float" has funded decades of growth without proportional debt or equity issuance.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'comparison',title:'Sears: Capital Trapped',rows:[{label:'Inventory',value:'$8B',percent:89},{label:'Cash',value:'$1B',percent:11}],footer:'Slow inventory turns starved the digital transformation, bankrupt 2018',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'flow',title:'Amazon: The Float',nodes:[{label:'Collect from customers',value:'Day 0',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Pay suppliers',value:'~Day 20',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'$30B+ free float',value:'Funds growth',color:'#10B981'}],footer:'$30B+ float funds decades of growth with no extra debt',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE CASH CYCLE','How Cash Flows Through a Business',['Working capital is the lifeblood of daily operations: Current Assets minus Current Liabilities. It measures whether a company can pay its short-term obligations. Too little working capital = risk of missing payroll or supplier payments. Too much = capital trapped in low-return assets instead of being invested in growth.','The Cash Conversion Cycle (CCC) measures how many days it takes to turn inventory investment into cash from sales: CCC = Days Inventory Outstanding + Days Sales Outstanding \u2212 Days Payable Outstanding. A shorter CCC means faster cash recovery. A negative CCC (like Amazon\'s) means the company receives customer cash before paying suppliers.','Each component is a lever: (1) Reduce DIO by improving inventory management and forecasting. (2) Reduce DSO by collecting receivables faster (incentivize early payment, automate invoicing). (3) Increase DPO by negotiating longer supplier terms. Each day saved in the cycle frees up cash equivalent to one day\'s operating costs.'],[{term:'Cash Conversion Cycle',definition:'DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO. Days from paying for inventory to collecting cash from customers.',example:'A company with 45 DIO, 30 DSO, and 60 DPO has CCC = 45+30\u221260 = 15 days.'},{term:'Days Sales Outstanding',definition:'Average days to collect payment after a sale. Lower is better.',example:'DSO of 30 means the company collects cash 30 days after invoicing customers.'}],'Circular flow: Cash \u2192 Inventory \u2192 Sale \u2192 Receivable \u2192 Cash, with DIO, DSO, and DPO labeled on each segment',void 0,{type:'bars',title:'Cash Conversion Cycle = DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO',items:[{label:'DIO',value:45,displayValue:'+45 days'},{label:'DSO',value:30,displayValue:'+30 days'},{label:'DPO',value:60,displayValue:'\u221260 days'},{label:'CCC',value:15,displayValue:'15 days'}],footer:'A shorter cycle means faster cash recovery',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company has DIO of 60 days, DSO of 45 days, and DPO of 30 days. What is the Cash Conversion Cycle?',['15 days','75 days','35 days','135 days'],1,'Correct! CCC = DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO = 60 + 45 \u2212 30 = 75 days. This company waits 75 days between paying for inventory and collecting cash from sales.','CCC = DIO (60) + DSO (45) \u2212 DPO (30) = 75 days. The company funds 75 days of operations out of its own cash before the cycle completes.'),(0,r.fb)('The Cash Conversion Cycle equals Days Inventory Outstanding + Days Sales Outstanding minus Days ___ Outstanding.',['Revenue','Payable','Equity','Profit'],1),(0,r.q)('Which company has better working capital efficiency: Company A (CCC = 45 days) or Company B (CCC = \u221210 days)?',['Company A, a positive CCC means the company is comfortably collecting cash and never has to wait on its own money','Company B, negative CCC means it collects from customers before paying suppliers','They\'re equal, since CCC only measures timing and timing has no effect on how much cash a company keeps','Cannot determine, because working capital efficiency depends entirely on the size of the company and its industry'],1,'A negative CCC means the company collects customer cash BEFORE paying suppliers. This is the gold standard of working capital efficiency, Amazon, Dell, and Costco all operate with negative or near-zero CCCs.'),(0,r.summaryCards)('Working Capital Management',[{takeaway:'Working capital is the cash cycle',detail:'CCC = DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO. A shorter cycle means faster cash recovery. Negative CCC means customers fund your operations.'},{takeaway:'Each day matters',detail:'One day of improvement in CCC frees up cash equal to one day of operating costs. For a $10B/year company, that\'s $27M per day.'},{takeaway:'Efficiency funds growth',detail:'Amazon, Dell, and Apple use working capital efficiency as a competitive advantage, funding growth without external capital.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Working Capital Management','Mergers & Acquisitions','You\'ll explore how companies buy, merge with, and integrate other businesses, including deal structures, synergy analysis, and why 70% of acquisitions fail to create value.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the Cash Conversion Cycle formula?',['Revenue \xf7 Expenses','DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO','Assets \u2212 Liabilities','Cash In \u2212 Cash Out'],1,'CCC = Days Inventory Outstanding + Days Sales Outstanding \u2212 Days Payable Outstanding. It measures the days between paying for inventory and collecting cash from customers.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is a negative CCC advantageous?',['It means the company is losing money, since any number below zero on a financial metric signals a loss','It means the company collects customer payments before paying suppliers, creating free financing','It indicates bankruptcy risk, because a company with negative working capital cannot cover its debts','It\'s not, a positive CCC is always better since the company holds cash for longer before spending it'],1,'Negative CCC means customers fund your operations. Amazon collects payment immediately but pays suppliers 60+ days later, creating billions in interest-free financing.'),(0,r.cq)('A company reduces its DSO from 60 to 45 days. Annual revenue is $3.65B. How much cash is freed?',['$150M','$45M','$60M','$300M'],0,'Daily revenue = $3.65B \xf7 365 = $10M. Reducing DSO by 15 days frees 15 \xd7 $10M = $150M in cash.')]),(0,r.L)(7,'Mergers & Acquisitions',30,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Mergers & Acquisitions','In 2000, AOL acquired Time Warner for $164 billion, the largest merger in history. Within two years, the combined company wrote off $99 billion in value, making it the most destructive deal ever. Why do so many acquisitions fail, and what separates the rare successes?','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand deal structures, synergy analysis, due diligence, and why disciplined M&A creates value while empire-building destroys it.','Timeline showing the AOL-Time Warner deal from announcement to $99B writeoff',{type:'line',title:'AOL, Time Warner: $164B Deal',points:[{label:'2000',value:'$164B'},{label:'2001',value:'$110B'},{label:'2002',value:'$65B'}],trend:'down',footer:'$99B written off, the most destructive merger ever',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Undisciplined M&A',detail:'AOL-Time Warner ($164B) lost $99B. HP-Autonomy ($11B) wrote off $8.8B. Sprint-Nextel ($35B) wrote off $30B. Studies show 70% of acquisitions fail to create value, overpayment and failed integration are the top killers.'},{label:'Disciplined M&A',detail:'Disney acquired Pixar ($7.4B, 2006), Marvel ($4B, 2009), and Lucasfilm ($4B, 2012). Combined, these three acquisitions generated over $50 billion in cumulative revenue. The difference: strategic fit, fair pricing, and cultural integration.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'bars',title:'Undisciplined M&A Writeoffs',items:[{label:'AOL, TW',value:99,displayValue:'$99B'},{label:'Sprint, Nextel',value:30,displayValue:'$30B'},{label:'HP, Autonomy',value:9,displayValue:'$8.8B'}],footer:'70% of acquisitions fail to create value',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'bars',title:'Disney: Disciplined M&A',items:[{label:'Pixar',value:74,displayValue:'$7.4B'},{label:'Marvel',value:40,displayValue:'$4B'},{label:'Lucasfilm',value:40,displayValue:'$4B'}],footer:'Three deals generated $50B+ in cumulative revenue',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE M&A FRAMEWORK','How Deals Are Structured and Why Most Fail',['Mergers & Acquisitions come in many forms: horizontal (same industry, Disney buying Pixar), vertical (supply chain, Amazon buying Whole Foods), and conglomerate (unrelated, GE buying NBC). The type matters because it determines what synergies are available and how likely integration success is.','Synergies are the supposed value created by combining two companies. Revenue synergies (cross-selling, new markets) are notoriously hard to achieve, studies show only 30% materialize. Cost synergies (eliminating duplicate functions, economies of scale) are more reliable at 60-70% realization. Smart acquirers only pay for cost synergies and treat revenue synergies as upside.','Due diligence is the investigation process before closing a deal. Financial due diligence verifies the target\'s numbers. Legal due diligence checks for lawsuits and liabilities. Operational due diligence assesses integration feasibility. Skipping or rushing due diligence has killed countless deals, HP discovered after acquiring Autonomy that $8.8B of the $11B price was based on fraudulent accounting.'],[{term:'Synergies',definition:'Value created by combining two companies that neither could achieve alone. Cost synergies (cutting redundancies) and revenue synergies (cross-selling).',example:'When Disney acquired 21st Century Fox, cost synergies from eliminating duplicate corporate functions saved $2B+ annually.'},{term:'Due Diligence',definition:'The investigation of a target company\'s financials, operations, legal status, and strategic fit before acquisition.',example:'Thorough due diligence of Whole Foods revealed consistent same-store sales and prime real estate locations that justified Amazon\'s $13.7B price.'},{term:'Control Premium',definition:'The amount paid above market price for full ownership, typically 20-40% for public companies.',example:'Microsoft paid a 45% premium for Activision Blizzard ($95/share vs. $65 pre-announcement).'}],'Funnel diagram: Target Identification \u2192 Valuation \u2192 Due Diligence \u2192 Negotiation \u2192 Integration \u2192 Value Creation (or Destruction)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The M&A Process',nodes:[{label:'Target ID'},{label:'Valuation'},{label:'Due Diligence'},{label:'Negotiation'},{label:'Integration'},{label:'Value Creation'}],footer:'Skipping due diligence has killed countless deals',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company acquires a target for $10B, projecting $2B in synergies (60% cost, 40% revenue). If historical data shows 70% of cost synergies and 30% of revenue synergies are typically realized, what is the realistic synergy value?',['$2B','$1.08B','$1.5B','$0.5B'],1,'Correct! Cost synergies: $1.2B \xd7 70% = $840M. Revenue synergies: $800M \xd7 30% = $240M. Total: $1.08B, roughly half the projected $2B. This is why disciplined acquirers discount projected synergies heavily.','Cost synergies realized: $1.2B \xd7 0.70 = $840M. Revenue synergies realized: $800M \xd7 0.30 = $240M. Realistic total: $1.08B. Most deals overestimate synergies by 40-60%.'),(0,r.fb)('The amount paid above market price for full ownership of a target is called the ___ premium.',['control','synergy','market','strategic'],0),(0,r.q)('Why did Disney\'s acquisitions of Pixar, Marvel, and Lucasfilm succeed while most acquisitions fail?',['Disney paid the lowest price possible','Strategic fit was clear, prices were disciplined relative to cash flow potential, and Disney preserved each brand\'s creative culture during integration','Disney got lucky three times','The targets were too small to fail'],1,'Disney paid fair prices for studios with proven IP, maintained creative independence (critical for content companies), and leveraged Disney\'s distribution to amplify each studio\'s revenue. Strategic fit + fair pricing + smart integration = rare M&A success.'),(0,r.summaryCards)('Mergers & Acquisitions',[{takeaway:'70% of acquisitions fail to create value',detail:'Overpayment, unrealistic synergies, and botched integration are the top three killers. Empire-building CEOs are the biggest risk factor.'},{takeaway:'Cost synergies are reliable, revenue synergies aren\'t',detail:'Historical data: 60-70% of cost synergies materialize vs. only 30% of revenue synergies. Smart acquirers only pay for cost synergies.'},{takeaway:'Due diligence is non-negotiable',detail:'HP lost $8.8B because it rushed due diligence on Autonomy. Spending 3-6 months verifying financials, operations, and culture can save billions.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Mergers & Acquisitions','Dividend Policy & Share Buybacks','You\'ll learn how companies return cash to shareholders, the trade-offs between dividends and buybacks, why some companies pay no dividends at all, and how buyback timing creates or destroys value.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What percentage of acquisitions historically fail to create shareholder value?',['30%','50%','70%','90%'],2,'Studies consistently show approximately 70% of acquisitions fail to create the value projected at announcement. Overpayment, integration failure, and unrealistic synergy assumptions are the primary causes.'),(0,r.cq)('What made Disney\'s acquisitions of Pixar ($7.4B), Marvel ($4B), and Lucasfilm ($4B) successful?',['They were cheap, and a low purchase price alone is what guarantees an acquisition will create value','Strategic fit, fair pricing, cultural preservation, and leveraging Disney\'s distribution created $50B+ in cumulative value','Disney fired all existing management and replaced the creative teams to impose its own corporate processes','The acquisitions were too small to fail, since a small target carries almost no integration or financial risk'],1,'Disney preserved each studio\'s creative independence while amplifying revenue through Disney\'s massive distribution network. Prices were reasonable relative to cash flow potential.'),(0,r.cq)('Cost synergies vs. revenue synergies: which are more likely to materialize?',['Revenue synergies (30% realization rate)','Cost synergies (60-70% realization rate)','Both materialize at the same rate, since any synergy promised in a deal model is equally achievable','Neither typically materializes, so projected synergies should always be treated as pure marketing'],1,'Cost synergies (eliminating duplicates, economies of scale) are controllable and predictable, 60-70% realization. Revenue synergies (cross-selling, new markets) depend on customer behavior and competition, only 30% materialize.')]),(0,r.L)(8,'Dividend Policy & Share Buybacks',30,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Dividend Policy & Share Buybacks','Apple has returned over $600 billion to shareholders since 2012, more than the GDP of Sweden, through dividends and share buybacks. The buyback program reduced Apple\'s share count by 38%, meaning each remaining share represents a larger piece of the company. This is the third decision in corporate finance: how much profit to return to owners.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand dividend policy, share buyback mechanics, and how to evaluate whether a company\'s capital return strategy creates or destroys value.','Pie chart showing Apple\'s $600B+ capital returns split between dividends and buybacks',{type:'pie',title:'Apple: $600B+ Returned to Shareholders',segments:[{label:'Buybacks',percent:80,color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Dividends',percent:20,color:'#10B981'}],centerLabel:'$600B+',footer:'Buybacks reduced share count by 38% since 2012',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Poorly timed buybacks',detail:'IBM spent $140 billion on buybacks from 2000-2020 while its stock went nowhere. Much of the buying occurred at peak valuations, propping up EPS artificially while the underlying business deteriorated. Shareholders would have been better served by R&D investment.'},{label:'Brilliant capital returns',detail:'Apple bought back $600B+ of stock, reducing share count by 38% since 2013. Because Apple generates massive FCF and buys consistently, the per-share value of every remaining share increased dramatically, contributing to a 10x stock price increase.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'IBM: Poorly Timed Buybacks',value:'$140B spent',detail:'Bought at peak valuations while the business deteriorated',footer:'Stock went nowhere for 20 years',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'bars',title:'Apple: Buybacks Cut Share Count',items:[{label:'2013',value:100,displayValue:'26.3B shares'},{label:'2024',value:62,displayValue:'16.3B shares'}],footer:'38% fewer shares, each share worth more, stock up 10x',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('RETURNING CASH','Dividends vs. Buybacks',['The Dividend Decision is one of the three pillars of corporate finance. Once a company has funded all positive-NPV investments, excess cash should be returned to shareholders. The two mechanisms are dividends (cash payments per share) and share buybacks (the company repurchasing its own stock on the open market).','Dividends provide predictable income but are "sticky", cutting a dividend signals financial distress and typically causes a 25-35% stock price decline. Buybacks are flexible, they can be accelerated, slowed, or paused without signaling distress. Buybacks also have a tax advantage: dividends are taxed immediately, while buybacks create tax-deferred gains through stock price appreciation.','The key question for buybacks: is the stock undervalued? Buying back overvalued stock destroys value, you\'re paying $1.20 for $1.00 of intrinsic value. Buying undervalued stock creates value, you\'re paying $0.80 for $1.00 of intrinsic value. The best capital allocators (Buffett, Apple, Teledyne) time buybacks when their stock is cheap.'],[{term:'Dividend Yield',definition:'Annual dividends per share \xf7 stock price. Measures the income return from dividends.',example:'A stock paying $4/year in dividends at a $100 stock price has a 4% dividend yield.'},{term:'Share Buyback',definition:'A company repurchasing its own shares on the open market, reducing share count and increasing per-share ownership.',example:'If a company with 1B shares buys back 100M, each remaining share now represents 1/900M of the company instead of 1/1B.'},{term:'Payout Ratio',definition:'Dividends \xf7 Net Income. Measures what percentage of profits is returned to shareholders as dividends.',example:'A company earning $10/share and paying $3/share in dividends has a 30% payout ratio.'}],'Two channels from company profits: Dividends (steady stream to shareholders) and Buybacks (recycling shares back to treasury), with the remaining flowing to reinvestment',void 0,{type:'comparison',title:'Dividends vs. Buybacks',rows:[{label:'Dividends',value:'Sticky, taxed now'},{label:'Buybacks',value:'Flexible, tax-deferred'},{label:'Buyback rule',value:'Create value only if undervalued'}],footer:'Return excess cash after funding all positive-NPV projects',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('Apple reduced its share count from 26.3B to 16.3B through buybacks (38% reduction). If earnings stayed flat, what happened to EPS?',['EPS stayed flat','EPS increased ~61%','EPS decreased 38%','Cannot determine'],1,'Correct! Same total earnings spread across 38% fewer shares means each share gets ~61% more earnings. If total earnings were $100B: before: $100B/26.3B = $3.80 EPS. After: $100B/16.3B = $6.13 EPS. That\'s a 61% increase in EPS from financial engineering alone.','When share count drops 38% (from 26.3B to 16.3B), each remaining share captures proportionally more earnings. $100B \xf7 16.3B = $6.13 vs. $100B \xf7 26.3B = $3.80, a 61% EPS increase with zero operational improvement.'),(0,r.fb)('A company that has increased its dividend every year for 25+ consecutive years is called a Dividend ___.',['King, which is the title for a company that has raised dividends for 50+ straight years','Aristocrat','Champion, which is the title for a company with the single highest dividend yield','Hero, which is the informal name for any company that pays a dividend at all'],1),(0,r.q)('A company has $500M in excess cash, a stock price of $100/share, and intrinsic value of $150/share. What is the best use of excess cash?',['Pay a special dividend, since handing cash directly to shareholders is always the safest option','Buy back shares, the stock is 33% undervalued, so each buyback dollar acquires $1.50 of intrinsic value','Acquire a competitor, because spending excess cash on growth is better than returning it to owners','Hold the cash as reserves, since a large cash pile protects the company no matter what the stock price is'],1,'When a stock trades at a 33% discount to intrinsic value, buybacks are the highest-return use of capital. Each $100 spent buys $150 of intrinsic value. This is the scenario where buybacks create the most shareholder value.'),(0,r.summaryCards)('Dividend Policy & Share Buybacks',[{takeaway:'Return excess cash after funding all positive-NPV investments',detail:'Cash that can\'t earn above cost of capital should go back to shareholders, via dividends or buybacks. Hoarding cash is a tax on shareholders.'},{takeaway:'Buybacks create value only when shares are undervalued',detail:'Apple\'s $600B in buybacks created enormous value because the company consistently generated more cash than the market expected. IBM\'s $140B in buybacks destroyed value because the stock was overvalued.'},{takeaway:'Dividends are sticky, buybacks are flexible',detail:'Cutting dividends signals distress (stock drops 25-35%). Buybacks can be paused without stigma. This flexibility makes buybacks the preferred method for most companies.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Dividend Policy & Share Buybacks','Risk Management & Hedging','You\'ll learn how companies protect themselves from financial risks, currency fluctuations, interest rate changes, and commodity price swings, using derivatives, insurance, and strategic hedging.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('Why are share buybacks generally preferred over dividends by most corporations today?',['Buybacks cost the company nothing, since repurchased shares can simply be resold later at a profit','Buybacks are tax-advantaged (deferring capital gains vs. immediate dividend tax) and flexible (can be paused without negative signals)','Dividends are illegal for technology companies, so growth firms have no choice but to repurchase shares','Buybacks always increase the stock price, because reducing share count mechanically guarantees a higher valuation'],1,'Buybacks defer taxes (capital gains vs. immediate dividend income tax) and can be adjusted flexibly. Cutting a dividend signals distress; pausing buybacks doesn\'t. This flexibility is why S&P 500 buybacks exceed dividends by ~2:1.'),(0,r.cq)('When do share buybacks DESTROY value?',['Never, buybacks always help shareholders because a smaller share count is good in every situation','When the company repurchases overvalued stock, paying more per share than the intrinsic value','Only during recessions, since buybacks are safe and value-creating in any normal economic environment','Whenever the company has excess cash, because returning cash to owners is always a waste of capital'],1,'Buying back overvalued stock means paying $1.20 for $1.00 of intrinsic value, wealth transfer from remaining shareholders to selling shareholders. IBM\'s $140B in buybacks at peak valuations created zero value.'),(0,r.cq)('Apple reduced share count by 38%. If total earnings stay constant, EPS increases by approximately:',['38%','61%','0%, earnings per share is based on total earnings','100%'],1,'With 38% fewer shares, same earnings: EPS = E/(0.62\xd7S) = 1.61 \xd7 (E/S). Each share captures 61% more earnings, which is why the stock price increased proportionally.')]),(0,r.L)(9,'Risk Management & Hedging',25,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Risk Management & Hedging','Southwest Airlines locked in jet fuel prices at $51/barrel through hedging contracts in 2007. When oil spiked to $147/barrel in 2008, competitors paid triple the fuel cost, but Southwest saved over $3.5 billion. Strategic hedging can be the difference between thriving and bankruptcy during a crisis.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand the main financial risks companies face, how derivatives and hedging work, and when risk management creates value.','Price chart showing Southwest\'s locked fuel costs vs. market price during the 2008 oil spike',{type:'comparison',title:'Southwest: Fuel Hedge (2008)',rows:[{label:'Locked-in price',value:'$51/barrel',percent:35},{label:'Market price',value:'$147/barrel',percent:100}],footer:'Strategic hedging saved Southwest $3.5B+',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'No risk management',detail:'When the Swiss franc was unpegged from the Euro in 2015, unhedged companies lost 20-30% of European revenue overnight. Several forex brokers went bankrupt within hours. FXCM, the largest US retail forex broker, lost $225M in a single day.'},{label:'Strategic hedging',detail:'Procter & Gamble hedges ~75% of its currency exposure. When the dollar strengthened 15% in 2022, P&G\'s hedges saved an estimated $1.5 billion in translated international earnings, protecting shareholder value while competitors scrambled.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'No Risk Management',value:'FXCM \u2212$225M',detail:'Swiss franc unpegged in 2015, unhedged firms lost 20-30% overnight',footer:'Several forex brokers went bankrupt within hours',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'P&G: Strategic Hedging',value:'$1.5B saved',detail:'Hedges ~75% of currency exposure',footer:'Protected shareholder value when the dollar surged 15%',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('MANAGING RISK','How Companies Protect Against Financial Uncertainty',['Corporate risk management identifies, measures, and mitigates financial risks that could harm a company. The four main categories: (1) Market risk, changes in interest rates, exchange rates, or commodity prices. (2) Credit risk, customers or counterparties failing to pay. (3) Operational risk, internal process failures, fraud, or disasters. (4) Liquidity risk, inability to meet short-term obligations.','Derivatives are financial contracts whose value derives from an underlying asset. The three main types used in corporate hedging: Forwards/Futures (lock in a future price for a commodity, currency, or interest rate), Options (the right but not obligation to buy/sell at a set price), and Swaps (exchange one type of cash flow for another, like fixed-for-floating interest rates).','The goal of hedging is NOT to make money on derivatives, it\'s to reduce uncertainty. Southwest Airlines doesn\'t hedge fuel to profit from oil prices. It hedges to stabilize costs so it can plan routes, set ticket prices, and make capital investments with confidence. Predictability of cash flows is the real value.'],[{term:'Hedge',definition:'A financial position taken to offset potential losses from an adverse price movement in an existing exposure.',example:'A US company expecting \u20ac100M in revenue hedges by buying a forward contract to sell \u20ac100M at today\'s exchange rate.'},{term:'Derivative',definition:'A financial contract whose value is based on (derived from) an underlying asset like a commodity, currency, or interest rate.',example:'An oil futures contract is a derivative, its value changes based on the underlying oil price.'},{term:'Value at Risk (VaR)',definition:'The maximum expected loss over a given period at a specific confidence level.',example:'A 95% daily VaR of $10M means there\'s a 95% chance the company won\'t lose more than $10M in a single day.'}],'Shield diagram with four quadrants (Market, Credit, Operational, Liquidity risk) and derivative instruments pointing at each',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Four Risk Categories',items:[{label:'Market Risk',value:'Rates, FX, commodities',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Credit Risk',value:'Counterparty default',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Operational Risk',value:'Process failure, fraud',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Liquidity Risk',value:'Can\'t meet obligations',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'Hedging creates value through predictability, not profit',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('Southwest Airlines locks in jet fuel at $51/barrel. Market price rises to $147/barrel. Southwest saves $96/barrel. On 2 billion gallons (~47.6 million barrels), what are the approximate savings?',['$96 million','$4.6 billion','$960 million','$9.6 billion'],1,'Correct! $96 savings/barrel \xd7 ~47.6M barrels \u2248 $4.6 billion. This hedge effectively funded Southwest\'s operations for the entire crisis while competitors were hemorrhaging cash.','$96/barrel \xd7 47.6M barrels = ~$4.57 billion in savings. This massive hedge payoff illustrates why strategic risk management is a competitive advantage, not just an insurance cost.'),(0,r.fb)('A financial contract whose value is derived from an underlying asset is called a ___.',['stock, which represents direct fractional ownership of the underlying company itself','derivative','dividend, which is the periodic cash payment a company makes to its shareholders','premium, which is the upfront price a buyer pays to enter an options contract'],1),(0,r.summaryCards)('Risk Management & Hedging',[{takeaway:'Hedging creates value through predictability, not profit',detail:'The goal isn\'t to make money on derivatives, it\'s to stabilize cash flows so the company can invest, price, and plan with confidence.'},{takeaway:'Four risk categories demand different tools',detail:'Market risk (derivatives), Credit risk (diversification, insurance), Operational risk (controls, insurance), Liquidity risk (cash reserves, credit lines).'},{takeaway:'Strategic hedging is a competitive advantage',detail:'Southwest\'s fuel hedging saved $3.5B+. P&G\'s currency hedging saved $1.5B. Companies that manage risk outperform those that gamble on price movements.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Risk Management & Hedging','Corporate Governance & Ethics','You\'ll learn how governance structures protect shareholders, why boards matter, and the scandals (Enron, WorldCom, FTX) that shaped modern corporate governance regulations.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the primary goal of corporate hedging?',['To make a profit on derivatives, treating the hedging desk as a way to speculate on price moves','To eliminate every financial risk the company faces, so that no outcome is ever uncertain again','To reduce cash flow uncertainty so the company can plan and invest with confidence','To increase the stock price, since investors reward any company that trades derivatives actively'],2,'Hedging doesn\'t aim for derivative profits or zero risk, it stabilizes cash flows. Southwest hedges fuel to set ticket prices confidently, not to speculate on oil.'),(0,r.cq)('Southwest saved ~$3.5B by hedging fuel at $51/barrel when prices hit $147. This illustrates:',['Hedging always makes money, since the hedge will gain value no matter which way prices move','Derivatives are simply gambling, because the outcome of a hedge depends entirely on luck','Strategic hedging can be the difference between thriving and bankruptcy during a crisis','Companies should always hedge 100% of every exposure, since more hedging is always safer'],2,'Southwest\'s hedge protected against an extreme scenario. The savings funded operations while competitors were grounding flights and losing money.'),(0,r.cq)('What are the three main types of derivatives used in corporate hedging?',['Stocks, bonds, and mutual funds','Forwards/Futures, Options, and Swaps','Insurance, reserves, and credit lines','P/E ratios, EV/EBITDA, and DCF'],1,'Forwards/Futures lock in prices, Options give the right (not obligation) to transact at set prices, and Swaps exchange cash flow types (e.g., fixed for floating interest rates).')]),(0,r.L)(10,'Corporate Governance & Ethics',25,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Corporate Governance & Ethics','FTX, once valued at $32 billion, collapsed in November 2022 when it was revealed that founder Sam Bankman-Fried had secretly transferred $8 billion in customer funds to his trading firm Alameda Research. No independent board oversight, no real audit committee, no separation of duties, the governance failures were as catastrophic as the fraud itself.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how corporate governance protects shareholders, why independent boards matter, and the regulatory framework built from scandal after scandal.','Governance structure diagram: Shareholders \u2192 Board \u2192 Management \u2192 Operations, with checks at each level',{type:'metric',title:'FTX: Governance Collapse',value:'$32B \u2192 $0',detail:'$8B in customer funds secretly transferred to Alameda',footer:'No independent board, no audit committee, no oversight',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Weak governance',detail:'FTX had no independent board members, no audit committee, and used QuickBooks for accounting a $32B company. Enron\'s board waived its own conflict-of-interest policies to allow CFO partnerships that hid $63B in debt. In both cases, weak governance enabled catastrophic fraud.'},{label:'Strong governance',detail:'Johnson & Johnson\'s Tylenol crisis (1982): when 7 people died from cyanide-laced capsules, the board immediately recalled 31 million bottles at a $100M cost, prioritizing stakeholder safety over short-term profits. The decision, guided by their governance credo, preserved the brand and is taught in every business school.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'grid',title:'Weak Governance',items:[{label:'No independent board',color:'#EF4444'},{label:'No audit committee',color:'#EF4444'},{label:'QuickBooks for $32B',color:'#EF4444'},{label:'$8B fraud enabled',color:'#EF4444'}],footer:'Weak governance enabled catastrophic fraud at FTX',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'J&J: Tylenol Crisis (1982)',value:'31M bottles recalled',detail:'Board prioritized stakeholder safety over a $100M cost',footer:'Strong governance preserved the brand for decades',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE GOVERNANCE FRAMEWORK','How Companies Are Supervised and Controlled',['Corporate governance is the system of rules, practices, and processes by which a company is directed and controlled. It balances the interests of shareholders, management, customers, suppliers, financiers, government, and the community. Good governance prevents fraud, aligns management incentives with shareholders, and ensures ethical decision-making.','The board of directors is the central governance mechanism. Independent directors (no financial ties to management) provide objective oversight. Key committees include: Audit Committee (financial reporting integrity), Compensation Committee (executive pay), and Nominating/Governance Committee (board composition). Post-Enron, Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX) law mandated independent audit committees for all public companies.','Executive compensation is the most visible, and controversial, governance issue. CEOs average 340x the median worker\'s pay (AFL-CIO, 2024). The governance debate: does high pay attract talent and align incentives (through stock options), or does it reflect capture of boards by management? Both sides have evidence, but the best-governed companies tie the majority of CEO pay to long-term stock performance, ensuring the CEO only gets rich if shareholders do.'],[{term:'Board of Directors',definition:'Elected representatives of shareholders who oversee management, set strategy, and ensure accountability.',example:'Apple\'s board includes independent directors from diverse backgrounds, finance, tech, public policy, providing broad oversight.'},{term:'Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX)',definition:'A 2002 US law mandating stricter financial reporting, independent audits, and CEO/CFO personal certification of financial statements. Enacted after Enron and WorldCom.',example:'Under SOX, CEOs must personally certify financial statements, making them criminally liable for fraud.'},{term:'Fiduciary Duty',definition:'The legal obligation of board members and officers to act in the best interest of shareholders, with loyalty and care.',example:'A board member who approves a deal benefiting themselves personally violates fiduciary duty and can face lawsuits.'}],'Three-layer pyramid: Shareholders (own) \u2192 Board (oversees) \u2192 Management (operates), with Audit, Compensation, and Governance committees as supporting pillars',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The Governance Structure',nodes:[{label:'Shareholders',value:'Own'},{label:'Board',value:'Oversees'},{label:'Management',value:'Operates'}],footer:'Audit, Compensation, and Governance committees provide checks',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('Which governance failure was most critical in FTX\'s collapse?',['The company grew too fast','There were no independent board members, no audit committee, and no separation between customer funds and corporate operations','The CEO was too young','Cryptocurrency is inherently risky'],1,'Correct! FTX had zero independent oversight. Without an independent board or audit committee, there was no one to question the $8B transfer of customer funds. The governance vacuum enabled the fraud.','FTX\'s governance was virtually nonexistent: no independent board, no audit committee, no proper accounting systems. These structures exist specifically to prevent exactly what happened, management misusing stakeholder money without oversight.'),(0,r.fb)('The 2002 law requiring CEO/CFO certification of financial statements, enacted after Enron, is called the ___-Oxley Act.',['Dodd','Glass','Sarbanes','Sherman'],2),(0,r.summaryCards)('Corporate Governance & Ethics',[{takeaway:'Governance exists because humans are fallible',detail:'Independent boards, audit committees, and regulations like SOX exist because history proves that without oversight, even respected executives commit fraud (Enron, WorldCom, FTX).'},{takeaway:'Board independence is non-negotiable',detail:'Independent directors with no financial ties to management provide objective oversight. FTX had zero; J&J\'s strong board saved the Tylenol brand. The correlation between governance quality and outcomes is clear.'},{takeaway:'Executive compensation should align with shareholders',detail:'The best governance ties CEO pay to long-term stock performance. Short-term bonuses incentivize short-term thinking and risk-taking, exactly what destroyed Lehman, Enron, and others.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Corporate Governance & Ethics','Capital Budgeting & Project Evaluation','You\'ll learn how companies evaluate potential investments using NPV, IRR, and payback period, the quantitative tools behind every major capital allocation decision.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What was the primary governance failure at FTX?',['Bad cryptocurrency picks, since the collapse came from holding tokens that lost most of their value','No independent board members, no audit committee, no separation of customer and corporate funds, zero oversight of management','The company was simply too small to need any real governance structures or independent oversight','Government interference, because regulators forced FTX into decisions that drained its customer funds'],1,'FTX lacked every basic governance structure: no independent board, no audit committee, using QuickBooks for a $32B company. Without oversight, management freely transferred $8B in customer funds.'),(0,r.cq)('What did the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (2002) mandate?',['Lower executive pay, by capping how much a public company CEO is allowed to earn each year','Independent audit committees, CEO/CFO personal certification of financials, and stricter internal controls, enacted after Enron and WorldCom','Higher dividend payments, by requiring public companies to return a fixed share of profits to shareholders','Government ownership of public companies, placing federal regulators directly on every corporate board'],1,'SOX was the direct regulatory response to Enron and WorldCom. It made CEOs personally criminally liable for financial fraud and required independent audit committees.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is J&J\'s Tylenol recall (1982) considered a governance success?',['They made a profit on the recall, since pulling product off shelves somehow generated extra revenue','The board prioritized stakeholder safety over $100M in short-term costs, guided by their governance credo, preserving the brand for decades','The government forced the recall, so the credit belongs to regulators rather than to J&J\'s board','They replaced Tylenol with a better product, and the recall was really just a routine product upgrade'],1,'J&J\'s board followed its written credo prioritizing customer safety. The $100M recall cost was insignificant compared to the brand trust preserved. This is governance working as intended, long-term stakeholder value over short-term profit.')]),(0,r.L)(11,'Capital Budgeting & Project Evaluation',35,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Capital Budgeting & Project Evaluation','Netflix spent $17 billion on content in 2023 alone, but how does it decide which shows to greenlight and which to kill? Behind every investment decision is capital budgeting: the quantitative framework that separates value-creating projects from money pits. Companies that master this process compound wealth; those that don\'t waste billions on bad bets.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand NPV, IRR, payback period, and how companies evaluate whether a project is worth pursuing, the same tools used by every CFO on the planet.','NPV decision tree showing project cash flows discounted back to present value',{type:'metric',title:'Netflix Content Spend (2023)',value:'$17 billion',detail:'Capital budgeting decides which shows to greenlight',footer:'NPV separates value-creating projects from money pits',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Poor capital budgeting',detail:'Quibi raised $1.75 billion for a short-form streaming service in 2020. No rigorous NPV analysis could have justified the investment, the projected cash flows never came close to the cost of capital. It shut down after 6 months, destroying nearly all invested capital.'},{label:'Disciplined capital budgeting',detail:'Amazon evaluated AWS infrastructure investments using rigorous NPV models with conservative assumptions. The $60B+ invested in data centers has generated over $80B in annual revenue with 30%+ margins, one of the greatest capital budgeting successes in corporate history.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Quibi: No NPV Discipline',value:'$1.75B raised',detail:'Projected cash flows never approached the cost of capital',footer:'Shut down after 6 months, nearly all capital destroyed',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'bars',title:'Amazon AWS: Disciplined Budgeting',items:[{label:'Invested',value:60,displayValue:'$60B+'},{label:'Annual Revenue',value:80,displayValue:'$80B+'}],footer:'30%+ margins, one of the greatest capital budgeting wins ever',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE NPV FRAMEWORK','How Companies Decide What to Invest In',['Capital budgeting is the process of evaluating potential long-term investments. Every dollar a company spends on a new factory, product, or acquisition is a capital budgeting decision. The fundamental question: will this project generate more cash than it costs, after accounting for the time value of money?','Net Present Value (NPV) is the gold standard of capital budgeting. NPV = Sum of all future cash flows discounted to present value minus the initial investment. If NPV > 0, the project creates value. If NPV < 0, it destroys value. The discount rate used is typically the company\'s Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC), representing the minimum return investors expect.','The NPV rule is simple: accept all projects with positive NPV, reject all projects with negative NPV. When capital is limited, rank projects by NPV and fund from highest to lowest until the budget runs out. This ensures every dollar of capital is directed to its highest-value use.'],[{term:'Net Present Value (NPV)',definition:'The sum of all future cash flows discounted to today\'s value, minus the initial investment. Positive NPV = value creation.',example:'A project costing $10M today that generates $3M/year for 5 years at a 10% discount rate has NPV = $1.37M, it creates value.'},{term:'Discount Rate',definition:'The rate used to convert future cash flows to present value, typically the company\'s WACC.',example:'If WACC is 10%, $100 received in one year is worth $100/1.10 = $90.91 today.'},{term:'Capital Rationing',definition:'When a company has more positive-NPV projects than available capital, forcing it to prioritize.',example:'With $500M to invest and $800M in positive-NPV projects, the CFO must rank and select the highest-value combination.'}],'Flowchart: Identify Project \u2192 Estimate Cash Flows \u2192 Choose Discount Rate \u2192 Calculate NPV \u2192 Accept if NPV > 0',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The NPV Framework',nodes:[{label:'Identify Project'},{label:'Estimate Cash Flows'},{label:'Choose Discount Rate'},{label:'Calculate NPV'},{label:'Accept if NPV > 0'}],footer:'Accept all positive-NPV projects, reject negative-NPV ones',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A project costs $5M upfront and generates $1.5M per year for 5 years. The discount rate is 10%. The NPV is approximately $688K. Should the company invest?',['No, a $688K NPV is too small relative to the $5M cost to be worth pursuing','Yes, any positive NPV means the project creates shareholder value','Only if there are no other projects available, since small-NPV projects are a last resort','Need more information, because NPV alone can never tell you whether to accept a project'],1,'Correct! The NPV rule says accept ALL positive-NPV projects. $688K of value creation means this project earns more than the 10% cost of capital. Every positive-NPV project, regardless of size, creates shareholder wealth.','Any positive NPV means the project returns MORE than the cost of capital. Even $688K of value creation is better than zero. The NPV rule: accept all positive-NPV projects when capital is available.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('BEYOND NPV','Internal Rate of Return & Payback Period','comparison','Compare three capital budgeting metrics: NPV (the gold standard), IRR (the percentage return), and Payback Period (how quickly you get your money back).',['Internal Rate of Return (IRR) is the discount rate that makes NPV equal to zero. Think of it as the project\'s percentage return. If IRR > WACC, the project creates value. IRR is intuitive, "this project returns 18%" is easier to grasp than "the NPV is $2.3M." But IRR has pitfalls: it can give multiple answers for unconventional cash flows and doesn\'t account for project scale.','Payback Period measures how long it takes to recover the initial investment. A project costing $10M that generates $2.5M/year has a 4-year payback. It\'s simple and useful for liquidity planning, but it ignores the time value of money and all cash flows after the payback date. A project with a 3-year payback that generates nothing afterward is worse than one with a 5-year payback that generates cash for 20 years.','The best companies use all three: NPV for the final decision (accept/reject), IRR for communicating returns to the board, and Payback Period for assessing liquidity risk. No single metric tells the whole story.'],[{term:'IRR',definition:'The discount rate that makes a project\'s NPV equal to zero, effectively the project\'s annualized return rate.',example:'A project with 18% IRR and 10% WACC creates value because its return exceeds the cost of capital by 8 percentage points.'},{term:'Payback Period',definition:'The time required to recover the initial investment from project cash flows.',example:'A $12M investment generating $3M/year has a 4-year payback period.'}],['NPV accounts for time value of money and project scale, making it the theoretically superior metric.','IRR can give misleading results when cash flows alternate between positive and negative (the "multiple IRR problem").','Short payback periods reduce risk but may cause companies to reject long-term value-creating investments.'],[{label:'Discount Rate (WACC)',min:4,max:20,default:10,unit:'%'},{label:'Project Duration',min:3,max:20,default:10,unit:'years'}],{company:'Netflix',metric:'Content investment IRR',outcome:'Squid Game cost $21.4M to produce and generated an estimated $900M in subscriber value, an IRR exceeding 4,000%',explanation:'Not all projects hit these returns, but Netflix\'s capital budgeting process identifies enough winners to justify the portfolio approach. The average content investment still exceeds their 10-12% WACC.'}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('REAL-WORLD COMPLICATIONS','Why Capital Budgeting Is Hard in Practice',['In textbooks, cash flows are certain. In reality, they\'re estimates, and often wildly wrong. Studies show that 65% of capital projects come in over budget and under-deliver on projected returns. The key is sensitivity analysis: test how NPV changes when assumptions vary. If NPV stays positive even with pessimistic assumptions, the project is robust.','Sunk costs are money already spent that cannot be recovered. They should NEVER factor into capital budgeting decisions. If a company has spent $50M developing a product that clearly won\'t work, the $50M is gone regardless. The only question is: does investing MORE money have a positive NPV going forward? This is emotionally difficult but financially essential.','Opportunity cost, what you give up by choosing one project over another, is a hidden cost that must be included. If a factory expansion uses land that could be sold for $20M, that $20M is a cost of the expansion, even though no cash changes hands. Ignoring opportunity costs leads to systematically overvaluing projects.'],[{term:'Sensitivity Analysis',definition:'Testing how changes in key assumptions (growth rate, costs, discount rate) affect NPV to assess project robustness.',example:'If NPV is positive under all scenarios from 5% to 15% growth, the project is robust. If it turns negative at 8% growth, it\'s risky.'},{term:'Sunk Cost',definition:'Money already spent that cannot be recovered and should not influence future investment decisions.',example:'A company spent $30M developing a drug that failed trials. The $30M is sunk, only future costs and revenues matter for the next decision.'},{term:'Opportunity Cost',definition:'The value of the next best alternative foregone when making a decision.',example:'Using a warehouse for manufacturing means forgoing $5M/year in rental income, that\'s an opportunity cost of the manufacturing project.'}],'Decision matrix showing optimistic, base, and pessimistic scenarios for a project, with NPV calculated under each',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Real-World Complications',items:[{label:'Sensitivity Analysis',value:'Test NPV under varied assumptions',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Sunk Costs',value:'Ignore, money already spent',color:'#EF4444'},{label:'Opportunity Cost',value:'Include the next-best alternative',color:'#F59E0B'}],footer:'65% of capital projects come in over budget',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The capital budgeting metric that calculates the discount rate making NPV equal to zero is called the ___ Rate of Return.',['Internal','External','Required','Expected'],0),(0,r.q)('Project A: NPV = $5M, IRR = 15%, Payback = 6 years. Project B: NPV = $2M, IRR = 25%, Payback = 2 years. You can only choose one. Which should you pick?',['Project B, a higher IRR percentage always means a better project regardless of its scale','Project A, higher NPV means more total value created for shareholders','Project B, a shorter payback is safer, so it should win even when its NPV is much lower','Cannot determine without more information, since NPV and IRR together still tell you nothing'],1,'NPV is the definitive metric for capital budgeting decisions. Project A creates $5M in shareholder value vs. $2M for Project B. IRR can be misleading about scale, a 25% return on a small project creates less value than a 15% return on a larger one. Always maximize NPV.'),(0,r.pc)('NPV Calculation',[{label:'Initial Investment',formula:'Cost today',result:'-$10,000,000'},{label:'Year 1 PV',formula:'$3M \xf7 1.10',result:'$2,727,273'},{label:'Year 2 PV',formula:'$3M \xf7 1.10\xb2',result:'$2,479,339'},{label:'Year 3 PV',formula:'$3M \xf7 1.10\xb3',result:'$2,253,944'},{label:'Year 4 PV',formula:'$3M \xf7 1.10\u2074',result:'$2,049,040'},{label:'Year 5 PV',formula:'$3M \xf7 1.10\u2075',result:'$1,862,764'},{label:'NPV',formula:'Sum of PVs \u2212 Investment',result:'+$1,372,360'}]),(0,r.tr)('Capital Budgeting Essentials',[['What is the NPV rule?','Accept all projects with NPV > 0 (they create value). Reject all projects with NPV < 0 (they destroy value). When capital is limited, rank by NPV.'],['Why is IRR sometimes misleading?','IRR doesn\'t account for project scale (a 50% return on $1M creates less value than 15% on $100M) and can give multiple answers for unconventional cash flows.'],['What are sunk costs and why do they matter?','Costs already incurred that can\'t be recovered. They should NEVER influence future decisions. "We\'ve already spent $50M" is not a reason to spend more.'],['How does sensitivity analysis help?','By testing NPV under different assumptions (optimistic, base, pessimistic), it reveals how robust a project is and which assumptions matter most.']]),(0,r.sc)('You\'re a project manager. Your team has spent $8M over 2 years developing a product. Testing reveals it needs another $5M to launch, with projected revenues of $4M/year for 3 years. WACC is 10%. The NPV of the REMAINING investment is approximately -$50K. What do you recommend?',[{label:'Continue, we\'ve already invested $8M and can\'t waste it',outcome:'This is the sunk cost fallacy. The $8M is gone regardless. The remaining investment has negative NPV, you\'d be investing $5M to get back less than $5M in present value. Continuing destroys MORE value.',correct:!1},{label:'Kill the project, the remaining NPV is negative, and the $8M is a sunk cost',outcome:'Correct but painful. The $8M is a sunk cost, it cannot be recovered. The only question is: does investing the NEXT $5M create value? With negative NPV, it doesn\'t. Killing the project prevents further value destruction.',correct:!0},{label:'Reduce scope to lower the remaining investment needed and re-evaluate',outcome:'Creative problem-solving. If you can reduce the $5M to $3M while preserving most revenue, NPV may turn positive. This is the best of both worlds, but only if the numbers actually work.',correct:!0}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'NPV is the most theoretically sound capital budgeting metric',a:!0},{s:'A project with a short payback period is always better than one with a long payback',a:!1},{s:'Sunk costs should be included in NPV calculations',a:!1},{s:'If IRR exceeds WACC, the project creates value',a:!0},{s:'Opportunity costs should be ignored in capital budgeting',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Capital Budgeting Concept',[['NPV','Sum of discounted cash flows minus initial investment'],['IRR','Discount rate that makes NPV equal to zero'],['Payback Period','Time to recover the initial investment'],['Sunk Cost','Past expenditure irrelevant to future decisions'],['Opportunity Cost','Value of the best alternative foregone']]),(0,r.ds)('Rank these capital budgeting steps in correct order:',['Identify the investment opportunity','Estimate future cash flows','Determine the appropriate discount rate (WACC)','Calculate NPV and IRR','Perform sensitivity analysis','Make accept/reject decision']),(0,r.simulationFinale)('Capital Budgeting Analyst','You\'re a financial analyst at a manufacturing company. The CEO presents three potential projects and asks for your recommendation. The company has $20M available and a WACC of 10%.',[{prompt:'Project Alpha: New factory costing $15M, expected to generate $4M/year for 8 years. NPV at 10% = $6.3M, IRR = 20%. Project Beta: Equipment upgrade costing $8M, expected to generate $2.5M/year for 5 years. NPV at 10% = $1.5M, IRR = 17%. Which do you recommend given the $20M budget?',options:[{label:'Only Project Alpha, it has the highest NPV',consequence:'Alpha alone uses $15M with $6.3M NPV. But you have $20M available. Can you do better?',score:1},{label:'Both Alpha and Beta, combined cost is $23M, exceeding budget, so only Alpha',consequence:'Wait, $15M + $8M = $23M exceeds $20M. You can only fund Alpha. Good catch on the budget constraint. NPV = $6.3M.',score:2},{label:'Both if possible, but since combined cost ($23M) exceeds $20M, fund Alpha ($6.3M NPV) and note Beta for future consideration',consequence:'Excellent analysis. You maximize NPV within the budget ($6.3M from Alpha), identify the constraint, and flag the next-best opportunity. This is professional capital budgeting.',score:3}]},{prompt:'The CEO insists on adding Project Gamma: a vanity headquarters renovation costing $12M with NPV = -$3M. "It\'ll boost morale and attract talent," the CEO argues. How do you respond?',options:[{label:'Include it, the CEO knows best about intangible benefits',consequence:'Negative NPV means the project destroys $3M in shareholder value. "Intangible benefits" are the most common excuse for value-destroying investments. Your job is to provide honest analysis.',score:0},{label:'Present the NPV analysis showing value destruction and recommend quantifying the claimed intangible benefits',consequence:'Perfect approach. You present facts without being confrontational. If morale and talent benefits are real, they should be quantifiable. If they can\'t be quantified, they probably aren\'t worth $3M.',score:3},{label:'Refuse outright, negative NPV means automatic rejection',consequence:'Correct conclusion but poor execution. CFOs and analysts must persuade with data, not dictate. Present the analysis and let the decision-makers weigh in.',score:1}]},{prompt:'After year 1, Project Alpha\'s actual cash flows are $2.5M instead of the projected $4M. Revised projections suggest $3M/year for the remaining 7 years. Should you continue or abandon?',options:[{label:'Abandon, the original projections were wrong',consequence:'The $15M initial investment is sunk. The question is: what\'s the NPV of continuing? $3M/year for 7 years at 10% = $14.6M in present value. Since continuing costs nothing additional, the project still creates value.',score:0},{label:'Continue, the remaining cash flows ($3M \xd7 7 years) still have positive NPV going forward, and the initial $15M is a sunk cost',consequence:'Exactly right. The $15M is sunk. The forward-looking NPV of $3M/year for 7 years at 10% is $14.6M, highly positive. Abandoning would waste future value. Always evaluate on a go-forward basis.',score:3},{label:'Continue but set a strict threshold, if next year also misses, abandon',consequence:'Reasonable risk management, but be careful: the decision should be based on forward-looking NPV, not arbitrary thresholds. If the revised NPV is still positive, continuation creates value.',score:2}]}],'Funding Alpha within budget constraints, pushing back on negative-NPV vanity projects with data, and correctly applying sunk-cost logic to the mid-project evaluation, scoring 9/9.','Capital budgeting is where corporate finance theory meets reality. The best analysts combine quantitative rigor (NPV, IRR) with practical judgment (sensitivity analysis, sunk cost discipline) to recommend decisions that maximize shareholder value, even when those decisions are unpopular.'),(0,r.summaryCards)('Capital Budgeting & Project Evaluation',[{takeaway:'NPV is the gold standard of capital budgeting',detail:'Accept projects with positive NPV, reject those with negative NPV. When capital is limited, rank by NPV and fund from highest to lowest.'},{takeaway:'IRR and Payback complement but don\'t replace NPV',detail:'IRR communicates returns intuitively but ignores scale. Payback measures liquidity risk but ignores time value and post-payback cash flows. Use all three together.'},{takeaway:'Sunk costs are irrelevant; opportunity costs are essential',detail:'Never let past spending influence future decisions. Always include the value of alternatives foregone. These two principles prevent the most common capital budgeting errors.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Capital Budgeting & Project Evaluation','Cost of Capital & WACC','You\'ll learn how companies calculate their cost of capital, the hurdle rate that every investment must clear, and why getting WACC wrong can lead to billions in misallocated capital.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What does a positive NPV indicate about a project?',['The project will definitely be profitable, since a positive NPV is a guarantee of actual cash earnings','The project is expected to create shareholder value by earning returns above the cost of capital','The project carries no risk at all, because a positive NPV removes the chance of any loss','The project will pay back quickly, since a positive NPV always means a short payback period'],1,'Positive NPV means discounted future cash flows exceed the initial investment, the project earns more than the cost of capital, creating value for shareholders.'),(0,r.cq)('A company spent $20M developing a product. Testing shows it needs $5M more to launch, with projected NPV of -$200K on the remaining investment. What should they do?',['Continue, because abandoning the project would waste the $20M that has already been spent','Kill the project, the $20M is a sunk cost and the remaining NPV is negative','Invest half the amount as a compromise, splitting the difference to limit the loss','Sell the project to a competitor, since any half-finished product can be sold for a profit'],1,'The $20M is a sunk cost, gone regardless. The only question is whether investing the NEXT $5M creates value. With negative NPV, it doesn\'t. Continuing wastes more money.'),(0,r.cq)('Project X has IRR of 30% and NPV of $1M. Project Y has IRR of 12% and NPV of $8M. You can only choose one. Which is better?',['Project X, a higher IRR percentage always beats a lower one no matter the dollar value','Project Y, higher NPV means more total value creation','They are equivalent, since IRR and NPV are just two ways of measuring the same thing','Cannot determine, because IRR and NPV pointing in different directions cancel each other out'],1,'NPV measures total value creation. Project Y creates $8M vs. $1M for Project X. IRR is misleading about scale, a 30% return on a small project creates less value than 12% on a much larger one.'),(0,r.cq)('What is the main weakness of the Payback Period method?',['It is too complicated, requiring more calculation than NPV or IRR to produce an answer','It ignores the time value of money and all cash flows after the payback date','It always gives the wrong answer, so payback period should never be used in any analysis','It requires too many assumptions, depending on more inputs than any other capital budgeting tool'],1,'Payback Period ignores TVM (a dollar today vs. a dollar in year 5) and ignores all value generated after the initial investment is recovered. A project with 3-year payback generating nothing afterwards looks better than a 5-year payback generating cash for 20 years.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is sensitivity analysis critical in capital budgeting?',['It makes the NPV calculation itself more accurate by adding precision to the base-case estimate','It tests how robust a project\'s NPV is under different assumptions, revealing which variables matter most and whether the project survives pessimistic scenarios','It eliminates all project risk, since testing assumptions removes the chance of a bad outcome','It is required by law, so every public company must run it before approving any project'],1,'Sensitivity analysis reveals whether NPV stays positive under pessimistic assumptions. If a project\'s NPV turns negative with only minor changes to growth or costs, it\'s fragile and risky despite a positive base-case NPV.')]),(0,r.L)(12,'Cost of Capital & WACC',35,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Cost of Capital & WACC','When Apple evaluates whether to build a new chip fabrication plant, it doesn\'t just ask "will this make money?" It asks "will this earn MORE than the 9% our investors expect?" That 9%, Apple\'s cost of capital, is the single most important number in corporate finance. Get it wrong, and you\'ll approve projects that destroy billions in value.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how to calculate WACC, why it matters as the hurdle rate for every investment, and how capital structure choices affect a company\'s cost of capital.','WACC formula diagram showing debt and equity components combining into a weighted average',{type:'metric',title:'Apple\'s Hurdle Rate',value:'~9% WACC',detail:'Every project must earn MORE than investors expect',footer:'The single most important number in corporate finance',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'WACC miscalculated',detail:'During the dot-com bubble, many companies used artificially low discount rates (3-5%) to justify absurd valuations. When reality hit, projects that "passed" the hurdle rate at 4% failed catastrophically at the true 12-15% cost of capital. $5 trillion in market value evaporated.'},{label:'WACC applied rigorously',detail:'Berkshire Hathaway uses a strict 15%+ hurdle rate for all investments. By refusing to invest below this threshold, Warren Buffett has compounded capital at 20% annually for 58 years, turning $1,000 into $37 million. Discipline in setting the hurdle rate IS the strategy.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Dot-Com Bubble: Fake WACC',value:'$5T evaporated',detail:'Artificially low 3-5% discount rates justified absurd valuations',footer:'Projects that "passed" at 4% failed at the true 12-15%',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Berkshire: Strict Hurdle Rate',value:'15%+ required',detail:'Refusing to invest below the threshold compounded 20%/yr',footer:'$1,000 became $37 million over 58 years',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE WACC FORMULA','How Companies Calculate Their Cost of Capital',['WACC (Weighted Average Cost of Capital) blends the cost of debt and the cost of equity, weighted by their proportions in the company\'s capital structure. The formula: WACC = (E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1-T)), where E = equity value, D = debt value, V = total value (E+D), Re = cost of equity, Rd = cost of debt, and T = tax rate.','Cost of debt (Rd) is straightforward: it\'s the interest rate the company pays on its borrowings. Because interest payments are tax-deductible, the after-tax cost is Rd \xd7 (1-T). If a company borrows at 6% with a 25% tax rate, its after-tax cost of debt is only 4.5%. This tax shield is one of the key advantages of debt financing.','Cost of equity (Re) is trickier, shareholders don\'t receive a contractual interest rate. The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) estimates it: Re = Rf + \u03b2 \xd7 (Rm - Rf), where Rf is the risk-free rate (treasury bonds), \u03b2 (beta) measures the stock\'s volatility relative to the market, and (Rm - Rf) is the market risk premium. A high-beta stock demands a higher return, raising the company\'s WACC.'],[{term:'WACC',definition:'Weighted Average Cost of Capital, the blended cost of all capital sources, used as the discount rate for evaluating investments.',example:'A company funded 60% by equity (cost 12%) and 40% by debt (after-tax cost 4%) has WACC = 0.6\xd712% + 0.4\xd74% = 8.8%.'},{term:'Beta (\u03b2)',definition:'A measure of a stock\'s volatility relative to the overall market. \u03b2=1 means it moves with the market; \u03b2>1 means more volatile.',example:'Tesla\'s beta of ~2.0 means it tends to move twice as much as the market, making its cost of equity much higher than a utility company with \u03b2=0.5.'},{term:'Tax Shield',definition:'The tax savings from deducting interest payments, which reduces the effective cost of debt financing.',example:'At a 25% tax rate, $100M in interest payments saves $25M in taxes, the debt effectively costs 25% less than the stated interest rate.'}],'Balance scale with Equity (higher cost, no tax benefit) on one side and Debt (lower cost, tax-deductible) on the other, balanced at the WACC fulcrum',void 0,{type:'stack',title:'WACC = (E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1\u2212T))',total:'Blended Cost',segments:[{label:'Equity (60%)',value:'Cost 12%',percent:60,color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Debt (40%)',value:'After-tax 4%',percent:40,color:'#3B82F6'}],footer:'Blended cost: 0.6\xd712% + 0.4\xd74% = 8.8%',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company has 70% equity (cost 11%) and 30% debt (cost 6%, tax rate 25%). What is the WACC?',['8.5%','9.05%','7.7%','10.3%'],1,'Correct! WACC = (0.70 \xd7 11%) + (0.30 \xd7 6% \xd7 (1-0.25)) = 7.7% + 1.35% = 9.05%. The after-tax cost of debt (4.5%) is much cheaper than equity (11%), pulling the WACC below the equity cost.','WACC = (E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1-T)) = (0.70 \xd7 11%) + (0.30 \xd7 6% \xd7 0.75) = 7.7% + 1.35% = 9.05%.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('THE OPTIMAL MIX','How Capital Structure Affects WACC','comparison','Adjust the debt-to-equity ratio to see how WACC changes. More debt lowers WACC (due to the tax shield) up to a point, then bankruptcy risk makes everything more expensive.',['As a company adds debt, WACC initially declines because debt is cheaper than equity (after-tax). This is the tax shield effect. But beyond a certain point, additional debt increases bankruptcy risk. Lenders demand higher interest rates, shareholders demand higher returns for the increased risk, and WACC begins to rise.','The optimal capital structure minimizes WACC, and therefore maximizes company value (since company value equals cash flows discounted at WACC). Finding this sweet spot is one of the most important decisions in corporate finance. Tech companies often use little debt (high growth uncertainty), while utilities use lots of debt (stable cash flows).','Modigliani and Miller (M&M), who won the Nobel Prize, proved that in a perfect world without taxes, bankruptcy costs, or information asymmetry, capital structure wouldn\'t matter. In the real world, taxes make debt cheaper (tax shield) and bankruptcy makes too much debt expensive. The optimal WACC sits between these forces.'],[{term:'Optimal Capital Structure',definition:'The debt-to-equity ratio that minimizes WACC and maximizes firm value.',example:'Apple maintains about 30% debt / 70% equity, enough to benefit from the tax shield without excessive bankruptcy risk.'},{term:'Modigliani-Miller Theorem',definition:'The foundational theory that capital structure is irrelevant in perfect markets, establishing the baseline from which real-world analysis departs.',example:'M&M shows that the value of a pizza doesn\'t change based on how you slice it, but taxes and bankruptcy costs do change the total slices available.'}],['Increasing debt from 0% to 30% typically reduces WACC by 1-2 percentage points due to the tax shield.','Beyond 50-60% debt for most companies, bankruptcy risk begins outweighing the tax benefits.','The "optimal" capital structure varies by industry: utilities can support 60%+ debt; tech companies often use under 20%.'],[{label:'Debt Percentage',min:0,max:80,default:30,unit:'%'},{label:'Interest Rate',min:3,max:15,default:6,unit:'%'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('CAPM & COST OF EQUITY','Calculating What Shareholders Expect',['The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) estimates the cost of equity: Re = Rf + \u03b2 \xd7 (Rm - Rf). With a risk-free rate of 4% (10-year Treasury), market risk premium of 6%, and a beta of 1.2: Re = 4% + 1.2 \xd7 6% = 11.2%. This means equity investors expect at least 11.2% return from this stock.','Beta captures systematic risk, the risk that cannot be diversified away. A beta of 1.5 means the stock is 50% more volatile than the market. Higher beta = higher expected return = higher cost of equity = higher WACC. This is why risky companies face higher hurdle rates for their investments.','The market risk premium (typically 5-7%) represents the extra return investors demand for holding stocks instead of risk-free government bonds. It\'s historically stable but debated constantly among academics and practitioners. Small changes in the market risk premium significantly impact cost of equity calculations.'],[{term:'CAPM',definition:'Capital Asset Pricing Model, estimates cost of equity based on the risk-free rate, beta, and market risk premium.',example:'Re = 4% + 1.3 \xd7 6% = 11.8% for a stock with beta of 1.3.'},{term:'Risk-Free Rate',definition:'The return on a theoretically zero-risk investment, typically the 10-year US Treasury bond yield.',example:'In 2024, the risk-free rate was approximately 4-4.5%, reflecting treasury bond yields.'},{term:'Market Risk Premium',definition:'The excess return expected from the stock market above the risk-free rate, historically averaging 5-7%.',example:'If stocks return 10% on average and treasuries yield 4%, the market risk premium is 6%.'}],'CAPM formula with visual: risk-free foundation + beta multiplier \xd7 market premium = cost of equity',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'CAPM: Cost of Equity',nodes:[{label:'Risk-Free Rate',value:'4%'},{label:'+ Beta \xd7 Premium',value:'1.2 \xd7 6%'},{label:'= Cost of Equity',value:'11.2%'}],footer:'Higher beta means a higher cost of equity and a higher WACC',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The tax benefit of deducting interest payments from taxable income is called the tax ___.',['credit','shield','rebate','haven'],1),(0,r.q)('Company A has WACC of 8%. Company B has WACC of 14%. Both evaluate a project returning 11%. What happens?',['Both should accept, an 11% return is good in absolute terms regardless of each firm\'s WACC','Both should reject, since an 11% return is never high enough to justify a real investment','A should accept (11% > 8% WACC) but B should reject (11% < 14% WACC)','The project is equally valuable to both, because WACC has no effect on whether a return is acceptable'],2,'WACC is the hurdle rate. Company A\'s cost of capital is 8%, so an 11% return creates 3% of excess value, accept. Company B\'s cost of capital is 14%, so an 11% return destroys 3% of value, reject. The same project can be value-creating for one company and value-destroying for another.'),(0,r.pc)('WACC Calculation',[{label:'Equity weight',formula:'$600M \xf7 $1,000M',result:'60%'},{label:'Cost of equity (CAPM)',formula:'4% + 1.2 \xd7 6%',result:'11.2%'},{label:'Equity component',formula:'60% \xd7 11.2%',result:'6.72%'},{label:'Debt weight',formula:'$400M \xf7 $1,000M',result:'40%'},{label:'After-tax cost of debt',formula:'6% \xd7 (1 - 0.25)',result:'4.5%'},{label:'Debt component',formula:'40% \xd7 4.5%',result:'1.80%'},{label:'WACC',formula:'6.72% + 1.80%',result:'8.52%'}]),(0,r.sl)('What is the typical market risk premium used in CAPM calculations?',2,12,6,'%'),(0,r.tf)([{s:'WACC decreases as a company adds more and more debt without limit',a:!1},{s:'Interest payments on debt are tax-deductible, reducing the effective cost of debt',a:!0},{s:'A stock with beta of 2.0 is expected to be twice as volatile as the market',a:!0},{s:'WACC is used as the discount rate in NPV calculations',a:!0},{s:'The cost of equity is always lower than the cost of debt',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the WACC Component',[['Risk-Free Rate','Return on government treasury bonds'],['Beta','Stock volatility relative to the market'],['Tax Shield','Tax savings from deductible interest'],['Market Risk Premium','Extra return demanded for stocks over bonds'],['WACC','Blended cost of all capital sources']]),(0,r.sc)('Your company\'s current WACC is 9%. The CFO proposes issuing $500M in cheap debt at 5% (after-tax: 3.75%) to buy back equity. This would move from 30% debt to 55% debt. What do you think?',[{label:'Great idea, replacing expensive equity with cheap debt will lower WACC',outcome:'Partially correct in theory, but 55% debt is aggressive. While the tax shield benefit is real, this level of leverage increases bankruptcy risk. Credit agencies may downgrade the debt, raising future borrowing costs. The initial WACC drop may reverse as risk premiums climb.',correct:!1},{label:'Risky, the tax shield helps but 55% debt significantly increases bankruptcy risk, and the WACC reduction may be offset by higher risk premiums',outcome:'Excellent analysis. You recognize the trade-off: the tax shield lowers WACC initially, but excessive leverage raises both the cost of debt (credit risk) and cost of equity (financial risk). The optimal point is likely somewhere between 30% and 55%.',correct:!0},{label:'Reject entirely, the company should have zero debt',outcome:'Zero debt means missing the tax shield entirely. Some debt is almost always optimal because interest tax deductions reduce WACC. The question is how much, not whether to use it at all.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Cost of Capital & WACC',[{takeaway:'WACC is the hurdle rate for all investments',detail:'Every project must earn above WACC to create value. WACC = (E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1-T)). It blends the cost of equity and after-tax cost of debt by their weights.'},{takeaway:'The tax shield makes debt cheaper, up to a point',detail:'Tax-deductible interest reduces debt\'s effective cost by the tax rate. But too much debt increases bankruptcy risk, raising all costs. The optimal capital structure balances these forces.'},{takeaway:'CAPM estimates the cost of equity',detail:'Re = Rf + \u03b2 \xd7 (Rm - Rf). Higher beta means more volatility, higher cost of equity, and a higher WACC hurdle rate. Risky companies need higher-return projects to create value.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Cost of Capital & WACC','Leveraged Buyouts & Private Equity','You\'ll learn how private equity firms use massive amounts of debt to acquire companies, restructure them, and sell them for profit, and why LBOs can either create enormous value or lead to spectacular collapses.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the WACC formula?',['Revenue \xf7 Assets, the ratio that measures how efficiently a company uses what it owns','(E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1-T))','Net Income \xf7 Equity, which is actually the return on equity rather than the cost of capital','Assets \u2212 Liabilities, which is the formula for shareholders\' equity, not the cost of capital'],1,'WACC blends the cost of equity and after-tax cost of debt, weighted by their proportions: (E/V \xd7 Re) + (D/V \xd7 Rd \xd7 (1-T)). The (1-T) factor captures the tax shield on debt.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is the after-tax cost of debt lower than the stated interest rate?',['Companies negotiate the rate down over time, so the stated rate is never the rate actually paid','Interest payments are tax-deductible, creating a tax shield that reduces the effective cost by the tax rate','Debt is risk-free, so lenders charge corporations almost nothing in real interest costs','Banks give large companies automatic discounts that quietly lower the true interest expense'],1,'At a 25% tax rate, a 6% interest rate effectively costs only 4.5% because $1.50 of every $6 in interest saves the company $1.50 in taxes. This tax shield makes debt cheaper than equity.'),(0,r.cq)('A stock has beta of 1.5, risk-free rate is 4%, market risk premium is 6%. What is the cost of equity?',['10%','13%','15%','9%'],1,'CAPM: Re = Rf + \u03b2 \xd7 (Rm - Rf) = 4% + 1.5 \xd7 6% = 4% + 9% = 13%. The high beta (1.5) means above-average risk, demanding a 13% return from equity investors.'),(0,r.cq)('Why can\'t a company minimize WACC by using 100% debt?',['It actually can, since debt is the cheapest source of capital it is optimal to use only debt','Excessive debt increases bankruptcy risk, causing both debt and equity costs to rise and offsetting the tax shield benefit','Government regulations cap the amount of debt a public company is legally allowed to carry','Equity holders would sue, because any added debt automatically violates their ownership rights'],1,'Beyond the optimal point, more debt increases default probability. Lenders demand higher rates, equity holders demand higher returns for the increased risk, and WACC rises. The optimal structure balances tax benefits against bankruptcy costs.'),(0,r.cq)('Two companies evaluate the same project returning 10%. Company A has 7% WACC, Company B has 13% WACC. Who should invest?',['Both, a 10% return is strong on its own and both firms benefit equally from taking it','Neither, since a 10% return is simply too low for any company to consider worthwhile','Only Company A, the project exceeds its 7% WACC but falls below Company B\'s 13% WACC','Only Company B, the firm with the higher WACC needs new projects the most to grow faster'],2,'WACC is the hurdle rate. For Company A (7% WACC), a 10% return creates 3% excess value, accept. For Company B (13% WACC), a 10% return destroys 3% of value, reject. Same project, different decisions based on cost of capital.')]),(0,r.L)(13,'Leveraged Buyouts & Private Equity',35,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Leveraged Buyouts & Private Equity','In 1988, KKR acquired RJR Nabisco for $25 billion in the largest leveraged buyout in history, using $20 billion in borrowed money. The deal, immortalized in "Barbarians at the Gate," transformed corporate America forever. Today, private equity firms manage over $8 trillion in assets and control companies employing 20 million Americans.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how LBOs work, how private equity firms create (and sometimes destroy) value, and the financial engineering behind the world\'s biggest deals.','LBO structure diagram showing equity contribution, debt layers, and target company cash flows',{type:'stack',title:'KKR, RJR Nabisco LBO (1988)',total:'$25B Deal',segments:[{label:'Debt',value:'$20B',percent:80,color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Equity',value:'$5B',percent:20,color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'The largest LBO in history, 80% borrowed money',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'LBO gone wrong',detail:'In 2005, private equity firms loaded Toys "R" Us with $7.5 billion in debt through an LBO. The massive interest payments ($400M/year) drained cash that could have funded e-commerce and store modernization. The company filed for bankruptcy in 2017 and liquidated, destroying 30,000 jobs.'},{label:'LBO done right',detail:'In 2013, Hilton Hotels was taken public after a Blackstone LBO. Blackstone bought Hilton for $26 billion in 2007, improved operations, expanded globally, and sold its stake for $14 billion profit, tripling its investment. The largest PE profit in history.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Toys "R" Us LBO',value:'$7.5B debt',detail:'$400M/year in interest drained cash from e-commerce',footer:'Bankrupt 2017, liquidated, 30,000 jobs destroyed',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Blackstone, Hilton LBO',points:[{label:'2007 buy',value:'$26B'},{label:'2010 trough',value:'$18B'},{label:'2013 exit',value:'$40B'}],trend:'up',footer:'Tripled the investment, the largest PE profit in history',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('LBO MECHANICS','How Leveraged Buyouts Actually Work',['A leveraged buyout (LBO) is the acquisition of a company using a significant amount of borrowed money (leverage), typically 60-80% debt and 20-40% equity. The target company\'s own assets and cash flows serve as collateral for the debt. It\'s like buying a house with a mortgage: you put down 20% and borrow 80%, then use the house (rental income) to pay the mortgage.','The LBO value creation model has three levers: (1) Debt paydown, the company\'s cash flows pay off acquisition debt, transferring value from lenders to equity holders. (2) Operational improvement, cut costs, grow revenue, improve margins. (3) Multiple expansion, buy at 8x EBITDA, improve the business, sell at 10x EBITDA. The combination of these three can generate 20-30% annual returns.','The PE fund structure: Limited Partners (pension funds, endowments, wealthy individuals) provide 95-99% of capital. General Partners (the PE firm) provide 1-5% but charge a 2% annual management fee plus 20% of profits (the "2 and 20" model). A PE fund typically holds companies for 3-7 years before selling.'],[{term:'Leveraged Buyout (LBO)',definition:'Acquisition of a company using primarily borrowed money, with the target\'s cash flows and assets serving as collateral.',example:'KKR acquired RJR Nabisco for $25B using $20B in debt (80%) and $5B in equity (20%).'},{term:'EBITDA Multiple',definition:'Enterprise Value divided by EBITDA, the standard valuation metric in PE. Buy low, sell high.',example:'Buying at 8x EBITDA ($800M for $100M EBITDA) and selling at 10x ($1B) creates $200M in value from multiple expansion alone.'},{term:'2 and 20',definition:'Standard PE fee structure: 2% annual management fee on committed capital plus 20% of profits above a hurdle rate.',example:'On a $10B fund, the PE firm earns $200M/year in management fees plus 20% of any gains above the 8% preferred return.'}],'LBO stack: Top layer (Equity 20-40%), Middle layer (Senior Debt 40-50%), Bottom layer (Mezzanine/High-Yield 10-20%), with arrows showing cash flow from target company servicing each layer',void 0,{type:'stack',title:'The LBO Capital Stack',total:'100% Purchase',segments:[{label:'Equity',value:'20-40%',percent:30,color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Senior Debt',value:'40-50%',percent:50,color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Mezzanine/High-Yield',value:'10-20%',percent:20,color:'#F59E0B'}],footer:'Target cash flows service every layer',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('An LBO uses 70% debt to acquire a company for $1 billion. The PE firm invests $300M in equity. After 5 years, the company is sold for $2 billion and all debt ($700M) is repaid. What is the equity return?',['100% ($2B - $1B = $1B profit, measuring the total company gain not the equity gain)','333% ($1B profit \xf7 $300M equity)','200% ($2B sale \xf7 $1B purchase, ignoring that the debt must be repaid out of the proceeds)','43% ($300M equity \xf7 $700M debt, which is a leverage ratio rather than a return figure)'],1,'Correct! Sale: $2B. Repay debt: $700M. Equity value: $1.3B. Profit on $300M equity: $1B. Return: $1B \xf7 $300M = 333%. This is leverage in action, the total return was 100% but the EQUITY return was 333% because debt amplified it.','Equity investors receive: $2B sale price \u2212 $700M debt repaid = $1.3B. They invested $300M, so profit = $1B. Return = $1B \xf7 $300M = 333%. Leverage amplified the 100% total return into a 333% equity return.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('LEVERAGE AMPLIFICATION','How Debt Magnifies Returns (And Losses)','comparison','Adjust the debt level and exit multiple to see how leverage amplifies both gains and losses for equity investors.',['Leverage is a double-edged sword. In the Hilton example, Blackstone\'s 3x return was amplified to a much higher equity return because debt magnified the gains. But in the Toys "R" Us example, the same leverage mechanism worked in reverse, when cash flows declined, there wasn\'t enough to service the debt, leading to bankruptcy and total equity loss.','The ideal LBO target has: (1) stable, predictable cash flows to service debt, (2) a strong market position with pricing power, (3) opportunities for operational improvement, (4) low existing debt, and (5) assets that can serve as collateral. Companies like Hilton Hotels, Dollar General, and Burger King have been successful LBO targets.','The "equity check", the PE firm\'s investment, is typically only 30-40% of the total acquisition price. This means a PE firm with a $5 billion fund can acquire companies worth $12-15 billion total. This leverage on leverage is how relatively small PE firms control massive portfolios of companies.'],[{term:'Debt Service Coverage Ratio',definition:'EBITDA \xf7 Total Debt Service (interest + principal). Measures whether cash flows can cover debt payments.',example:'DSCR of 2.0x means the company generates twice the cash needed for debt payments, comfortable. Below 1.0x signals distress.'}],['At 70% leverage, a 20% increase in company value translates to a 67% increase in equity value.','At 70% leverage, a 30% DECREASE in company value wipes out ALL equity, bankruptcy.','The best PE returns come from operational improvement + debt paydown, not just financial engineering.'],[{label:'Debt Level',min:30,max:85,default:65,unit:'%'},{label:'Exit Multiple (x EBITDA)',min:5,max:14,default:9,unit:'x'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('PE VALUE CREATION','What Private Equity Firms Actually Do',['The best PE firms are not just financial engineers, they\'re operators. After acquiring a company, they install experienced management, professionalize operations, and drive efficiency improvements. Dollar General\'s PE owners transformed it from a struggling retailer into one of America\'s fastest-growing stores by optimizing supply chain, real estate selection, and pricing.','The three sources of PE returns, in order of reliability: (1) Debt paydown (most reliable, cash flow mechanically reduces debt, transferring value to equity). (2) EBITDA growth (moderately reliable, operational improvements are within management\'s control). (3) Multiple expansion (least reliable, depends on market conditions at exit). The best PE returns combine all three.','Critics argue PE firms sometimes create value by cutting too aggressively, laying off workers, reducing quality, and loading companies with unsustainable debt. The Toys "R" Us and Sears examples support this critique. Defenders counter that PE provides capital, expertise, and accountability that many companies desperately need. The truth depends on the specific firm and deal.'],[{term:'Bolt-on Acquisition',definition:'A smaller acquisition made by a PE portfolio company to add capabilities, customers, or geographic reach.',example:'After acquiring a regional dental chain, a PE firm buys 15 smaller practices to create a national platform, a "buy and build" strategy.'},{term:'Exit Strategy',definition:'How a PE firm sells its investment, typically through IPO, sale to a strategic buyer, or sale to another PE firm.',example:'Blackstone took Hilton public via IPO in 2013, gradually selling shares over the following years for a total $14B profit.'}],'Value creation bridge: Entry EBITDA \u2192 Operational Improvement \u2192 Revenue Growth \u2192 Debt Paydown \u2192 Multiple Expansion \u2192 Exit Value',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'PE Value Creation Bridge',nodes:[{label:'Entry EBITDA'},{label:'Operational Improvement'},{label:'Revenue Growth'},{label:'Debt Paydown'},{label:'Multiple Expansion'},{label:'Exit Value'}],footer:'The best PE returns combine all three levers',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The standard private equity fee structure of 2% management fee plus 20% of profits is called ___.',['2 and 20','fee and carry','management premium','performance fee'],0),(0,r.q)('A PE firm buys a company for 8x EBITDA ($160M for $20M EBITDA) using 65% debt. Over 4 years, EBITDA grows to $30M and they sell at 10x ($300M). The remaining debt is $60M. What is the approximate equity return?',['88% total return, measuring the rise in company value rather than the return on equity invested','329% equity return, ($300M - $60M debt - $56M original equity) = $184M profit on $56M invested','140% return, dividing the profit by the full $160M purchase price instead of the equity invested','50% return, applying the EBITDA growth rate as if it were the return to equity holders'],1,'Original equity: 35% \xd7 $160M = $56M. Exit: $300M \u2212 $60M remaining debt = $240M equity value. Profit: $184M on $56M = 329%. All three value levers fired: EBITDA grew 50%, multiple expanded from 8x to 10x, and debt paydown converted $44M of debt into equity value.'),(0,r.tr)('LBO & Private Equity Essentials',[['What makes a good LBO target?','Stable cash flows, strong market position, improvement potential, low existing debt, and valuable assets for collateral.'],['What are the three PE value creation levers?','Debt paydown (reliable), operational improvement (moderate), and multiple expansion (market-dependent).'],['Why is leverage a double-edged sword?','It amplifies both gains and losses. 70% debt turns a 20% gain into 67% equity return, but a 30% decline wipes out all equity.'],['What is the "2 and 20" model?','PE firms charge 2% annual management fee on capital plus 20% of profits above a hurdle rate (typically 8%).']]),(0,r.sc)('A PE firm is evaluating two LBO targets. Company A: Stable utility with $50M EBITDA, 5% growth, but already efficient. Company B: Turnaround retailer with $30M EBITDA declining 3%/year, but potential for major operational improvement. Which is better?',[{label:'Company A, stable cash flows are perfect for LBO debt service',outcome:'Good instinct on cash flow stability, but limited upside. With only 5% growth and no improvement opportunity, returns will come mainly from debt paydown and multiple expansion, typically generating 12-15% IRR. Solid but not spectacular.',correct:!0},{label:'Company B, turnaround potential means higher returns',outcome:'High risk, high reward. Declining EBITDA means debt service could become unsustainable. Turnaround success rates are only ~30%. If it works, returns could be 40%+; if it fails, total equity loss. Most PE firms avoid declining businesses for LBOs.',correct:!1},{label:'Neither, too much risk in both',outcome:'Company A is actually a classic PE target. Stable cash flows, reasonable growth, and low risk of debt distress. Being too conservative means missing solid opportunities.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'LBOs typically use 60-80% debt financing',a:!0},{s:'Private equity firms typically hold investments for 15-20 years',a:!1},{s:'The target company\'s cash flows are used to repay LBO debt',a:!0},{s:'All LBOs create value for the acquired company',a:!1},{s:'PE firms charge "2 and 20", 2% management fee plus 20% of profits',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the PE Term',[['Limited Partners','Pension funds and endowments that provide capital'],['General Partners','The PE firm that manages investments'],['Bolt-on Acquisition','Smaller purchases to grow a portfolio company'],['Exit Strategy','How the PE firm eventually sells the investment'],['Carry','The 20% share of profits PE managers earn']]),(0,r.ds)('Order the LBO process from start to finish:',['Identify target company with stable cash flows','Arrange debt financing (bank loans, bonds)','Contribute PE equity (20-40% of purchase price)','Close acquisition and install new management','Implement operational improvements and pay down debt','Exit via IPO or sale after 3-7 years']),(0,r.summaryCards)('Leveraged Buyouts & Private Equity',[{takeaway:'LBOs use debt to amplify equity returns',detail:'60-80% debt means a 20% increase in company value can translate to 60-80% equity return. But leverage amplifies losses too, a 30% decline at 70% leverage wipes out all equity.'},{takeaway:'PE value creation has three levers',detail:'Debt paydown (most reliable), operational improvement (moderate), and multiple expansion (market-dependent). The best PE returns combine all three.'},{takeaway:'The ideal LBO target has stable, predictable cash flows',detail:'Debt service requires consistent cash generation. Companies with volatile earnings make poor LBO candidates because a bad year can trigger default.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Leveraged Buyouts & Private Equity','IPOs & Going Public','You\'ll learn what happens when private companies go public, the IPO process, pricing mechanics, and why the biggest "pop" on IPO day actually means the company left money on the table.',25)],[(0,r.cq)('In an LBO, what serves as collateral for the acquisition debt?',['The PE firm\'s other portfolio companies, which are pledged to back each new acquisition loan','The target company\'s own assets and cash flows','Government guarantees, since federal programs backstop the debt used in private equity deals','The broader stock market, with the lender holding shares of unrelated public companies as security'],1,'LBO debt is secured by the acquired company\'s assets and repaid from its cash flows. This is why stable, asset-rich companies with predictable cash flows are ideal LBO targets.'),(0,r.cq)('A PE firm invests $200M equity in an LBO. Five years later, the equity is worth $800M. What is the return?',['200%','300%','400%','600%'],1,'Return = ($800M - $200M) \xf7 $200M = 300%. The PE firm quadrupled its money (4x), which is a 300% return. This is typical of a successful LBO where all three value levers fire.'),(0,r.cq)('Why did the Toys "R" Us LBO fail?',['The market for toys collapsed entirely, leaving the company with no customers to sell to','The $7.5B in debt created $400M/year in interest payments that drained cash needed for e-commerce investment and store modernization','The PE firms ran the day-to-day operations badly, making poor merchandising and staffing choices','Children simply stopped playing with toys, so demand for the entire product category disappeared'],1,'The massive debt burden consumed cash flow that should have funded the digital transformation. While Amazon was investing billions in e-commerce, Toys "R" Us was sending billions to bondholders.'),(0,r.cq)('What are the three sources of PE value creation?',['Revenue growth, cost cutting, and layoffs, treating workforce reduction as a value lever itself','Debt paydown, operational improvement, and multiple expansion','Higher prices, lower wages, and tax avoidance, squeezing customers and workers to lift profits','Financial engineering alone, since rearranging the balance sheet is the only real PE skill'],1,'Debt paydown mechanically transfers value to equity. Operational improvement grows EBITDA. Multiple expansion increases the valuation ratio at exit. The best PE deals combine all three for 25%+ annual returns.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is leverage called a "double-edged sword" in LBOs?',['It simply costs too much, since the interest on acquisition debt eats away all the deal profit','It amplifies both gains AND losses, a 20% gain at 70% leverage becomes 67% equity return, but a 30% loss wipes out all equity','It is illegal in some states, so PE firms can only structure leveraged deals in certain places','It only works in bull markets, because leverage produces no benefit at all when prices are flat'],1,'At 70% debt, equity holders capture all upside on 100% of the company value with only 30% of the capital at risk. But they also absorb all losses first. A small decline in value can destroy equity completely.')]),(0,r.L)(14,'IPOs & Going Public',35,[(0,r.hookOpener)('IPOs & Going Public','When Facebook went public in May 2012 at $38/share, it raised $16 billion, the largest tech IPO in history. But the stock dropped 50% within months as investors questioned its mobile strategy. Meanwhile, when Snowflake IPO\'d in 2020 at $120, the stock doubled on day one to $245, meaning the company "left" $3.4 billion on the table. The IPO process is one of the most consequential and misunderstood events in corporate finance.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how the IPO process works, the role of underwriters, pricing mechanics, and why the biggest first-day "pop" actually represents money the company gave away.','IPO timeline from S-1 filing through roadshow to first-day trading',{type:'comparison',title:'Snowflake IPO (2020)',rows:[{label:'Offer price',value:'$120',percent:47},{label:'Day-one close',value:'$254',percent:100}],footer:'A 112% pop, $3.4B "left on the table"',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'IPO mispriced',detail:'WeWork filed for an IPO at a $47 billion valuation in 2019. During the roadshow, investors analyzed the S-1 and discovered staggering losses, governance nightmares, and self-dealing by CEO Adam Neumann. The IPO was pulled. When WeWork finally went public via SPAC in 2021 at $9B, it later filed for bankruptcy.'},{label:'IPO executed well',detail:'Google\'s 2004 IPO used a Dutch auction to set the price at $85/share, minimizing the first-day pop to 18%. This meant Google captured more of its own value versus giving it away to institutional investors who flip shares on day one. Google\'s $1.67B raised funded the growth to a $2T company.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'line',title:'WeWork: IPO Pulled',points:[{label:'2019 S-1 filed',value:'$47B'},{label:'2021 SPAC',value:'$9B'},{label:'2023 bankruptcy',value:'$0'}],trend:'down',footer:'The S-1 revealed losses, governance failures, self-dealing',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Google: Dutch Auction IPO',value:'18% pop only',detail:'Auction pricing captured more of Google\'s own value',footer:'$1.67B raised funded growth to a $2T company',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE IPO PROCESS','How Private Companies Become Public',['An Initial Public Offering (IPO) is when a private company sells shares to the public for the first time. Companies go public to raise capital for growth, provide liquidity for early investors and employees, increase visibility, and use stock as acquisition currency. The process typically takes 6-12 months and costs 3-7% of proceeds in underwriting fees.','The IPO process: (1) Select underwriters (investment banks like Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley). (2) File S-1 registration with the SEC, this document discloses financials, risks, use of proceeds. (3) SEC review and comment period. (4) Roadshow, management presents to institutional investors in 2 weeks of meetings. (5) Book-building, underwriters collect investor interest and set the price range. (6) Pricing, the final offer price is set the night before trading begins.','The underwriter\'s dilemma: banks want the IPO to "pop" (go up 15-25% on day one) because it rewards their institutional clients who get IPO allocations. But every dollar of pop is money the issuing company left on the table. A 50% first-day pop on a $10B IPO means the company could have raised $5 billion more. The best CFOs push for accurate pricing; the system incentivizes underpricing.'],[{term:'IPO (Initial Public Offering)',definition:'The first sale of a company\'s stock to public investors, transitioning from private to public ownership.',example:'Airbnb\'s December 2020 IPO priced at $68/share, raising $3.5B. It opened at $146, a 115% pop.'},{term:'Underwriter',definition:'An investment bank that manages the IPO process: due diligence, SEC filing, roadshow, pricing, and share distribution.',example:'Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley co-led Facebook\'s $16B IPO, earning ~$176M in underwriting fees.'},{term:'S-1 Filing',definition:'The SEC registration statement disclosing a company\'s financials, business model, risks, and planned use of IPO proceeds.',example:'WeWork\'s S-1 revealed $1.7B in losses on $1.8B revenue, governance red flags, and CEO self-dealing, killing the IPO.'},{term:'First-Day Pop',definition:'The percentage increase from IPO offer price to the closing price on the first day of trading. Represents money left on the table.',example:'Snowflake IPO\'d at $120, closed at $254 on day one, a 112% pop, meaning ~$3.4B left on the table.'}],'Funnel diagram: S-1 Filing \u2192 SEC Review \u2192 Roadshow \u2192 Book Building \u2192 Pricing \u2192 First Day of Trading',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The IPO Process',nodes:[{label:'S-1 Filing'},{label:'SEC Review'},{label:'Roadshow'},{label:'Book Building'},{label:'Pricing'},{label:'First Day of Trading'}],footer:'Every dollar of "pop" is money the company left behind',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('Snowflake priced its IPO at $120/share, selling 28M shares. The stock closed at $254 on day one. How much money did Snowflake "leave on the table"?',['$1.34 billion','$3.75 billion','$254 million','$7.1 billion'],1,'Correct! ($254 - $120) \xd7 28M shares = $134 \xd7 28M = $3.75 billion. If Snowflake had priced at $254, it could have raised an additional $3.75B. That "pop" was a transfer of wealth from Snowflake to investors who received IPO allocations.','Money left on the table = (Close price - Offer price) \xd7 Shares sold = ($254 - $120) \xd7 28M = $3.75B. The underwriters\' institutional clients captured this value, not Snowflake.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('IPO PRICING DYNAMICS','How Underpricing Transfers Wealth','comparison','Adjust the IPO offer price and first-day close to see how much money gets left on the table versus raised for the company.',['IPO underpricing is systematic, the average first-day return for US IPOs has been 15-20% since the 1990s. This means companies collectively leave billions on the table annually. Why does it persist? Underwriters benefit from rewarding institutional clients with cheap shares. Institutional investors reciprocate with trading commissions and future business.','Alternative IPO methods have emerged. Direct listings (Spotify, Coinbase) skip underwriters, existing shares trade directly on the exchange with no new capital raised but no underwriting fees or lockup periods. SPACs (Special Purpose Acquisition Companies) offer a "blank check" merger path but have been plagued by conflicts of interest and poor post-merger performance.','The lockup period (typically 90-180 days) prevents insiders from selling immediately after the IPO. When lockups expire, supply increases often push prices down. Smart investors watch lockup expiration dates. Facebook dropped from $38 to $19 partly because 1.5 billion locked-up shares were about to become eligible for sale.'],[{term:'Direct Listing',definition:'An alternative to traditional IPO where existing shares trade directly on the exchange without underwriters, new shares, or a lockup period.',example:'Spotify went public via direct listing in 2018, avoiding ~$300M in underwriting fees.'},{term:'SPAC',definition:'Special Purpose Acquisition Company, a shell company that IPOs and then merges with a private company to take it public.',example:'Virgin Galactic went public through a SPAC merger with Social Capital Hedosophia in 2019.'},{term:'Lockup Period',definition:'A 90-180 day period after an IPO during which insiders cannot sell their shares.',example:'When Facebook\'s lockup expired, 1.5B shares hit the market, contributing to the stock\'s decline from $38 to $19.'}],['The average US IPO first-day pop is 15-20%, representing a systematic wealth transfer from companies to institutional investors.','Direct listings save 3-7% in underwriting fees but raise no new capital for the company.','SPAC mergers peaked at 613 deals in 2021 but declined 90%+ as regulatory scrutiny increased and returns disappointed.'],[{label:'Offer Price ($)',min:20,max:200,default:100,unit:'$'},{label:'First-Day Close ($)',min:20,max:400,default:140,unit:'$'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('IPO READINESS & ALTERNATIVES','When and Why Companies Go Public',['Not every company should IPO. The ideal IPO candidate has: (1) consistent revenue growth (25%+), (2) a clear path to profitability, (3) a large addressable market, (4) strong management team, and (5) a compelling narrative for public investors. Companies that IPO too early face brutal public market scrutiny, quarterly earnings pressure, short sellers, and activist investors.','The costs of being public are substantial: $2-5M/year in regulatory compliance (SOX), quarterly reporting pressure that discourages long-term thinking, executive distraction from investor relations, and vulnerability to hostile takeovers and activist campaigns. Some companies (like Dell in 2013) actually go private to escape these pressures.','The decision to go public is one of the most consequential in a company\'s lifecycle. It provides capital and liquidity but surrenders control and privacy. Warren Buffett has kept Berkshire Hathaway public but structured it to resist short-term pressures. Many founders (Zuckerberg, Brin/Page) maintain control through dual-class share structures that give them 10x voting power per share.'],[{term:'Dual-Class Shares',definition:'A share structure where founders hold shares with 10x voting power, maintaining control even after going public.',example:'Mark Zuckerberg controls 58% of Facebook\'s voting power with only 13% economic ownership through Class B shares (10 votes each).'},{term:'Quiet Period',definition:'SEC-mandated period before and after an IPO when the company cannot make promotional statements about the stock.',example:'During the quiet period, the CEO cannot give media interviews hyping the stock, only the prospectus speaks.'},{term:'Green Shoe Option',definition:'An overallotment option allowing underwriters to sell up to 15% more shares than originally planned if demand is strong.',example:'If an IPO sells 10M shares, the green shoe allows selling up to 11.5M shares to meet excess demand.'}],'Decision tree: Stay Private (control, privacy, flexibility) vs. Go Public (capital, liquidity, visibility, but quarterly scrutiny and compliance costs)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'IPO Readiness & Alternatives',items:[{label:'Traditional IPO',value:'Underwriters, new capital',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Direct Listing',value:'No underwriters, no new capital',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'SPAC',value:'Blank-check merger path',color:'#F59E0B'}],footer:'Being public costs $2-5M/year in compliance',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The SEC registration document that discloses a company\'s financials, risks, and business model before an IPO is called the ___.',['10-K','S-1','10-Q','8-K'],1),(0,r.q)('Google used a Dutch auction for its IPO instead of the traditional book-building process. Why?',['It was cheaper to administer, since a Dutch auction skips the underwriting fees of a normal IPO','A Dutch auction lets the market set the price directly, minimizing the first-day pop and ensuring the company captures more of its own value instead of gifting it to institutional investors','Google\'s bankers strongly recommended it, as it gave the underwriters more control over allocations','It was required by the SEC, which mandates Dutch auctions for any technology company going public'],1,'In a Dutch auction, all investors bid and the price is set where demand meets supply. This reduces the underwriter\'s ability to underprice the stock for their clients. Google\'s 18% first-day pop was much smaller than the 40-80% pops common in 2004 tech IPOs.'),(0,r.pc)('IPO Money Left on the Table',[{label:'Shares offered',formula:'28M shares',result:'28,000,000'},{label:'IPO offer price',formula:'Set by underwriters',result:'$120'},{label:'Capital raised',formula:'28M \xd7 $120',result:'$3.36B'},{label:'Day-one close',formula:'Market price',result:'$254'},{label:'Money left on table',formula:'($254 \u2212 $120) \xd7 28M',result:'$3.75B'},{label:'Effective underpricing',formula:'$3.75B \xf7 ($3.36B + $3.75B)',result:'52.7%'}]),(0,r.sc)('You\'re the CFO of a fast-growing SaaS company with $500M ARR growing 40%/year. Your board wants to IPO. The lead underwriter (Goldman Sachs) suggests pricing at $8B valuation, but your DCF shows $12B intrinsic value. What do you do?',[{label:'Accept the $8B pricing, Goldman knows the market and a big first-day pop will generate positive press',outcome:'You just gave away ~$4B in value. A 50% first-day pop means institutional investors captured $4B that should have gone to your company and existing shareholders. Positive press from a pop is fleeting; the lost capital is permanent.',correct:!1},{label:'Push back hard, present your DCF analysis, demand $11-12B pricing, and threaten to switch underwriters or pursue a direct listing if they won\'t negotiate',outcome:'Excellent. You have leverage, a hot company with 40% growth doesn\'t need to accept lowball pricing. The threat of a direct listing removes the underwriter\'s monopoly power. Pricing closer to intrinsic value maximizes capital raised for your shareholders.',correct:!0},{label:'Cancel the IPO and stay private until the market values you at $12B+',outcome:'Not unreasonable, but you lose the opportunity to raise growth capital and provide employee liquidity. A better approach is to negotiate harder on pricing rather than abandoning the IPO entirely.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'An IPO first-day "pop" is always good for the company going public',a:!1},{s:'The S-1 filing must disclose the company\'s financials, risks, and use of proceeds',a:!0},{s:'A direct listing allows a company to go public without underwriters or new share issuance',a:!0},{s:'The lockup period allows insiders to sell shares immediately after the IPO',a:!1},{s:'Dual-class share structures give founders outsized voting control relative to economic ownership',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the IPO Term',[['Book Building','Underwriters collect investor interest to set the IPO price'],['Green Shoe','Option to sell 15% more shares if demand is strong'],['Lockup Period','90-180 days where insiders cannot sell shares'],['Direct Listing','Going public without underwriters or new shares'],['Dutch Auction','Market-based pricing where investors bid directly']]),(0,r.ds)('Order the traditional IPO process:',['Select investment bank underwriters','File S-1 registration statement with the SEC','SEC review, comments, and amendments','Management roadshow to institutional investors (2 weeks)','Book building, collect investor demand and set price range','Final pricing set the night before trading begins','First day of public trading on the exchange']),(0,r.summaryCards)('IPOs & Going Public',[{takeaway:'The IPO process is designed to benefit underwriters and their clients',detail:'Systematic 15-20% first-day underpricing transfers billions annually from issuing companies to institutional investors. The underwriter profits by rewarding its buy-side clients, who reciprocate with commissions.'},{takeaway:'First-day pops represent money left on the table',detail:'A 50% pop on a $10B IPO means $5B the company could have raised. Alternatives like direct listings and Dutch auctions reduce this wealth transfer.'},{takeaway:'Going public is a trade-off: capital and liquidity vs. control and privacy',detail:'Public markets provide growth capital and employee liquidity but impose quarterly scrutiny, compliance costs ($2-5M/year for SOX), and vulnerability to activist investors.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('IPOs & Going Public','Financial Distress & Restructuring','You\'ll learn what happens when companies can\'t pay their debts, the mechanics of bankruptcy, restructuring, and how distressed investors turn corporate catastrophe into profit.',30)],[(0,r.cq)('What does "money left on the table" mean in an IPO context?',['The fees paid to underwriters, which reduce the net proceeds the company ultimately receives','The difference between the first-day closing price and the IPO offer price, multiplied by shares sold, representing capital the company could have raised','The cash the company sets aside in reserve rather than using it to fund growth projects','The taxes paid on the IPO proceeds, which lower the amount the company gets to keep'],1,'If the stock closes 50% above the offer price, the company could have priced 50% higher and raised more capital. That difference is wealth transferred from the company to IPO investors.'),(0,r.cq)('Snowflake IPO\'d at $120 and closed day one at $254. With 28M shares sold, how much was left on the table?',['$120M','$3.75B','$7.1B','$254M'],1,'($254 \u2212 $120) \xd7 28M = $134 \xd7 28M = $3.75B. Snowflake effectively gave $3.75B to investors who received IPO allocations at $120.'),(0,r.cq)('What is the main advantage of a direct listing over a traditional IPO?',['It raises more capital, since selling shares directly brings in more cash than a normal IPO','It avoids 3-7% underwriting fees and eliminates the systematic underpricing problem, though no new capital is raised','It guarantees a higher stock price, because skipping underwriters always lifts the opening valuation','It is much faster to complete, since a direct listing has no SEC review or filing requirements'],1,'Direct listings skip underwriters entirely, existing shares trade on the exchange. No underwriting fees (3-7% savings) and no institutional client favoritism. But the company doesn\'t raise new capital.'),(0,r.cq)('Why did WeWork\'s IPO fail in 2019?',['The overall stock market crashed in 2019, closing the IPO window for every company that year','The S-1 revealed massive losses, governance failures, and CEO self-dealing, investors rejected the $47B valuation after reading the actual financials','WeWork ran out of office space to lease, so it had no product left to sell to its customers','Competitors undercut WeWork\'s pricing, pushing it out of the shared-office market before the IPO'],1,'The S-1 is meant to inform investors. WeWork\'s S-1 disclosed $1.7B losses, a CEO who leased his own buildings back to the company, and a made-up "Community Adjusted EBITDA" metric. Transparency worked, investors said no.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a dual-class share structure and why do founders use it?',['Two different stock prices for the same company, with one class trading higher than the other','A structure where founder shares have 10x voting power, allowing founders to maintain control (e.g., 58% voting power with 13% economic ownership) even after dilution from the IPO','Shares that pay double the normal dividend, used to reward the company\'s earliest investors','A way to avoid SEC regulations, letting founders skip the disclosure rules other companies face'],1,'Dual-class structures let founders maintain control after going public. Zuckerberg, Brin/Page, and Snap\'s Evan Spiegel all use this structure. Critics argue it reduces accountability; proponents say it enables long-term thinking.')]),(0,r.L)(15,'Financial Distress & Restructuring',35,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Financial Distress & Restructuring','In 2009, General Motors, once the world\'s largest company, filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy with $172 billion in debt. The US government injected $49.5 billion in taxpayer money. Forty days later, a "New GM" emerged: leaner, debt-free, and on a path to profitability. Bankruptcy didn\'t kill GM, it saved it. Understanding financial distress is understanding how companies die, survive, and sometimes come back stronger.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand the causes of financial distress, Chapter 7 vs. Chapter 11 bankruptcy, the restructuring process, and how distressed debt investors profit from corporate catastrophe.','Timeline showing GM\'s journey from $172B debt to Chapter 11 to emergence as New GM',{type:'line',title:'GM: Distress to Rebirth',points:[{label:'2009 debt',value:'$172B'},{label:'After Chapter 11',value:'$17B'},{label:'2011 profit',value:'$7.6B'}],trend:'up',footer:'Bankruptcy didn\'t kill GM, it saved it',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'No restructuring option',detail:'Lehman Brothers filed for Chapter 11 in September 2008, the largest bankruptcy in US history at $639 billion in assets. Unlike GM, Lehman had no viable restructuring path. The liquidation triggered a global financial crisis that destroyed $10 trillion in wealth worldwide.'},{label:'Successful restructuring',detail:'Marvel Entertainment filed for bankruptcy in 1996 with $700M in debt. Through Chapter 11 restructuring, it shed debt, retained its IP portfolio, and emerged lean. Disney later acquired Marvel for $4 billion in 2009. Today, Marvel IP has generated $30B+ in box office revenue alone.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Lehman Brothers',value:'$639B bankruptcy',detail:'No viable restructuring path, pure liquidation',footer:'Triggered a crisis that destroyed $10T worldwide',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Marvel: Restructured & Reborn',points:[{label:'1996 debt',value:'$700M'},{label:'1998 restructured',value:'$250M'},{label:'2009 Disney sale',value:'$4B'}],trend:'up',footer:'Marvel IP has since generated $30B+ in box office',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('WHEN COMPANIES CAN\'T PAY','The Mechanics of Financial Distress',['Financial distress occurs when a company cannot meet its debt obligations. Warning signs include: declining cash flows, rising debt-to-equity ratios, covenant violations, credit rating downgrades, and negative working capital. The "zone of insolvency", where total liabilities approach or exceed total assets, is where management\'s fiduciary duty shifts from shareholders to creditors.','Chapter 7 bankruptcy is liquidation: the company ceases operations, a trustee sells all assets, and proceeds are distributed to creditors in strict priority (secured creditors \u2192 unsecured creditors \u2192 preferred stock \u2192 common stock). Shareholders almost always receive nothing. Chapter 11 is reorganization: the company continues operating while restructuring its debts under court protection. A reorganization plan must be approved by creditors and the court.','The Absolute Priority Rule governs who gets paid first in bankruptcy: (1) Secured creditors (backed by collateral), (2) Administrative claims (lawyers, advisors), (3) Unsecured creditors (bondholders, suppliers), (4) Preferred stockholders, (5) Common stockholders. In practice, common equity is almost always wiped out, which is why bankruptcy risk matters so much to stock investors.'],[{term:'Chapter 11',definition:'Bankruptcy reorganization, the company continues operating while restructuring debts under court supervision. A plan must be approved by creditors.',example:'GM used Chapter 11 to shed $172B in debt, close unprofitable brands (Pontiac, Saturn), and emerge in 40 days as a viable company.'},{term:'Chapter 7',definition:'Bankruptcy liquidation, the company ceases all operations. A trustee sells assets and distributes proceeds to creditors by priority.',example:'Toys "R" Us converted from Chapter 11 to Chapter 7 in 2018 when no viable reorganization plan could be developed, liquidating all 735 US stores.'},{term:'Absolute Priority Rule',definition:'The legal order of payment in bankruptcy: secured debt \u2192 unsecured debt \u2192 preferred equity \u2192 common equity.',example:'In Lehman\'s bankruptcy, secured creditors recovered ~40 cents on the dollar. Unsecured creditors got ~27 cents. Common shareholders got zero.'},{term:'Debtor-in-Possession (DIP)',definition:'In Chapter 11, the existing management typically continues running the company under court oversight, known as debtor-in-possession.',example:'GM\'s management stayed in place during Chapter 11 to maintain operational continuity while restructuring the balance sheet.'}],'Priority waterfall: Secured Creditors (top, most protected) \u2192 Unsecured Creditors \u2192 Preferred Stock \u2192 Common Stock (bottom, wiped out first)',void 0,{type:'stack',title:'Absolute Priority Waterfall',total:'Who Gets Paid',segments:[{label:'Secured Creditors',value:'Paid first',percent:40,color:'#10B981'},{label:'Unsecured Creditors',value:'Next',percent:30,color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Preferred Stock',value:'Then',percent:20,color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Common Stock',value:'Last, usually $0',percent:10,color:'#EF4444'}],footer:'Common equity is almost always wiped out',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company in Chapter 11 has $500M in assets. It owes $300M to secured creditors, $250M to unsecured creditors, and has $100M in equity. After reorganization, assets are revalued at $400M. Who gets what?',['Everyone gets a proportional share, with the $400M divided in proportion to what each is owed','Secured creditors get $300M in full, unsecured get $100M (40 cents on the dollar), equity gets $0','Each of the three groups splits the $400M equally, taking roughly $133M apiece regardless of priority','Secured creditors get $200M and unsecured get $200M, splitting the assets evenly between the two'],1,'Correct! The Absolute Priority Rule: secured creditors are paid first ($300M in full from $400M), leaving $100M for unsecured creditors ($100M/$250M = 40 cents on the dollar). Common equity is wiped out, nothing remains after creditors.','Absolute Priority: Secured creditors ($300M) \u2192 Remaining: $100M \u2192 Unsecured creditors get $100M of $250M owed (40\xa2/$1) \u2192 Equity: $0. Shareholders are last in line and first to lose everything.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('DISTRESSED DEBT INVESTING','How Investors Profit from Bankruptcy','comparison','Adjust the recovery rate and purchase price to see how distressed debt investors profit by buying bonds of bankrupt companies at deep discounts.',['Distressed debt investors (like Oaktree Capital, Apollo) buy the bonds of bankrupt or near-bankrupt companies at 20-40 cents on the dollar, then profit when restructuring recovers 50-80 cents. The spread between purchase price and recovery is the profit. Howard Marks built a $180 billion empire at Oaktree primarily through distressed debt investing.','The restructuring process: (1) File Chapter 11 and get automatic stay (creditors can\'t seize assets). (2) Negotiate with creditor committees. (3) Develop a Plan of Reorganization (debt reduction, asset sales, new capital structure). (4) Creditors vote on the plan. (5) Court confirms the plan. (6) Company emerges from bankruptcy with a clean balance sheet. The entire process typically takes 6-18 months.','Debt-to-equity conversions are the most common restructuring tool: unsecured creditors agree to convert their debt claims into equity in the reorganized company. They take a haircut on face value but gain ownership of a now-viable company. This is how distressed investors become the new owners, buying debt at 30 cents, converting to equity worth 60 cents.'],[{term:'Automatic Stay',definition:'A Chapter 11 protection that immediately halts all creditor collection actions, lawsuits, and asset seizures when bankruptcy is filed.',example:'When Hertz filed Chapter 11, the automatic stay prevented creditors from seizing its 700,000-vehicle fleet while restructuring was negotiated.'},{term:'Debt-to-Equity Swap',definition:'Creditors exchange debt claims for equity ownership in the restructured company, reducing the debt burden and gaining ownership.',example:'In GM\'s restructuring, bondholders who were owed $27B received 10% equity in the new GM, a massive haircut but better than zero in liquidation.'}],['Distressed debt investors typically buy at 20-40 cents on the dollar and aim for 50-80 cent recoveries.','The average Chapter 11 process takes 6-18 months from filing to emergence.','Approximately 70% of companies that file Chapter 11 successfully reorganize; 30% convert to Chapter 7 liquidation.'],[{label:'Bond Purchase Price (\xa2/$1)',min:5,max:60,default:30,unit:'\xa2'},{label:'Recovery Rate (\xa2/$1)',min:20,max:90,default:60,unit:'\xa2'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('RESTRUCTURING STRATEGIES','How Companies Come Back from the Brink',['Out-of-court restructuring (workouts) avoids the cost and stigma of bankruptcy. The company negotiates directly with creditors to extend maturities, reduce interest rates, or convert debt to equity. Workouts are faster and cheaper but require creditor cooperation, a single holdout can force formal bankruptcy filing.','Operational restructuring accompanies financial restructuring: closing unprofitable divisions, reducing headcount, renegotiating contracts, and selling non-core assets. GM closed Pontiac, Saturn, Hummer, and Saab during its restructuring, focusing resources on Chevrolet, Buick, GMC, and Cadillac. The leaner company generated higher margins.','Prepackaged bankruptcy ("pre-pack") is a hybrid: the company negotiates the restructuring plan with creditors BEFORE filing Chapter 11. The bankruptcy filing is then a formality, in and out in 30-60 days versus 12-18 months. This preserves business value by minimizing the time in bankruptcy court and reducing uncertainty for customers and suppliers.'],[{term:'Workout',definition:'An out-of-court restructuring where the company negotiates directly with creditors to modify debt terms without filing for bankruptcy.',example:'A company might negotiate extending bond maturities from 3 years to 7 years and reducing the interest rate from 8% to 5% in a workout.'},{term:'Prepackaged Bankruptcy',definition:'A restructuring plan negotiated with creditors before filing Chapter 11, allowing rapid emergence (30-60 days vs. 12-18 months).',example:'CEC Entertainment (Chuck E. Cheese) used a pre-pack to restructure $1.9B in debt and emerge from Chapter 11 in just 6 months during COVID.'}],'Three paths from distress: Out-of-Court Workout (fastest, cheapest) \u2192 Prepackaged Chapter 11 (moderate) \u2192 Traditional Chapter 11 (longest, most expensive)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'Three Paths from Distress',nodes:[{label:'Out-of-Court Workout',value:'Fastest, cheapest',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Prepackaged Chapter 11',value:'30-60 days',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Traditional Chapter 11',value:'12-18 months',color:'#F59E0B'}],footer:'Pre-packs preserve business value by minimizing court time',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The bankruptcy chapter that allows a company to continue operating while restructuring debts is Chapter ___.',['7','11','13','15'],1),(0,r.q)('A company\'s bonds trade at 25 cents on the dollar. A distressed investor buys $100M face value for $25M. After restructuring, bonds recover at 55 cents. What is the investor\'s return?',['55% return, mistaking the 55-cent recovery rate itself for the investor\'s percentage gain','120% return, ($55M - $25M) / $25M = 120%','30% return, taking the gap between the 55-cent recovery and the 25-cent cost as the return','200% return, dividing the $55M recovery by the $25M cost without subtracting the cost first'],1,'Purchase: $25M. Recovery: 55% \xd7 $100M = $55M. Profit: $30M. Return: $30M/$25M = 120%. This is the distressed debt playbook, buy at deep discounts, profit from restructuring recovery.'),(0,r.tr)('Financial Distress Essentials',[['What triggers financial distress?','Declining cash flows, covenant violations, inability to service debt, credit downgrades, and negative working capital.'],['Chapter 7 vs. Chapter 11?','Chapter 7 = liquidation (company dies). Chapter 11 = reorganization (company restructures and continues operating).'],['What is the Absolute Priority Rule?','Secured creditors \u2192 Unsecured creditors \u2192 Preferred equity \u2192 Common equity. Each level must be fully paid before the next receives anything.'],['How do distressed investors profit?','Buy bonds at 20-40 cents, wait for restructuring to recover 50-80 cents. The spread is the profit.']]),(0,r.sc)('You\'re on the board of a retail chain with $2B in debt and declining revenues. The company has 6 months of cash left. A creditor group offers a workout: extend maturities 3 years and reduce interest by 2%, in exchange for 30% equity. The alternative is Chapter 11. What do you recommend?',[{label:'Accept the workout, Chapter 11 would destroy the brand and scare away customers',outcome:'Good instinct. The workout avoids $50-100M in bankruptcy costs, preserves customer confidence, and keeps control with existing management. Giving up 30% equity is painful but better than the uncertainty and cost of Chapter 11.',correct:!0},{label:'File Chapter 11 to get better terms, the automatic stay gives us leverage',outcome:'Risky. Chapter 11 costs $50-100M in legal/advisory fees, takes 12-18 months, and "bankruptcy" headlines drive away customers. For a retailer, lost foot traffic during bankruptcy can be fatal. The workout offer is reasonable.',correct:!1},{label:'Reject both and try to grow out of the debt',outcome:'With only 6 months of cash, there\'s no time to "grow out" of $2B in debt. This is denial, the most dangerous response to financial distress. Action must be taken now.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'In Chapter 7 bankruptcy, the company continues operating',a:!1},{s:'Common stockholders are typically wiped out in bankruptcy',a:!0},{s:'The automatic stay prevents creditors from seizing assets during Chapter 11',a:!0},{s:'A prepackaged bankruptcy takes the same amount of time as a traditional Chapter 11',a:!1},{s:'Distressed debt investors buy bonds at face value and wait for interest payments',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Restructuring Term',[['Chapter 7','Complete liquidation, company ceases to exist'],['Chapter 11','Reorganization, company restructures and survives'],['Automatic Stay','Court order halting all creditor actions'],['DIP Financing','New loans to a company during bankruptcy'],['Pre-pack','Restructuring plan negotiated before filing']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Financial Distress & Restructuring',[{takeaway:'Bankruptcy is not always death, it can be rebirth',detail:'Chapter 11 allows companies to shed unsustainable debt and emerge stronger. GM, Marvel, and American Airlines all used Chapter 11 to restructure and thrive. Chapter 7 liquidation is the true death.'},{takeaway:'The Absolute Priority Rule determines who gets paid',detail:'Secured creditors first, common equity last. Shareholders are almost always wiped out, which is why excessive leverage is so dangerous for equity investors.'},{takeaway:'Distressed debt investing profits from the spread between panic and recovery',detail:'Buying bonds at 20-40 cents when the market panics, then recovering 50-80 cents through restructuring, generates 50-200% returns for patient, analytical investors.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Financial Distress & Restructuring','International Corporate Finance','You\'ll learn how multinational corporations manage currency risk, navigate different legal and tax systems, and structure cross-border deals, where a single currency move can erase billions in value.',30)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the key difference between Chapter 7 and Chapter 11 bankruptcy?',['Chapter 7 is only for small companies while Chapter 11 is reserved for large corporations','Chapter 7 is liquidation (company dies), Chapter 11 is reorganization (company restructures debts and continues operating)','Chapter 7 is always voluntary while Chapter 11 can only be triggered by creditors filing against the firm','There is no real difference, the two chapters are just alternative names for the same process'],1,'Chapter 7 = death (assets sold, company dissolved). Chapter 11 = surgery (debts restructured, company continues). GM used Chapter 11 to shed $172B in debt and emerge viable in 40 days.'),(0,r.cq)('Under the Absolute Priority Rule, who gets paid last in bankruptcy?',['Secured creditors','Unsecured bondholders','Preferred stockholders','Common stockholders'],3,'Common stockholders are at the bottom of the priority waterfall. Secured creditors, unsecured creditors, and preferred stock must all be fully satisfied before common equity receives anything, which almost never happens.'),(0,r.cq)('A distressed investor buys $50M face value of bonds at 30 cents on the dollar. Recovery is 65 cents. What is the profit?',['$17.5M, bought for $15M, recovered $32.5M','$32.5M, taking the full 65-cent recovery as profit without subtracting the purchase cost','$50M, treating the entire face value of the bonds as the investor\'s realized profit','$7.5M, multiplying the face value by the 35-cent gap and then halving the result by mistake'],0,'Purchase: 30% \xd7 $50M = $15M. Recovery: 65% \xd7 $50M = $32.5M. Profit: $32.5M \u2212 $15M = $17.5M. Return: $17.5M/$15M = 117%.'),(0,r.cq)('Why did GM\'s Chapter 11 succeed while Toys "R" Us converted to Chapter 7?',['GM had government support, and a federal bailout is the only thing that lets a Chapter 11 succeed','GM had a viable core business and could restructure around profitable brands (Chevy, Cadillac), while Toys "R" Us couldn\'t develop a viable plan to compete with Amazon and service its debt simultaneously','GM filed for bankruptcy first, and filing earlier than a competitor is what determines the outcome','Toys "R" Us simply chose Chapter 7 voluntarily, even though a reorganization plan was available to it'],1,'Successful restructuring requires a viable core business. GM could shed unprofitable brands and emerge competitive. Toys "R" Us faced a structural challenge, its business model was being destroyed by e-commerce, making no debt level sustainable.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a prepackaged bankruptcy and why is it preferred?',['Filing for bankruptcy without a lawyer, which lowers costs by skipping the legal advisory fees','A restructuring plan negotiated with creditors BEFORE filing Chapter 11, allowing emergence in 30-60 days vs. 12-18 months, preserving business value','A Chapter 7 liquidation done quickly, selling off the company\'s assets faster than a normal filing','A government-assisted bankruptcy where federal agencies negotiate the plan on the company\'s behalf'],1,'Pre-packs combine the benefits of out-of-court negotiation with the legal certainty of court approval. By negotiating the plan beforehand, the company minimizes time in bankruptcy, critical for preserving customer and supplier relationships.')]),(0,r.L)(16,'International Corporate Finance',40,[(0,r.hookOpener)('International Corporate Finance','When the Swiss National Bank unexpectedly removed the franc\'s peg to the Euro in January 2015, the franc appreciated 30% in minutes. Companies with unhedged Swiss franc debt saw their obligations explode overnight, several Eastern European borrowers defaulted within weeks. In a world where 40% of S&P 500 revenue comes from abroad, managing international finance is not optional, it\'s survival.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand currency risk, cross-border capital structure, international tax planning, and how multinational corporations navigate the complexities of operating across borders.','World map showing currency flows between major economies with exchange rate volatility indicators',{type:'metric',title:'Swiss Franc Unpegged (2015)',value:'+30% in minutes',detail:'Unhedged franc-debt obligations exploded overnight',footer:'40% of S&P 500 revenue comes from abroad',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Unmanaged currency risk',detail:'In 2022, the strong US dollar erased $50+ billion in profits for American multinationals. Microsoft lost $595M in a single quarter from currency translation alone. Companies that didn\'t hedge international exposure saw earnings fall 5-10% even as underlying business performance improved.'},{label:'Strategic international finance',detail:'Toyota pioneered "natural hedging" by building factories in its major markets. When the yen strengthens, Toyota\'s Japanese exports become more expensive, but its US-built cars are unaffected. This operational hedge saved Toyota billions compared to competitors who relied solely on financial derivatives.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Unmanaged Currency Risk',value:'$50B+ erased',detail:'The strong 2022 dollar erased multinational profits',footer:'Microsoft lost $595M in a single quarter on FX',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Toyota: Natural Hedging',value:'Factories in markets',detail:'US-built cars unaffected when the yen strengthens',footer:'Operational hedge saved Toyota billions',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('CURRENCY RISK','The Three Types of Foreign Exchange Exposure',['Transaction exposure is the most direct: when a company has contractual cash flows in a foreign currency, exchange rate changes between the transaction date and settlement date create gains or losses. A US company invoicing \u20ac10M for delivery in 90 days faces transaction risk if the Euro weakens, \u20ac10M might be worth $11M today but only $10.5M in 90 days.','Translation exposure affects financial statements: when a multinational consolidates foreign subsidiaries into the parent\'s reporting currency, exchange rate changes alter reported revenue, assets, and equity, even if the underlying business hasn\'t changed. Microsoft\'s $595M currency loss was primarily translation exposure.','Economic exposure is the most strategic and hardest to manage: long-term changes in exchange rates that affect a company\'s competitive position. If the yen weakens 20%, Japanese automakers can cut US prices by 20% while maintaining margins, devastating for American competitors. Toyota\'s factory-in-market strategy addresses economic exposure through operational hedging.'],[{term:'Transaction Exposure',definition:'Risk from contractual foreign currency cash flows between agreement and settlement dates.',example:'A US exporter agrees to sell goods for \xa51 billion. If the yen weakens from \xa5110/$ to \xa5120/$, the $9.09M expected becomes only $8.33M, a $760K loss.'},{term:'Translation Exposure',definition:'The impact of exchange rate changes on consolidated financial statements of a multinational.',example:'Microsoft\'s European revenue in Euros didn\'t change, but the strong dollar meant those Euros translated into fewer dollars on the income statement.'},{term:'Natural Hedge',definition:'Matching revenue and costs in the same currency through operational decisions like local production.',example:'Toyota builds Camrys in Kentucky, US dollar revenue matched with US dollar costs, eliminating currency risk on those units.'}],'Three concentric rings: Transaction (inner, short-term) \u2192 Translation (middle, quarterly) \u2192 Economic (outer, long-term competitive position)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Three Types of FX Exposure',items:[{label:'Transaction',value:'Contractual cash flows',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Translation',value:'Consolidated statements',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Economic',value:'Long-term competitive position',color:'#F59E0B'}],footer:'Match the currency of debt to the currency of revenue',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A US company expects to receive \u20ac50M in 90 days. Today\'s rate is $1.10/\u20ac. In 90 days, the rate moves to $1.05/\u20ac. What is the transaction loss?',['$500,000, taking only a tenth of the true currency move when calculating the loss','$2.5 million','$5 million, doubling the actual loss by applying the rate change twice to the same euros','$50 million'],1,'Correct! Expected: \u20ac50M \xd7 $1.10 = $55M. Actual: \u20ac50M \xd7 $1.05 = $52.5M. Loss: $55M \u2212 $52.5M = $2.5M. A 4.5% currency move cost $2.5M in real cash, and the company did nothing wrong operationally.','At $1.10/\u20ac: $55M expected. At $1.05/\u20ac: $52.5M received. Transaction loss = $2.5M from currency movement alone.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('GLOBAL CAPITAL STRUCTURE','How Multinationals Optimize Across Borders','comparison','Adjust currency mix and tax rates to see how multinational corporations structure capital across different countries.',['Cross-border capital structure decisions are far more complex than domestic ones. Companies must consider: (1) Different tax rates (Ireland 12.5% vs. US 21% vs. France 25%), (2) Currency of debt matching currency of assets, (3) Repatriation costs and withholding taxes, (4) Transfer pricing regulations, and (5) Political and legal risks that vary by country.','Transfer pricing, the prices charged between subsidiaries of the same multinational, is the primary mechanism for international tax planning. By setting high prices for goods sold from low-tax subsidiaries to high-tax subsidiaries, companies shift profits to lower-tax jurisdictions. This is legal within "arm\'s length" rules but aggressively scrutinized by tax authorities worldwide.','The rise of BEPS (Base Erosion and Profit Shifting) regulations and the OECD\'s global minimum tax (15%) are reshaping international corporate finance. The old playbook of routing profits through Ireland, Luxembourg, and the Netherlands is becoming less effective as countries coordinate to prevent tax base erosion. Companies are adapting by focusing on substance-based tax planning rather than pure profit shifting.'],[{term:'Transfer Pricing',definition:'The prices charged for goods and services between subsidiaries of the same multinational, used to allocate profits across jurisdictions.',example:'Apple\'s Irish subsidiary charges other Apple entities for intellectual property rights, concentrating profits in Ireland\'s 12.5% tax jurisdiction.'},{term:'BEPS',definition:'Base Erosion and Profit Shifting, OECD framework to prevent multinationals from shifting profits to low-tax jurisdictions without real economic substance.',example:'The OECD\'s Pillar Two establishes a 15% global minimum tax, reducing the benefit of low-tax havens.'}],['40% of S&P 500 revenue comes from international operations, making currency management a core competency.','The OECD global minimum tax of 15% affects ~8,000 multinational enterprises with revenue above \u20ac750M.','Natural hedging (matching revenue and cost currencies) can eliminate 60-80% of transaction exposure without derivative costs.'],[{label:'Home Tax Rate',min:10,max:35,default:21,unit:'%'},{label:'Foreign Tax Rate',min:5,max:35,default:12,unit:'%'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('CROSS-BORDER DEALS & POLITICAL RISK','Navigating International Complexity',['Cross-border M&A involves additional complexities: different legal systems, regulatory approvals in multiple countries, cultural integration challenges, and currency risk on the deal itself. When InBev (Belgian-Brazilian) acquired Anheuser-Busch for $52 billion in 2008, it navigated US, Brazilian, and Belgian regulatory frameworks plus currency hedging on a dollar-denominated deal funded partly in Euros.','Political risk, the possibility of government actions adverse to business, ranges from regulatory changes to expropriation. Russia\'s seizure of foreign company assets after the 2022 invasion of Ukraine cost Western companies $60+ billion. Tools to manage political risk include: political risk insurance, joint ventures with local partners, diversification across countries, and structuring investments to align with host government interests.','Emerging market finance presents unique opportunities and risks: higher growth rates (6-8% vs. 2-3% in developed markets) attract capital, but currency volatility, weak legal frameworks, corruption, and political instability can destroy value. The key principle: risk-adjusted returns, not raw returns, determine whether international investment creates value.'],[{term:'Political Risk',definition:'The risk that government actions (regulation, expropriation, sanctions) will adversely affect business operations or investment value.',example:'Western companies wrote off $60B+ in Russian assets after the 2022 invasion of Ukraine, political risk materialized overnight.'},{term:'Country Risk Premium',definition:'Additional return demanded by investors for investing in a country with higher political, economic, or legal risk compared to developed markets.',example:'An investment in Brazil might require a 4-5% country risk premium above the US-based discount rate to compensate for currency volatility and political uncertainty.'}],'Risk map: Developed Markets (low political risk, low returns) \u2192 Emerging Markets (moderate risk, higher returns) \u2192 Frontier Markets (high risk, highest potential returns)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'Country Risk Spectrum',nodes:[{label:'Developed Markets',value:'Low risk, low returns',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Emerging Markets',value:'Moderate risk, higher returns',color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Frontier Markets',value:'High risk, highest potential',color:'#EF4444'}],footer:'Risk-adjusted returns determine value creation',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The strategy of matching revenue and cost currencies through local production to reduce currency risk is called ___ hedging.',['financial','natural','synthetic','speculative'],1),(0,r.q)('Microsoft reports \u20ac5B in European revenue. The dollar strengthens 10% against the Euro. What happens to Microsoft\'s reported revenue (in dollars)?',['Increases ~10%, assuming a stronger dollar lifts the dollar value of euro-denominated sales','Decreases ~10%, the same Euros translate into fewer dollars, reducing reported revenue by ~$500M','Stays the same, since the European business itself did not change its underlying performance','Increases by \u20ac500M, confusing the currency effect with real growth in the European unit'],1,'Translation exposure: \u20ac5B at the old rate might equal $5.5B. After a 10% dollar strengthening, \u20ac5B translates to ~$5B, a $500M decrease in reported revenue even though the European business performed identically.'),(0,r.q)('How would you hedge a $100M receivable in Japanese Yen due in 6 months?',['Buy a forward contract to sell \xa5 at today\'s rate in 6 months, locking in the dollar value regardless of future exchange rate movements. Cost: the forward premium (typically 1-3% annualized). This eliminates transaction exposure completely.','Build a factory in Japan immediately.','Convert all company assets to Yen.','Ignore it, currencies usually don\'t move much.'],0,'A forward contract is the textbook hedge for a known foreign-currency receivable: it locks in the exchange rate for a small premium, removing transaction exposure entirely. Building a factory or converting all assets are drastic, costly overreactions, and ignoring the exposure leaves $100M at the mercy of currency swings.'),(0,r.sc)('Your company is expanding to Brazil. Revenue will be in Brazilian Reais (BRL), but your debt is in US dollars. The Real has depreciated 40% against the dollar over the past 5 years. How do you structure the financing?',[{label:'Borrow in BRL locally to match revenue currency, even though interest rates are higher (12% vs. 5% USD)',outcome:'Smart currency matching. Higher interest rates in Brazil reflect expected Real depreciation, the "real" cost difference is smaller than it appears. Borrowing in BRL means your debt payments decline in dollar terms as the Real weakens, matching your declining translated revenue.',correct:!0},{label:'Borrow in USD at 5% and save on interest costs',outcome:'Dangerous currency mismatch. If the Real depreciates another 40%, your BRL revenue buys fewer dollars, but your USD debt stays the same. Many Latin American companies have been bankrupted by exactly this mismatch. The 7% interest rate "savings" is illusory.',correct:!1},{label:'Don\'t expand to Brazil, the currency risk is too high',outcome:'Currency risk can be managed through proper structuring. Avoiding a high-growth market (Brazil GDP ~$2T) because of currency risk means forfeiting substantial opportunities. The answer is to hedge, not avoid.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'Transaction exposure affects contractual cash flows in foreign currencies',a:!0},{s:'Translation exposure changes the actual cash flows of foreign operations',a:!1},{s:'Natural hedging involves matching revenue and cost currencies through operational decisions',a:!0},{s:'Transfer pricing is illegal in all jurisdictions',a:!1},{s:'The OECD global minimum tax is set at 15%',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the International Finance Term',[['Transaction Exposure','Risk on contractual foreign currency cash flows'],['Translation Exposure','Impact of FX on consolidated financial statements'],['Economic Exposure','Long-term competitive position affected by FX'],['Transfer Pricing','Intercompany prices to allocate profits globally'],['Country Risk Premium','Extra return demanded for emerging market risk']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('International Corporate Finance',[{takeaway:'Currency risk comes in three forms: transaction, translation, and economic',detail:'Transaction is short-term and hedgeable. Translation affects reported financials but not cash flows. Economic exposure affects long-term competitive position and requires operational strategies like natural hedging.'},{takeaway:'Match the currency of debt to the currency of revenue',detail:'Currency mismatches have bankrupted countless companies. Borrowing in dollars when revenue is in a depreciating local currency is a ticking time bomb.'},{takeaway:'International tax planning is being transformed by global coordination',detail:'The OECD\'s 15% global minimum tax and BEPS regulations are reducing the benefits of pure profit-shifting strategies. Substance-based planning is the future.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('International Corporate Finance','Corporate Bonds & Debt Markets','You\'ll learn how the $130 trillion global bond market works, credit ratings, yield spreads, covenants, and why understanding debt markets is essential for every corporate finance professional.',30)],[(0,r.cq)('What are the three types of foreign exchange exposure?',['Short-term, medium-term, and long-term, categorizing FX risk simply by the time horizon involved','Transaction (contractual cash flows), Translation (financial statements), and Economic (competitive position)','Import, export, and domestic, sorting exposure by the direction goods flow across a border','Hedged, unhedged, and partial, classifying exposure by how much protection has been put in place'],1,'Transaction = cash flow risk on specific deals. Translation = reporting risk on consolidated statements. Economic = strategic risk to competitive positioning from long-term FX shifts.'),(0,r.cq)('A US company receives \u20ac50M when the rate is $1.05/\u20ac instead of the expected $1.10/\u20ac. What is the transaction loss?',['$2.5M','$5M','$500K','$50M'],0,'Expected: \u20ac50M \xd7 $1.10 = $55M. Received: \u20ac50M \xd7 $1.05 = $52.5M. Loss = $2.5M from the 4.5% Euro depreciation.'),(0,r.cq)('Why does Toyota build factories in its major markets instead of exporting everything from Japan?',['It is always cheaper to build cars abroad, so local production simply lowers manufacturing costs','Natural hedging, matching local currency revenue with local currency costs eliminates currency risk on those units, protecting against yen appreciation','Government mandates require local production, forcing Toyota to build a plant in every market it sells in','Shipping finished cars is too expensive, so logistics costs alone drive the factory locations'],1,'When Toyota builds in the US, both revenue (dollars) and costs (dollars) are in the same currency. Yen movements don\'t affect profitability on those units, a powerful operational hedge.'),(0,r.cq)('What is transfer pricing and why do tax authorities scrutinize it?',['The price a company sets for products sold to ordinary retail consumers in different countries','The prices charged between subsidiaries of the same multinational, used to shift profits to low-tax jurisdictions, reducing overall tax bills, which tax authorities view as base erosion','The cost of relocating employees between a company\'s offices in different countries or cities','The fees banks charge for wire transfers of money sent between two different countries'],1,'Transfer pricing is legal within arm\'s length rules but is the primary mechanism for international tax optimization. Authorities scrutinize it because aggressive transfer pricing erodes their tax base.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is borrowing in a local currency generally preferred for foreign operations even when interest rates are higher?',['Local banks are simply more trustworthy than foreign lenders, so local debt is always safer','Matching debt currency to revenue currency eliminates the devastating risk of currency mismatch, where depreciating local revenue must service stable foreign debt','It is always cheaper once you adjust for inflation, so local borrowing wins on cost every time','Government regulations require it, forcing every foreign subsidiary to borrow only in the local currency'],1,'Higher interest rates in emerging markets typically reflect expected currency depreciation (interest rate parity). The "real" cost difference is small, but the protection against currency mismatch is invaluable.')]),(0,r.L)(17,'Corporate Bonds & Debt Markets',40,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Corporate Bonds & Debt Markets','The global bond market is worth $130 trillion, nearly double the $70 trillion stock market, yet most people know almost nothing about it. When Apple issued $17 billion in bonds in 2013, it was the largest corporate bond offering in history. Apple had $145 billion in cash, so why borrow? Because the after-tax cost of debt (2.4%) was cheaper than repatriating overseas cash (35% tax rate). Understanding debt markets is understanding how the world actually finances itself.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how corporate bonds work, credit ratings, yield spreads, bond covenants, and why the debt market is the true foundation of global finance.','Bond yield curve showing the relationship between credit quality and borrowing costs across maturities',{type:'comparison',title:'Global Markets Compared',rows:[{label:'Bond market',value:'$130T',percent:100},{label:'Stock market',value:'$70T',percent:54}],footer:'The bond market is how the world actually finances itself',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Ignored bond market signals',detail:'In 2007, credit spreads on mortgage-backed securities widened dramatically, bond markets were screaming "danger." Stock investors ignored the signals. Within 18 months, the financial crisis destroyed $10 trillion in wealth. The bond market is the "smart money", it sees crises before the stock market.'},{label:'Understood bond markets',detail:'In 2020, the Fed cut rates to zero and launched massive bond buying. Companies that understood debt markets issued record bonds at historically low rates, Apple locked in 40-year money at 2.6%, saving billions in future interest costs. Those who waited paid 5%+ by 2023.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Ignored Bond Signals (2007)',value:'$10T destroyed',detail:'Credit spreads widened, stock investors ignored the warning',footer:'The bond market sees crises before stocks do',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Apple: Locked-In Low Rates',value:'2.6% for 40 years',detail:'Issued record bonds when the Fed cut rates to zero',footer:'Those who waited paid 5%+ by 2023',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('HOW BONDS WORK','The Mechanics of Corporate Debt',['A corporate bond is a loan from investors to a company. The company (issuer) promises to pay periodic interest (coupons) and return the principal (face value) at maturity. A $1,000 bond with a 5% coupon pays $50/year in interest and returns $1,000 at maturity. The bond trades on secondary markets, where its price fluctuates based on interest rates, credit quality, and market conditions.','Bond prices and yields move inversely: when interest rates rise, existing bonds with lower coupons become less attractive, pushing prices down. A 1% rise in rates causes approximately a 7-8% decline in the price of a 10-year bond (duration effect). When rates fall, bond prices rise. This inverse relationship is the most fundamental concept in fixed income.','Credit ratings from Moody\'s, S&P, and Fitch assess the probability of default. Investment grade (BBB/Baa and above) companies borrow at low spreads above treasuries. High yield (below BBB, aka "junk bonds") companies pay significantly more. The spread between corporate bonds and treasuries is the credit spread, it widens during crises (investors demand more compensation for risk) and tightens during good times.'],[{term:'Coupon Rate',definition:'The annual interest rate paid on the bond\'s face value, expressed as a percentage.',example:'A $1,000 bond with a 5% coupon pays $50/year in interest ($25 semi-annually).'},{term:'Yield to Maturity (YTM)',definition:'The total annual return if the bond is held to maturity, accounting for coupon payments and the difference between purchase price and face value.',example:'A bond bought at $950 with a 5% coupon maturing in 5 years has a YTM of ~6.2% because you also gain $50 at maturity.'},{term:'Credit Spread',definition:'The additional yield a corporate bond pays above a risk-free treasury bond of the same maturity. Compensates for default risk.',example:'If 10-year treasuries yield 4% and an A-rated corporate bond yields 5.2%, the credit spread is 120 basis points (1.2%).'},{term:'Bond Covenant',definition:'Contractual restrictions in a bond agreement that limit the issuer\'s actions to protect bondholders (e.g., limits on additional debt, required financial ratios).',example:'A covenant might require the company to maintain a debt-to-EBITDA ratio below 4.0x, preventing excessive additional borrowing.'}],'Yield ladder: Treasury Bonds (lowest yield, risk-free) \u2192 Investment Grade (moderate spread) \u2192 High Yield/Junk (wide spread, highest yield, highest default risk)',void 0,{type:'bars',title:'The Yield Ladder',items:[{label:'Treasuries',value:30,displayValue:'Risk-free'},{label:'Investment Grade',value:55,displayValue:'Moderate spread'},{label:'High Yield / Junk',value:100,displayValue:'Wide spread'}],footer:'Credit spread = yield above treasuries for default risk',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('If interest rates rise 1% and you hold a 10-year bond with a duration of 8 years, what happens to the bond\'s price?',['Rises approximately 8%, assuming bond prices move in the same direction as interest rates','Falls approximately 8%','Stays the same, since the coupon payments on the bond are fixed and do not change','Falls 1%, applying the rate change directly as the price change instead of using duration'],1,'Correct! Duration measures price sensitivity to interest rate changes. A duration of 8 means a 1% rate increase causes an approximately 8% price decline. This is why rising rates are devastating for long-term bondholders.','Price change \u2248 \u2212Duration \xd7 Change in yield = \u22128 \xd7 1% = \u22128%. Longer-duration bonds are more sensitive to rate changes.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('CREDIT SPREADS & RISK','How the Market Prices Default Risk','comparison','Adjust the credit rating and economic conditions to see how corporate bond spreads change relative to treasuries.',['Credit spreads reflect the market\'s collective assessment of default probability. AAA companies (Apple, Microsoft, Johnson & Johnson) borrow at 50-80 basis points above treasuries. BB-rated "junk" companies pay 300-500 basis points more. CCC-rated companies pay 800-1500+ basis points, sometimes reflecting expected default.','Spread movements tell a story about the economy. When spreads widen (increase), the market is pricing in higher default risk, a leading indicator of recession. When spreads tighten, confidence is rising. The "credit cycle", tightening spreads (easy lending) \u2192 excessive risk-taking \u2192 widening spreads (crisis) \u2192 defaults \u2192 recovery, repeats every 7-10 years.','Bond covenants are the contractual guardrails protecting bondholders. Maintenance covenants require ongoing compliance with financial ratios (debt/EBITDA < 4x). Incurrence covenants only apply when the company takes new action (like issuing more debt). "Covenant-lite" loans, popular in 2019-2021, stripped most protections, leading to higher losses during the 2022 downturn as companies had more freedom to take risky actions.'],[{term:'Duration',definition:'A measure of a bond\'s price sensitivity to interest rate changes. Higher duration = more price volatility from rate movements.',example:'A 30-year treasury bond has duration ~20, a 1% rate increase causes a ~20% price decline. A 2-year note has duration ~2.'},{term:'Covenant-Lite',definition:'Loan or bond structures with minimal protective covenants, giving the borrower more flexibility but less protection for lenders.',example:'In 2021, 90%+ of leveraged loans were covenant-lite. When conditions deteriorated in 2022, lenders had few tools to intervene early.'}],['AAA credit spreads: 50-80 basis points. BB (junk): 300-500bp. CCC: 800-1500+bp.','The global corporate bond market is ~$40 trillion, larger than any single stock market.','During the 2008 crisis, investment grade spreads widened from 100bp to 600bp+, a 6x increase reflecting systemic panic.'],[{label:'Credit Rating (1=AAA, 10=CCC)',min:1,max:10,default:4,unit:''},{label:'Economic Stress Index',min:1,max:10,default:3,unit:''}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('BOND ISSUANCE & STRATEGY','How Companies Use Debt Markets Strategically',['Companies choose between bank loans (private, flexible, floating rate) and bonds (public, fixed rate, larger amounts). Investment grade companies prefer bonds for large, long-term financing at fixed rates. Smaller and riskier companies rely more on bank loans and leveraged loans because they cannot access public bond markets efficiently.','Refinancing strategy is critical: companies monitor interest rates and refinance (issue new bonds to repay old ones) when rates decline. Apple has refinanced billions in bonds when rates dropped, locking in lower costs for decades. Conversely, companies that fail to refinance before rate rises face "maturity walls", large amounts of debt coming due that must be refinanced at higher rates.','The high-yield (junk) bond market, pioneered by Michael Milken in the 1980s, democratized corporate borrowing. Before Milken, only investment-grade companies could issue bonds. High-yield bonds funded the LBO boom, enabled telecom infrastructure buildout, and financed countless growth companies. The market now exceeds $1.5 trillion, controversial but essential for corporate finance.'],[{term:'Investment Grade',definition:'Bonds rated BBB/Baa or above by rating agencies. Lower default risk, lower yields. Eligible for most institutional portfolios.',example:'Apple\'s AA+ rated bonds yield about 0.5-1% above treasuries, extremely cheap debt for the company.'},{term:'High-Yield (Junk) Bonds',definition:'Bonds rated below BBB/Baa. Higher default risk, higher yields. Also called "speculative grade" or "junk bonds."',example:'A BB-rated company might issue bonds at 7-8% when treasuries yield 4%, the 3-4% spread compensates for 2-3% annual default probability.'},{term:'Maturity Wall',definition:'A concentration of debt maturities in a short period, creating refinancing risk if market conditions are unfavorable.',example:'A company with $5B in bonds maturing in 2025-2026 faces a "maturity wall", if rates are high and credit is tight, refinancing becomes expensive or impossible.'}],'Spectrum: Bank Loans (flexible, floating rate, smaller) \u2192 Investment Grade Bonds (fixed rate, large, cheap) \u2192 High-Yield Bonds (expensive, growth-oriented) \u2192 Leveraged Loans (PE-backed, covenant-lite)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The Debt Financing Spectrum',nodes:[{label:'Bank Loans',value:'Flexible, floating'},{label:'Investment Grade Bonds',value:'Fixed, large, cheap'},{label:'High-Yield Bonds',value:'Expensive, growth'},{label:'Leveraged Loans',value:'PE-backed, cov-lite'}],footer:'Companies refinance when rates decline',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The additional yield a corporate bond pays above a risk-free treasury bond is called the credit ___.',['premium','spread','margin','buffer'],1),(0,r.q)('Apple had $145B in cash but issued $17B in bonds at 2.4% after-tax cost. Why borrow when you have cash?',['Apple genuinely could not access its cash, since the $145B was frozen and unavailable to spend','The after-tax cost of debt (2.4%) was far cheaper than repatriating overseas cash at a 35% tax rate, borrowing domestically and using foreign cash for international operations was value-maximizing','Apple\'s CFO simply made a mistake, since a company with that much cash never needs to borrow','Bond markets required it, forcing any large company to issue debt before it can use its own cash'],1,'Apple\'s cash was largely overseas. Bringing it home would cost 35% in taxes. Borrowing at 2.4% after-tax was dramatically cheaper. This "synthetic repatriation" is a textbook example of optimal capital structure decision-making.'),(0,r.pc)('Bond Yield Calculation',[{label:'Face value',formula:'Par amount',result:'$1,000'},{label:'Coupon rate',formula:'Annual interest / Face',result:'5%'},{label:'Annual coupon',formula:'$1,000 \xd7 5%',result:'$50'},{label:'Purchase price',formula:'Market price',result:'$950'},{label:'Current yield',formula:'$50 / $950',result:'5.26%'},{label:'Approx. YTM (5yr)',formula:'($50 + ($1000-$950)/5) / (($1000+$950)/2)',result:'~5.8%'}]),(0,r.sc)('Your company has $2B in bonds maturing in 18 months. Current rates are 5.5% (your existing bonds pay 3.5%). The CFO wants to wait, hoping rates will fall. The credit markets are tightening. What do you advise?',[{label:'Wait for lower rates, the Fed might cut soon',outcome:'Dangerous gamble. If credit spreads widen or your company faces any operational issues before refinancing, you could face a liquidity crisis. Maturity walls have bankrupted companies that waited too long. The 2% rate increase is painful but manageable.',correct:!1},{label:'Refinance now at 5.5%, lock in certainty and eliminate the maturity wall risk, even though it costs 2% more than the old bonds',outcome:'Prudent risk management. The $40M/year in additional interest ($2B \xd7 2%) is the cost of certainty. If credit markets seize up in the next 18 months, you could face far worse: distressed rates of 8-10% or inability to refinance at all.',correct:!0},{label:'Refinance half now and half later, split the difference',outcome:'A reasonable compromise, but you still face $1B in maturity wall risk. Given tightening credit conditions, eliminating the risk entirely is more prudent for a $2B exposure.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'Bond prices and interest rates move in the same direction',a:!1},{s:'AAA-rated bonds have the lowest credit spreads',a:!0},{s:'High-yield bonds are rated BBB and above',a:!1},{s:'Duration measures a bond\'s price sensitivity to interest rate changes',a:!0},{s:'The global bond market is larger than the global stock market',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Bond Term',[['Coupon','Periodic interest payment to bondholders'],['Duration','Price sensitivity to interest rate changes'],['Credit Spread','Extra yield above treasuries for default risk'],['Covenant','Contractual restriction protecting bondholders'],['Maturity Wall','Concentration of debt coming due in a short period']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Corporate Bonds & Debt Markets',[{takeaway:'The bond market is the foundation of global finance at $130 trillion',detail:'It\'s nearly double the stock market in size and often leads equity markets in signaling economic stress. Widening credit spreads predicted the 2008 crisis months before stocks crashed.'},{takeaway:'Bond prices and yields move inversely',detail:'When rates rise, existing bond prices fall. Duration measures sensitivity: a 10-year bond with duration 8 loses ~8% for each 1% rate increase. This relationship is the bedrock of fixed income.'},{takeaway:'Credit spreads price default risk and economic health',detail:'AAA spreads: 50-80bp. Junk spreads: 300-1500bp. Spread movements are a real-time gauge of economic confidence, widening means trouble, tightening means optimism.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Corporate Bonds & Debt Markets','Treasury Management & Cash Flow','You\'ll learn how corporate treasurers manage the daily reality of cash, forecasting, investing short-term surplus, managing bank relationships, and ensuring the company never runs out of money.',30)],[(0,r.cq)('Why do bond prices fall when interest rates rise?',['The companies that issued the bonds become less profitable whenever interest rates go up','Existing bonds with lower coupons become less attractive compared to new bonds issued at higher rates, so their prices drop to equalize yields','Higher rates cause inflation, and inflation directly destroys the face value printed on the bond','Rating agencies automatically downgrade every bond whenever market interest rates increase'],1,'If new bonds pay 6% and your bond pays 4%, no one will pay face value for your bond. The price must drop until the yield on your bond matches the new 6% market rate. This inverse relationship is the most fundamental concept in fixed income.'),(0,r.cq)('What does a credit spread of 200 basis points mean?',['The bond yields 200% per year, treating each basis point as a full percentage point of yield','The bond yields 2% more than a comparable-maturity treasury bond, compensating investors for the company\'s default risk','The bond\'s price is 200% of its face value, so it trades at double what it will repay at maturity','The company has 200 days left until it defaults, with the spread acting as a countdown to failure'],1,'200 basis points = 2.00%. If treasuries yield 4%, this bond yields 6%. The 2% spread compensates for the risk that the company might default. Higher risk = wider spread.'),(0,r.cq)('Apple had $145B cash but issued $17B in bonds. Why?',['Cash was offshore and repatriating at 35% tax was far more expensive than borrowing at 2.4% after-tax, borrowing was value-maximizing','Apple genuinely needed more cash, since its $145B reserve was not enough to fund operations','The bond market was about to close for good, so Apple rushed to borrow while it still could','The CEO simply wanted more leverage, adding debt for its own sake with no financial rationale'],0,'Borrowing at 2.4% after-tax was dramatically cheaper than paying 35% tax to bring offshore cash home. This "synthetic repatriation" saved Apple billions in taxes.'),(0,r.cq)('A bond has duration of 12 years. Rates rise 0.5%. What is the approximate price change?',['\u221212%','\u22126%','\u22120.5%','+6%'],1,'Price change \u2248 \u2212Duration \xd7 Rate change = \u221212 \xd7 0.5% = \u22126%. Long-duration bonds are highly sensitive to rate movements.'),(0,r.cq)('What are bond covenants and why do they matter?',['Government regulations that control how corporate bonds can be bought and sold on exchanges','Contractual restrictions in bond agreements that protect bondholders by limiting the issuer\'s actions (e.g., restricting additional debt, requiring minimum financial ratios)','Promises by the issuer to pay higher coupon rates if the company performs well over time','Agreements between the rating agencies on how to assign credit ratings to a company\'s debt'],1,'Covenants are the bondholders\' defense against risky management behavior. Without them (covenant-lite), companies can take on excessive debt, pay massive dividends, or make risky acquisitions that endanger bondholders\' recovery.')]),(0,r.L)(18,'Treasury Management & Cash Flow',40,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Treasury Management & Cash Flow','In March 2023, Silicon Valley Bank collapsed in 48 hours, the second-largest bank failure in US history, partly because it failed at the most basic treasury function: matching the duration of assets and liabilities. SVB invested short-term deposits into long-term bonds, and when rates rose, the bonds lost $17 billion in value. Treasury management isn\'t glamorous, but when it fails, companies die overnight.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand cash flow forecasting, short-term investment strategies, bank relationship management, and why the corporate treasurer is the unsung guardian of a company\'s survival.','Dashboard showing cash inflows, outflows, and the critical minimum cash balance line',{type:'metric',title:'SVB Collapse (2023)',value:'48 hours',detail:'Short-term deposits invested in long-term bonds, $17B lost',footer:'A failure of the most basic treasury function',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Treasury failure',detail:'Silicon Valley Bank invested deposits in long-duration bonds. When rates rose, those bonds lost $17B in value. A bank run followed, $42B withdrawn in one day. SVB collapsed in 48 hours. MF Global (2011) also failed from treasury mismanagement, losing $1.6B in customer funds through unauthorized bets on European sovereign debt.'},{label:'Treasury excellence',detail:'Microsoft\'s treasury team manages $111B in cash and investments with military precision: 80% in ultra-safe short-term instruments, 20% in diversified fixed income. This conservative approach has provided Microsoft with unlimited strategic flexibility, funding the $69B Activision acquisition entirely from cash and debt without straining liquidity.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Treasury Failure: SVB',value:'$42B withdrawn',detail:'Duration mismatch caused $17B in bond losses',footer:'A bank run collapsed SVB in 48 hours',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'stack',title:'Microsoft: Treasury Excellence',total:'$111B Managed',segments:[{label:'Ultra-safe short-term',value:'80%',percent:80,color:'#10B981'},{label:'Diversified fixed income',value:'20%',percent:20,color:'#3B82F6'}],footer:'Funded the $69B Activision deal without straining liquidity',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE TREASURER\'S MANDATE','Managing the Lifeblood of the Company',['The corporate treasurer ensures the company always has enough cash to meet obligations while earning returns on excess cash. Key responsibilities: (1) Cash flow forecasting, predicting daily, weekly, and monthly cash needs. (2) Liquidity management, maintaining credit lines and cash reserves. (3) Short-term investing, earning returns on surplus cash. (4) Bank relationship management, negotiating fees, credit facilities, and payment systems.','Cash flow forecasting is the foundation of treasury management. The 13-week cash flow forecast is the standard tool, projecting every expected inflow (customer payments, asset sales) and outflow (payroll, supplier payments, debt service, taxes) for the next quarter. Accuracy is critical: overestimating cash can lead to missed payments; underestimating leads to uninvested (earning nothing) cash.','The optimal cash balance trades off three costs: (1) Holding cost, cash earns less than invested capital, so too much cash is a drag on returns. (2) Shortage cost, running out of cash means defaulting on obligations, which can be fatal. (3) Transaction cost, converting investments to cash takes time and may incur losses. Companies like Apple and Google hold "excess" cash for strategic flexibility, but activist investors increasingly push for cash returns to shareholders.'],[{term:'Cash Flow Forecasting',definition:'Predicting future cash inflows and outflows to ensure the company can meet all obligations and invest surplus optimally.',example:'A 13-week forecast might show a $50M shortfall in week 8 when a bond payment is due, allowing the treasurer to arrange a credit line draw in advance.'},{term:'Revolving Credit Facility',definition:'A committed credit line from banks that allows the company to borrow and repay repeatedly, providing a liquidity backstop.',example:'Amazon maintains a $10B revolving credit facility, it rarely draws on it, but having it ensures liquidity in any crisis.'},{term:'Money Market Funds',definition:'Short-term investment vehicles that invest in treasuries, commercial paper, and repos, providing near-cash returns with high liquidity.',example:'Corporate treasurers park excess cash in money market funds yielding 4-5% while maintaining same-day liquidity.'}],'Treasury dashboard: Cash inflows \u2192 Cash pool \u2192 Three buckets: Operating cash (daily needs), Reserve cash (buffer), Investment cash (earn returns)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'The Treasurer\'s Mandate',items:[{label:'Cash Forecasting',value:'13-week forecast',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Liquidity Management',value:'Credit lines & reserves',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Short-Term Investing',value:'Earn on surplus',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Bank Relationships',value:'Fees & facilities',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'The treasurer is the guardian of corporate survival',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company has $500M in cash, $200M in weekly operating expenses, and a policy of maintaining 4 weeks of cash reserves. How much "free cash" is available for short-term investment?',['$500M, treating the entire cash balance as free to invest with no reserve set aside','$300M, reserving only one week of expenses instead of the required four weeks','$0, the company needs $800M but only has $500M, indicating a liquidity shortfall','$100M, subtracting an arbitrary reserve without checking it against the 4-week policy'],2,'Correct! 4 weeks \xd7 $200M/week = $800M needed in reserves. The company only has $500M, it has a $300M liquidity gap. The treasurer needs to draw on credit lines or reduce the reserve requirement. This is exactly why cash forecasting matters.','$200M/week \xd7 4 weeks = $800M needed. $500M available. Gap = $300M. The company cannot invest anything, it doesn\'t have enough for its own reserve policy.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('CASH MANAGEMENT OPTIMIZATION','Balancing Safety, Liquidity, and Return','comparison','Adjust the allocation between cash, short-term investments, and longer-term investments to see how the return profile changes against the liquidity risk.',['The investment policy statement (IPS) governs how corporate treasurers invest surplus cash. Typical priorities in order: (1) Safety of principal, never lose money. (2) Liquidity, ability to access cash quickly. (3) Return, earn as much as possible given constraints 1 and 2. This "SLR" hierarchy is the opposite of how most individual investors think.','Common treasury instruments: US Treasury bills (safest, most liquid), commercial paper (short-term corporate IOUs, slightly higher yield), repurchase agreements (repos, overnight loans secured by treasuries), money market funds (diversified pools of short-term instruments), and certificates of deposit. Each offers a different point on the safety-liquidity-return spectrum.','Bank relationship management is a strategic function. Companies maintain relationships with multiple banks for: payment processing, credit facilities, foreign exchange, cash management services, and investment banking. The lead bank often receives the most lucrative business in exchange for providing the largest credit facility. Diversifying banking relationships reduces concentration risk, as SVB depositors painfully learned.'],[{term:'Investment Policy Statement',definition:'The formal document governing how a treasury department can invest surplus cash, specifying allowed instruments, credit quality, maturity limits, and concentration limits.',example:'An IPS might specify: no more than 5% in any single issuer, minimum A-1/P-1 credit rating, maximum 90-day maturity.'},{term:'Commercial Paper',definition:'Short-term unsecured debt issued by corporations, typically maturing in 1-270 days. A key money market instrument.',example:'Apple issues commercial paper at rates slightly above T-bills, providing corporations a slightly higher yield for investing surplus cash.'}],['SVB\'s failure: $17B in unrealized losses on long-duration bonds held against short-term deposits.','S&P 500 companies held $3.7 trillion in cash and short-term investments in 2024.','The average corporate money market fund returned 4.5-5% in 2024, up from near-zero in 2020-2021.'],[{label:'Cash Reserve (%)',min:10,max:60,default:30,unit:'%'},{label:'Short-Term Investment Yield',min:1,max:6,default:4.5,unit:'%'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('CASH FLOW ANALYSIS','Reading the Cash Flow Statement Like a Pro',['The cash flow statement has three sections: Operating Cash Flow (OCF), cash generated from core business operations. Investing Cash Flow (ICF), cash spent on or received from long-term assets (CapEx, acquisitions, asset sales). Financing Cash Flow (FCF), cash from or to capital providers (debt issuance/repayment, equity issuance, dividends, buybacks).','Free Cash Flow (FCF) is the most important metric in corporate finance: FCF = Operating Cash Flow \u2212 Capital Expenditures. It represents cash available for all capital providers, debt repayment, dividends, buybacks, acquisitions, or reserves. A company can report positive net income while having negative FCF (by booking revenue before collecting cash), which is why Enron fooled investors for years.','Cash flow quality analysis asks: Is the cash flow sustainable and recurring, or one-time? Companies can temporarily boost OCF by delaying supplier payments (increasing DPO), accelerating customer collections (decreasing DSO), or selling receivables (factoring). Analysts look for consistent FCF generation relative to net income, a FCF/Net Income ratio consistently below 0.8 is a red flag.'],[{term:'Free Cash Flow (FCF)',definition:'Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures. Cash available to all capital providers after maintaining and growing the business.',example:'Apple: $110B operating cash flow \u2212 $11B CapEx = $99B in FCF, the engine behind $600B+ in shareholder returns.'},{term:'Cash Conversion Ratio',definition:'FCF \xf7 Net Income. Measures how efficiently a company converts accounting profits into real cash. Above 1.0 is ideal.',example:'A cash conversion ratio of 1.2 means the company generates 20% more cash than reported profits, strong quality of earnings.'}],'Three columns: Operating CF (core business cash) \u2192 Investing CF (growth spending) \u2192 Financing CF (capital provider flows) \u2192 Net change in cash position',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The Cash Flow Statement',nodes:[{label:'Operating CF',value:'Core business cash'},{label:'Investing CF',value:'Growth spending'},{label:'Financing CF',value:'Capital provider flows'}],footer:'Free Cash Flow = Operating CF \u2212 CapEx',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The most important cash flow metric, calculated as Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures, is called ___ Cash Flow.',['Gross','Net','Free','Total'],2),(0,r.q)('Company A: Net Income $500M, FCF $600M. Company B: Net Income $500M, FCF $200M. Which has better earnings quality?',['Both are equal, since they report the same net income and net income is what defines quality','Company A, its FCF exceeds net income (1.2x cash conversion), indicating high-quality sustainable earnings','Company B, a lower FCF means the company is plowing more cash back into growth investments','Cannot determine, because earnings quality cannot be judged from net income and FCF alone'],1,'Company A converts 120% of net income to cash, excellent quality. Company B converts only 40%, a red flag suggesting aggressive revenue recognition, inventory buildup, or delayed CapEx maintenance. Cash doesn\'t lie; accounting earnings can.'),(0,r.tr)('Treasury Management Essentials',[['What is the 13-week cash flow forecast?','A rolling quarterly projection of all expected cash inflows and outflows, used to ensure the company can meet obligations and identify investment opportunities.'],['What is the SLR hierarchy?','Safety \u2192 Liquidity \u2192 Return. Corporate treasurers prioritize not losing money, then accessibility, then yield, the opposite of most investment approaches.'],['What caused SVB\'s collapse?','Duration mismatch: short-term deposits invested in long-term bonds. When rates rose, bonds lost $17B in value, triggering a bank run.'],['Why is FCF more reliable than net income?','FCF represents actual cash generated; net income can be manipulated through accounting choices. Enron had positive net income but negative FCF for years before collapse.']]),(0,r.sc)('You\'re the treasurer of a tech company with $2B in cash. The CEO wants to invest 60% in higher-yielding corporate bonds (2-3 year maturity) and 40% in money market funds. Your operating expenses are $300M/month. What\'s your recommendation?',[{label:'Agree with the CEO, the higher yield on corporate bonds will generate $20-30M more per year',outcome:'Dangerous. $1.2B in 2-3 year bonds is illiquid relative to $300M/month in operating expenses. If a downturn hits and revenue drops, you need that cash accessible. SVB failed precisely because of this duration mismatch.',correct:!1},{label:'Recommend 75% money market / 25% short-term bonds, maintain at least 6 months of operating expenses ($1.8B) in highly liquid instruments',outcome:'Excellent treasury management. 6 months of operating cash ($1.8B) in liquid instruments provides a robust safety buffer. The remaining $200M can be invested in slightly longer-duration instruments for extra yield. Safety and liquidity first, return third.',correct:!0},{label:'Keep 100% in cash, earn nothing but be completely safe',outcome:'Overly conservative. With money market funds yielding 4-5%, keeping $2B entirely in non-interest-bearing cash costs $80-100M/year in forgone income. Some yield optimization is appropriate within the safety-first framework.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'Free Cash Flow equals Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures',a:!0},{s:'Corporate treasurers prioritize return above safety and liquidity',a:!1},{s:'SVB collapsed because of a duration mismatch between assets and liabilities',a:!0},{s:'A cash conversion ratio above 1.0 indicates high earnings quality',a:!0},{s:'Companies should invest all surplus cash in long-term bonds for maximum yield',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Treasury Term',[['13-Week Forecast','Rolling quarterly projection of cash inflows and outflows'],['Revolving Credit Facility','Committed credit line for liquidity backup'],['Commercial Paper','Short-term unsecured corporate debt instrument'],['Free Cash Flow','OCF minus CapEx, cash available to capital providers'],['Duration Mismatch','Assets and liabilities with different maturity profiles']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Treasury Management & Cash Flow',[{takeaway:'The treasurer\'s job is to ensure the company never runs out of cash',detail:'13-week cash flow forecasting, revolving credit facilities, and conservative investment policies form the foundation. SVB\'s failure shows what happens when treasury fundamentals are ignored.'},{takeaway:'Safety \u2192 Liquidity \u2192 Return is the sacred order',detail:'Corporate treasurers must never lose principal, must maintain access to cash, and only then optimize for yield. This SLR hierarchy has been validated by every corporate treasury disaster in history.'},{takeaway:'Free Cash Flow is the truth that accounting earnings can hide',detail:'FCF = OCF \u2212 CapEx. A cash conversion ratio consistently below 0.8x is a red flag. Enron, WorldCom, and countless frauds were detectable through cash flow analysis long before the accounting fraud was revealed.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Treasury Management & Cash Flow','ESG & Sustainable Finance','You\'ll learn how environmental, social, and governance factors are transforming corporate finance, from green bonds to carbon pricing, and why ESG is no longer optional for companies accessing capital markets.',35)],[(0,r.cq)('What caused Silicon Valley Bank\'s collapse?',['Bad loans to tech startups that defaulted in large numbers and wiped out the bank\'s capital','A duration mismatch, short-term deposits invested in long-term bonds that lost $17B when rates rose, triggering a $42B bank run in one day','Heavy losses on cryptocurrency holdings that the bank had bought during the 2021 market boom','Sudden government regulation that forced the bank to shut down its core lending operations'],1,'SVB invested deposits in long-duration bonds. When rates rose, those bonds lost massive value. Depositors panicked, withdrew $42B in one day, and SVB collapsed in 48 hours, a textbook treasury management failure.'),(0,r.cq)('What is Free Cash Flow and why does it matter?',['Revenue minus expenses, which is actually the definition of net income rather than free cash flow','Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures, it represents actual cash available to capital providers and cannot be manipulated as easily as accounting earnings','Profit after taxes, the bottom-line earnings figure that appears on the income statement','The total cash sitting in the company\'s bank accounts at the end of the reporting period'],1,'FCF = OCF \u2212 CapEx. It measures real cash generation. Companies like Enron reported growing profits while FCF was negative, cash flow analysis would have detected the fraud years earlier.'),(0,r.cq)('A company has $1B cash, $150M in weekly operating expenses, and a 4-week reserve policy. How much can be invested in longer-term instruments?',['$1B, treating the entire cash balance as investable and ignoring the reserve policy','$400M, after reserving $600M (4 weeks \xd7 $150M) for operations','$0, assuming a company with a reserve policy can never invest any of its cash','$850M, reserving only one week of expenses instead of the required four weeks'],1,'The reserve required is 4 weeks \xd7 $150M/week = $600M. The company holds $1B, so investable cash = $1B \u2212 $600M = $400M. The remaining $600M must stay in highly liquid instruments to cover the four-week buffer.'),(0,r.cq)('What is the SLR hierarchy in treasury management?',['Sales, Leverage, and Returns, the three figures a treasurer reports to the board each quarter','Safety of principal first, Liquidity second, Return third, the priority order for investing corporate cash','Stock, Loan, and Reserve, the three categories a company can park its surplus cash into','Small, Large, and Regular, a way of sorting investments by the typical size of each position'],1,'SLR = Safety \u2192 Liquidity \u2192 Return. Never lose money, always have access to cash, then optimize yield. This is the opposite of how most retail investors think, but it\'s how corporate treasurers must operate.'),(0,r.cq)('Company X has net income of $200M but FCF of only $80M. What does this suggest?',['The company is very profitable, since a $200M net income is a strong number on its own','Potential earnings quality concerns, the 0.4x cash conversion ratio suggests accounting profits aren\'t translating into real cash, possibly due to aggressive revenue recognition, inventory buildup, or deferred CapEx','The company is investing heavily in growth, and the low FCF is simply normal capital spending','Nothing unusual at all, since net income and free cash flow are rarely the same number anyway'],1,'A cash conversion ratio of 0.4 is a major red flag. Real cash generation is far below reported profits. This could indicate: aggressive revenue recognition, rising inventory (cash trapped), or deferred maintenance. Investigate further.')]),(0,r.L)(19,'ESG & Sustainable Finance',40,[(0,r.hookOpener)('ESG & Sustainable Finance','In 2023, global green bond issuance exceeded $575 billion, from near-zero a decade earlier. BlackRock, the world\'s largest asset manager with $10 trillion under management, now requires all portfolio companies to disclose climate risks. When Larry Fink, BlackRock\'s CEO, says "climate risk is investment risk," it\'s not activism, it\'s fiduciary duty. ESG has moved from the fringe to the center of corporate finance.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how ESG factors affect corporate valuation, the mechanics of green bonds and sustainability-linked loans, and why ignoring ESG is now a financial risk, not just an ethical choice.','Chart showing the exponential growth of green bond issuance from $11B (2014) to $575B+ (2023)',{type:'line',title:'Green Bond Issuance',points:[{label:'2014',value:'$11B'},{label:'2019',value:'$258B'},{label:'2023',value:'$575B+'}],trend:'up',footer:'From near-zero to $575B+ in under a decade',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'ESG risks ignored',detail:'BP\'s Deepwater Horizon spill (2010) cost $65 billion in cleanup, fines, and settlements. Volkswagen\'s emissions fraud (2015) cost $33 billion. PG&E\'s wildfire liability (2019) forced bankruptcy at $30 billion. In each case, ESG failures, environmental negligence, governance fraud, destroyed more value than most companies ever create.'},{label:'ESG integrated strategically',detail:'Orsted (formerly Danish Oil and Natural Gas) transformed from a fossil fuel company into the world\'s largest offshore wind developer. Its stock price increased 500% from 2016-2021. Tesla\'s EV focus aligned financial incentives with environmental impact, creating a $800B+ company. ESG integration can be the strategy, not a constraint.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'bars',title:'ESG Failures Destroyed Value',items:[{label:'BP Spill',value:65,displayValue:'$65B'},{label:'VW Emissions',value:33,displayValue:'$33B'},{label:'PG&E Wildfire',value:30,displayValue:'$30B'}],footer:'ESG failures destroyed more value than most firms create',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Orsted: ESG as Strategy',points:[{label:'2016 share price',value:'$36'},{label:'2021 share price',value:'$216'}],trend:'up',footer:'Transformed into the world\'s largest offshore wind developer',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE THREE PILLARS OF ESG','Environmental, Social, and Governance in Finance',['Environmental factors: carbon emissions, energy efficiency, waste management, water usage, biodiversity impact, and climate transition risk. Companies face two types of climate risk: physical risk (damage from extreme weather, sea level rise) and transition risk (policy changes, carbon pricing, technology disruption). A coal company faces massive transition risk; a coastal property company faces physical risk.','Social factors: labor practices, supply chain conditions, diversity and inclusion, community impact, data privacy, and product safety. Social risks can be equally devastating: Nike\'s 1990s sweatshop scandal cut its stock 50%. Facebook\'s Cambridge Analytica scandal erased $120 billion in market cap. Social license to operate is earned, not given.','Governance factors: board independence, executive compensation, shareholder rights, audit quality, anti-corruption, and transparency. Governance is the most directly measurable ESG pillar and the most clearly linked to financial performance. Companies with strong governance (independent boards, aligned compensation, transparent reporting) consistently outperform those without.'],[{term:'ESG',definition:'Environmental, Social, and Governance, the three categories of non-financial factors that affect a company\'s risk profile and long-term value.',example:'A company with high carbon emissions (E risk), poor labor practices (S risk), and a captured board (G risk) faces compounding ESG risk.'},{term:'Green Bond',definition:'A bond where proceeds are specifically allocated to environmental projects (renewable energy, clean transportation, sustainable buildings).',example:'Apple issued a $4.7B green bond to fund renewable energy projects, waste reduction, and recycled materials in its supply chain.'},{term:'Carbon Pricing',definition:'A mechanism that puts a cost on carbon emissions, through carbon taxes or cap-and-trade systems, to incentivize emission reduction.',example:'The EU Emissions Trading System prices carbon at \u20ac80-100/ton, making fossil fuels more expensive and renewables more competitive.'},{term:'Stranded Assets',definition:'Assets that lose value prematurely due to the energy transition, fossil fuel reserves that cannot be burned under climate targets.',example:'If carbon budgets are enforced, an estimated $10-20 trillion in fossil fuel reserves may become stranded, worthless.'}],'Three interconnected circles: Environmental (planet), Social (people), Governance (process), with financial performance at the intersection',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'The Three Pillars of ESG',items:[{label:'Environmental',value:'Carbon, climate risk',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Social',value:'Labor, supply chain',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Governance',value:'Board, transparency',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'ESG risks compound, VW\'s scandal hit all three',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('BP\'s Deepwater Horizon spill cost $65 billion. If BP\'s market cap was $180 billion before the spill, what percentage of company value was destroyed?',['10%','36%','65%','50%'],1,'Correct! $65B \xf7 $180B = 36% of company value destroyed by a single environmental failure. And this understates the total impact, BP\'s stock underperformed for a decade afterward. Environmental risk is financial risk.','$65B cost \xf7 $180B pre-spill market cap = 36% value destruction. BP\'s stock dropped even more than this initially, reflecting uncertainty about total costs.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('GREEN FINANCE GROWTH','How ESG is Reshaping Capital Markets','comparison','Adjust the ESG score and capital cost to see how sustainability performance affects a company\'s cost of capital and access to funding.',['The "greenium", the lower yield (cheaper borrowing cost) on green bonds compared to conventional bonds from the same issuer, has averaged 2-10 basis points. It\'s small but growing as demand from ESG-mandated investors exceeds supply. More importantly, companies with poor ESG scores face a "brown penalty", higher costs and reduced access to capital as investors exclude them.','Sustainability-linked loans (SLLs) tie interest rates to ESG performance targets. If a company meets its emissions reduction or diversity targets, the interest rate drops 10-25 basis points. If it misses, rates increase. This creates a direct financial incentive for ESG improvement. SLL issuance exceeded $350 billion in 2023.','The regulatory landscape is accelerating: the EU\'s Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) mandates ESG disclosure for 50,000+ companies. The SEC\'s climate disclosure rules require public companies to report climate risks and emissions. The ISSB (International Sustainability Standards Board) is creating global ESG reporting standards. Compliance costs are rising but standardization is reducing greenwashing.'],[{term:'Greenium',definition:'The lower yield (cheaper borrowing cost) on green bonds compared to conventional bonds from the same issuer, reflecting excess ESG investor demand.',example:'A company issues a conventional bond at 5.00% and a green bond at 4.92%, the 8bp greenium reflects ESG investor demand.'},{term:'Sustainability-Linked Loan',definition:'A loan with interest rates tied to achieving ESG performance targets, rates decrease if targets are met, increase if missed.',example:'An airline\'s loan charges 4.5% if it reduces fleet emissions 10% by 2026; 4.75% if it misses the target.'}],['Green bond issuance: $11B (2014) \u2192 $575B+ (2023), 50x growth in under a decade.','Companies in the top ESG quartile have 20% lower cost of equity on average vs. the bottom quartile.','The EU carbon price reached \u20ac100/ton in 2023, a 10x increase from \u20ac10/ton in 2018.'],[{label:'ESG Score (1-100)',min:10,max:100,default:65,unit:''},{label:'Carbon Price ($/ton)',min:10,max:200,default:80,unit:'$'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('ESG INTEGRATION IN VALUATION','How Sustainability Affects the Numbers',['ESG factors enter DCF analysis through multiple channels: (1) Revenue, companies with strong ESG attract sustainability-conscious customers and government contracts. (2) Costs, carbon taxes, remediation liabilities, and regulatory fines increase costs for poor ESG performers. (3) WACC, ESG leaders access cheaper capital (greenium); ESG laggards face higher costs (brown penalty). (4) Terminal value, companies with unsustainable business models face lower terminal values as transition risks materialize.','Materiality varies by industry: carbon emissions are material for energy companies but less so for software companies. Water usage is critical for agriculture and semiconductors but not for consulting firms. The SASB (Sustainability Accounting Standards Board) identifies material ESG factors for 77 industries, helping analysts focus on what actually matters financially.','The debate: ESG skeptics argue that ESG investing sacrifices returns for feel-good signaling. ESG proponents counter that ESG analysis identifies risks (like Deepwater Horizon, VW emissions fraud) that traditional financial analysis misses. The emerging consensus: ESG factors are material to financial performance, but only when integrated rigorously, not as a marketing label.'],[{term:'Materiality',definition:'The concept that specific ESG factors vary in financial relevance by industry, only material factors should affect valuation.',example:'Carbon emissions are highly material for utilities (regulated, carbon-taxed) but low materiality for a software company with minimal emissions.'},{term:'Greenwashing',definition:'Making misleading claims about a company\'s or product\'s environmental credentials to attract ESG-conscious investors or customers.',example:'A fossil fuel company claiming to be "net zero by 2050" without a credible transition plan, stated ambitions without concrete actions.'}],'Valuation impact flowchart: ESG Factors \u2192 Revenue, Costs, WACC, Terminal Value \u2192 Adjusted DCF \u2192 ESG-Integrated Fair Value',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'ESG Integration in Valuation',nodes:[{label:'ESG Factors'},{label:'Revenue, Costs, WACC, Terminal Value'},{label:'Adjusted DCF'},{label:'ESG-Integrated Fair Value'}],footer:'Material ESG factors vary by industry',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('A bond where proceeds are specifically allocated to environmental projects is called a ___ bond.',['social','sustainability','green','impact'],2),(0,r.q)('Volkswagen\'s emissions fraud cost $33 billion. This is an example of which ESG risk category?',['Environmental only, since the scandal was entirely about the level of emissions from the cars','Social only, since the harm was limited to deceiving the public about vehicle performance','Governance only, the fraud was a failure of internal controls, board oversight, and corporate culture','All three: Environmental (emissions), Social (public health deception), and Governance (management fraud and board failure)'],3,'VW\'s scandal hit all three pillars: Environmental (excess emissions), Social (deceiving regulators and consumers about public health impacts), and Governance (management-directed fraud with inadequate board oversight). ESG risks compound.'),(0,r.ds)('Order the ESG investment approaches from least to most integrated:',['Negative screening, exclude sin stocks (tobacco, weapons)','ESG integration, incorporate material ESG factors into financial analysis','Best-in-class, invest in ESG leaders within each sector','Impact investing, target measurable social/environmental outcomes alongside returns','Thematic investing, focus on sustainability themes (clean energy, water)']),(0,r.sc)('Your company\'s board is debating whether to issue a green bond or a conventional bond. The green bond would cost $2M more in verification and reporting but offers a 7 basis point lower yield on $1 billion. What do you recommend?',[{label:'Issue the conventional bond, the 7bp savings don\'t justify $2M in extra costs',outcome:'Let\'s check the math: 7bp on $1B = $700K/year savings. On a 10-year bond, that\'s $7M in total savings vs. $2M in costs. Net benefit: $5M. Plus, the green bond signals ESG commitment to the growing pool of ESG-mandated investors, improving future capital access.',correct:!1},{label:'Issue the green bond, the $7M in interest savings over 10 years exceeds the $2M cost, and it positions the company favorably with ESG investors for future financing',outcome:'Excellent financial and strategic thinking. The greenium delivers clear NPV-positive savings ($5M net over 10 years). The strategic benefit, demonstrated ESG commitment to a rapidly growing investor base, is potentially worth even more in future financing cost reductions.',correct:!0},{label:'Issue both, conventional for general purposes, green for specific projects',outcome:'Not unreasonable for large issuers, but if the company has qualifying green projects, the green bond dominates the conventional bond financially (lower cost) and strategically (ESG signaling).',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'ESG stands for Environmental, Social, and Governance',a:!0},{s:'Green bonds always have higher yields than conventional bonds',a:!1},{s:'Climate risk is only relevant for energy companies',a:!1},{s:'The EU Emissions Trading System puts a price on carbon emissions',a:!0},{s:'ESG materiality is the same across all industries',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the ESG Term',[['Stranded Assets','Fossil fuel reserves that may become worthless under climate targets'],['Greenium','Lower yield on green bonds vs. conventional bonds'],['Materiality','Industry-specific relevance of ESG factors to financial performance'],['Transition Risk','Risk from policy changes, carbon pricing, and technology shifts'],['Greenwashing','Misleading claims about environmental credentials']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('ESG & Sustainable Finance',[{takeaway:'ESG risk is financial risk, the numbers prove it',detail:'BP ($65B), VW ($33B), PG&E ($30B in bankruptcy), ESG failures have destroyed more value than most companies ever create. Ignoring ESG is not a neutral financial decision.'},{takeaway:'Green finance has moved from niche to mainstream',detail:'Green bonds ($575B+), sustainability-linked loans ($350B+), and ESG-mandated assets ($35T+) have created a parallel financing system that rewards ESG leaders and penalizes laggards.'},{takeaway:'ESG integration improves valuation accuracy when done rigorously',detail:'Material ESG factors affect revenue, costs, WACC, and terminal value. Industry-specific materiality (SASB framework) ensures analysis focuses on financially relevant factors, not greenwashing.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('ESG & Sustainable Finance','Financial Regulation & Compliance','You\'ll learn how regulations like Dodd-Frank, Basel III, and SOX shape every aspect of corporate finance, and why understanding the regulatory landscape is essential for any finance professional.',35)],[(0,r.cq)('Why did BlackRock\'s CEO say "climate risk is investment risk"?',['It was purely marketing, a statement designed to attract more ESG-minded clients to the firm','Because physical climate risks (extreme weather, sea level rise) and transition risks (carbon pricing, policy changes) directly affect the financial performance and valuation of portfolio companies','The government legally required him to make the statement as a condition of operating the firm','Climate change is not a real financial risk, and the comment had no basis in portfolio analysis'],1,'Climate risk affects asset values through two channels: physical risk (direct damage) and transition risk (policy/technology changes). Companies that don\'t plan for these risks face potential value destruction, making it a fiduciary obligation to assess them.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a "greenium" in bond markets?',['A premium charged for green bonds','The lower yield (cheaper borrowing cost) on green bonds compared to conventional bonds from the same issuer, reflecting excess demand from ESG-mandated investors','A government subsidy for green companies','A tax on polluting companies'],1,'ESG-mandated investors (pension funds, sovereign wealth funds) must buy green bonds, creating demand that exceeds supply. This excess demand pushes yields down, the greenium, making green financing cheaper.'),(0,r.cq)('BP\'s Deepwater Horizon cost $65B on a $180B market cap. What does this demonstrate about ESG risk?',['Environmental accidents are inevitable','A single environmental failure destroyed 36% of company value, proving that ESG risks are material financial risks that can dwarf quarterly earnings variations','BP should have had better insurance','Oil companies are too risky to invest in'],1,'36% of market cap destroyed by one event. Traditional financial analysis focuses on earnings, margins, and growth. ESG analysis asks: what\'s the probability and magnitude of catastrophic ESG failure? For BP, that risk was underpriced.'),(0,r.cq)('What is ESG materiality and why does it vary by industry?',['All ESG factors are equally important for all companies','Specific ESG factors have different financial relevance depending on the industry, carbon emissions are material for energy but not software, while data privacy is material for tech but not mining','Materiality only applies to large companies','It\'s a legal requirement'],1,'SASB identifies material ESG factors for 77 industries. Focusing on material factors ensures analysis is financially relevant, not every ESG factor matters equally to every company.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a sustainability-linked loan (SLL)?',['A loan only available to sustainable companies','A loan with interest rates tied to ESG performance targets, rates decrease if targets are met and increase if missed, creating direct financial incentives for ESG improvement','A government-backed environmental loan','A loan denominated in carbon credits'],1,'SLLs connect the cost of capital directly to ESG outcomes. This creates a quantifiable financial incentive: meet your emissions/diversity targets and pay less interest; miss them and pay more.')]),(0,r.L)(20,'Financial Regulation & Compliance',40,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Financial Regulation & Compliance','After the 2008 financial crisis destroyed $10 trillion in wealth and cost 8.7 million jobs, Congress passed the Dodd-Frank Act, 2,300 pages of regulation that fundamentally restructured financial oversight. Banks that were "too big to fail" now face stress tests, higher capital requirements, and restrictions on proprietary trading. Every major financial regulation was written in blood, born from a crisis that cost people their jobs, homes, and savings.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand the regulatory framework governing corporate finance, key regulations like SOX, Dodd-Frank, and Basel III, and why compliance is a strategic function, not just a cost center.','Timeline of major financial regulations: Glass-Steagall (1933) \u2192 SOX (2002) \u2192 Dodd-Frank (2010) \u2192 Basel III (2017)',{type:'metric',title:'Dodd-Frank Act (2010)',value:'2,300 pages',detail:'Born after the crisis destroyed $10T and 8.7M jobs',footer:'Every major financial regulation was written in blood',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Regulation ignored or circumvented',detail:'Wirecard, the German payment company, fabricated \u20ac1.9 billion in cash that didn\'t exist. Auditors (EY) failed to verify basic bank statements for three years. When the fraud was exposed in 2020, the \u20ac24 billion company collapsed in days. The failure was in both the company\'s compliance and the regulatory framework\'s enforcement.'},{label:'Regulation as competitive advantage',detail:'JPMorgan Chase invested $12 billion/year in compliance and technology, building systems that exceeded regulatory requirements. During the 2023 banking crisis, JPMorgan\'s regulatory capital cushion ($50B+ above requirements) allowed it to absorb First Republic Bank, turning compliance investment into a $200B asset acquisition opportunity.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Wirecard: Regulation Circumvented',value:'\u20ac1.9B fabricated',detail:'Auditors failed to verify bank statements for three years',footer:'The \u20ac24B company collapsed in days',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'metric',title:'JPMorgan: Compliance Advantage',value:'$12B/year invested',detail:'$50B+ capital cushion above requirements',footer:'Compliance enabled the $200B First Republic acquisition',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE REGULATORY FRAMEWORK','Key Regulations Every Finance Professional Must Know',['Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX, 2002): Born from Enron and WorldCom. Requires CEO/CFO personal certification of financials, independent audit committees, internal controls testing (Section 404), and whistleblower protections. Cost: $2-5M/year for compliance. Impact: Financial fraud decreased 40% in the decade after SOX, and the quality of financial reporting measurably improved.','Dodd-Frank Act (2010): Born from the 2008 crisis. Created the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB), imposed stress tests on large banks, restricted proprietary trading (Volcker Rule), mandated central clearing of derivatives, and established the Financial Stability Oversight Council. Dodd-Frank didn\'t prevent all risk, but it built shock absorbers that made the 2023 banking stress manageable versus catastrophic.','Basel III (2017 implementation): International banking regulation requiring banks to hold more and higher-quality capital. Minimum Common Equity Tier 1 ratio of 4.5% (vs. 2% under Basel II), plus capital conservation buffers and countercyclical buffers. The result: major banks entered the 2020 COVID crisis with 2-3x the capital they had in 2008, absorbing enormous losses without systemic failure.'],[{term:'SOX Section 404',definition:'Requires companies to document, test, and report on the effectiveness of internal controls over financial reporting. The most costly and impactful provision of SOX.',example:'A company must test every control in its financial reporting process, from invoice approval to journal entry review, and have its auditor opine on effectiveness.'},{term:'Volcker Rule',definition:'A Dodd-Frank provision prohibiting banks from proprietary trading (trading for their own profit) and limiting investments in hedge funds and private equity.',example:'Goldman Sachs shut down its proprietary trading desk after Volcker Rule implementation, pivoting those traders to client-facing roles.'},{term:'Stress Test',definition:'Annual regulatory exercise where the Fed evaluates whether large banks can withstand severe economic scenarios (deep recession, market crash) and still maintain adequate capital.',example:'The Fed\'s 2024 stress test modeled a 40% stock market crash, 10% unemployment, and 36% home price decline, banks had to prove survival with capital above minimums.'},{term:'Basel III',definition:'International banking regulation framework requiring higher capital ratios, better liquidity management, and leverage limits for banks.',example:'Basel III\'s Liquidity Coverage Ratio requires banks to hold enough liquid assets to cover 30 days of cash outflows in a stress scenario.'}],'Regulatory pyramid: Basel III (foundation/capital) \u2192 Dodd-Frank (systemic risk/consumer protection) \u2192 SOX (financial reporting/internal controls) \u2192 SEC/PCAOB (enforcement)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'Major Financial Regulations',nodes:[{label:'Glass-Steagall',value:'1933'},{label:'SOX',value:'2002'},{label:'Dodd-Frank',value:'2010'},{label:'Basel III',value:'2017'}],footer:'Compliance is a strategic function, not just a cost center',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('SOX requires CEO/CFO personal certification of financial statements. If fraud is later discovered, what is the consequence?',['A small fine','Criminal prosecution, CEOs face up to 20 years in prison and $5 million in personal fines for knowingly certifying false financials','Nothing, certification is symbolic','The company pays a fine, not the CEO'],1,'Correct! SOX Section 906 makes personal certification a criminal act if knowingly false. This is why Enron-style CEO denials of knowledge no longer work as a defense, you signed it, you\'re responsible.','SOX Section 302 (civil) and Section 906 (criminal) hold executives personally liable. Up to 20 years imprisonment and $5M fine for knowing certification of false financials.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('COMPLIANCE COST VS. BENEFIT','The Economics of Financial Regulation','comparison','Adjust the compliance investment level and regulatory environment to see how costs compare to the potential losses from non-compliance.',['The cost of compliance is significant but quantifiable: SOX compliance costs $2-5M/year for public companies. Dodd-Frank compliance costs large banks $1-2B/year collectively. But the cost of non-compliance is catastrophic and unpredictable: fines, criminal prosecution, reputational destruction, and potential bankruptcy.','The compliance function has evolved from a back-office cost center to a strategic capability. Companies that invest proactively in compliance technology (RegTech) can meet regulatory requirements at lower cost while gaining competitive advantages: faster product launches (pre-approved compliance frameworks), better data management, and stronger risk detection. JPMorgan\'s $12B tech spend is as much about competitive advantage as compliance.','Regulatory arbitrage, structuring transactions to exploit differences between regulatory regimes, is a constant tension. Companies may incorporate in Delaware for business-friendly laws, list in London for lighter disclosure requirements, or route transactions through less-regulated jurisdictions. Regulators respond with anti-avoidance rules and international coordination. The arms race continues.'],[{term:'RegTech',definition:'Technology solutions that help companies manage regulatory compliance more efficiently, automated reporting, real-time monitoring, AI-powered risk detection.',example:'An AI system that monitors all trades in real-time for suspicious patterns can detect insider trading faster than manual review and at 1/10th the cost.'},{term:'Regulatory Arbitrage',definition:'Structuring transactions or entities to exploit differences between regulatory regimes for financial advantage.',example:'A bank might route a derivative trade through its London subsidiary where capital requirements are lower than in New York.'}],['SOX compliance costs: $2-5M/year. The 2008 crisis cost: $10 trillion. The math favors regulation.','Banks\' Common Equity Tier 1 ratios: ~4% (2007, pre-crisis) \u2192 ~12% (2024, post-Basel III). Triple the capital cushion.','Wirecard\'s regulatory failure cost \u20ac24B in market value and destroyed Germany\'s reputation as a well-regulated market.'],[{label:'Annual Compliance Budget ($M)',min:1,max:20,default:5,unit:'$M'},{label:'Regulatory Scrutiny Level',min:1,max:10,default:6,unit:''}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('COMPLIANCE IN PRACTICE','Building a Culture of Regulatory Excellence',['The three lines of defense model structures compliance: (1) First line, business units own their risks and comply with regulations in daily operations. (2) Second line, the compliance and risk management functions set policies, monitor adherence, and advise business units. (3) Third line, internal audit independently verifies that lines 1 and 2 are working. This layered approach ensures no single failure point.','Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and Know Your Customer (KYC) regulations require financial institutions to verify customer identities and monitor transactions for suspicious activity. Non-compliance penalties are severe: HSBC paid $1.9B for AML failures related to drug cartels. Danske Bank faced $2B+ in fines for \u20ac200B in suspicious Russian money flows through its Estonian branch.','The future of regulation: AI and machine learning are transforming both compliance and regulation. Regulators are developing "SupTech" (supervisory technology) to monitor markets in real-time. Companies are using AI for transaction monitoring, regulatory reporting, and predictive compliance. The regulatory landscape will become more technology-driven, more data-intensive, and harder to circumvent through traditional arbitrage.'],[{term:'Three Lines of Defense',definition:'A governance model where business units (1st), compliance functions (2nd), and internal audit (3rd) each play distinct roles in managing risk and ensuring regulatory compliance.',example:'A trader (1st line) follows trading limits. Compliance (2nd line) monitors for breaches. Internal audit (3rd line) tests whether monitoring is effective.'},{term:'AML/KYC',definition:'Anti-Money Laundering and Know Your Customer, regulations requiring financial institutions to verify identities and monitor for illicit financial activity.',example:'Opening a bank account requires government ID verification, source-of-funds documentation, and ongoing transaction monitoring, all KYC/AML requirements.'}],'Three Lines of Defense: 1st Line (Business, owns risk) \u2192 2nd Line (Compliance, monitors) \u2192 3rd Line (Internal Audit, verifies) \u2192 Board/Regulators (oversee all)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Compliance in Practice',items:[{label:'Tone at the Top',value:'Leadership sets culture',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Controls & Testing',value:'Verify, don\'t trust',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Whistleblower Channels',value:'Surface issues early',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'A culture of regulatory excellence prevents catastrophe',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The Dodd-Frank provision prohibiting banks from proprietary trading is called the ___ Rule.',['Volcker','Glass','Sherman','Clayton'],0),(0,r.q)('HSBC paid $1.9B in fines for AML failures. Danske Bank faced $2B+ for \u20ac200B in suspicious flows. What do these cases demonstrate?',['AML regulations are too strict','The cost of compliance failure far exceeds the cost of compliance, and criminal penalties can threaten institutional survival','Banks should avoid international operations','Regulators are unfair to banks'],1,'$1.9B and $2B+ in fines vs. perhaps $50-100M/year in robust AML systems. The math is clear: invest in compliance or face existential penalties. Both cases also led to criminal charges against individual executives.'),(0,r.tr)('Financial Regulation Essentials',[['What triggered SOX?','Enron and WorldCom accounting frauds. SOX requires CEO/CFO certification, independent audits, and internal controls testing.'],['What triggered Dodd-Frank?','The 2008 financial crisis. Dodd-Frank imposed stress tests, restricted proprietary trading (Volcker Rule), and created consumer protections.'],['What is Basel III?','International banking capital requirements: minimum 4.5% CET1 ratio, liquidity coverage ratio, and leverage limits. Banks now hold 2-3x the capital they had in 2008.'],['What are the three lines of defense?','Business units own risk (1st), compliance monitors and advises (2nd), internal audit independently verifies (3rd).']]),(0,r.sc)('Your company is preparing for its first SOX audit. The CFO wants to minimize compliance costs by testing only "the most important" controls. The auditor insists on testing all material controls. What do you advise?',[{label:'Side with the CFO, testing fewer controls saves money',outcome:'Dangerous shortcut. SOX Section 404 requires testing ALL material controls. If the auditor issues an adverse opinion on internal controls, the stock price typically drops 5-10% and institutional investors flee. The $500K saved on testing is dwarfed by potential losses.',correct:!1},{label:'Side with the auditor, test all material controls. The cost of a failed SOX audit (adverse opinion, stock decline, investor flight) far exceeds the cost of thorough testing',outcome:'Correct and prudent. A SOX failure has cascading consequences: adverse audit opinion \u2192 stock decline \u2192 institutional selling \u2192 higher cost of capital \u2192 potential SEC investigation. The $2-5M investment in proper compliance is insurance against much larger losses.',correct:!0},{label:'Delay the SOX audit by a quarter to reduce costs through a phased approach',outcome:'SOX deadlines are regulatory requirements, not suggestions. Missing the deadline triggers SEC enforcement action and signals governance failure to investors. Compliance must be on time and thorough.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'SOX was enacted in response to the 2008 financial crisis',a:!1},{s:'The Volcker Rule restricts banks from proprietary trading',a:!0},{s:'Basel III requires banks to hold less capital than Basel II',a:!1},{s:'CEO/CFO can face criminal prosecution for certifying false financial statements under SOX',a:!0},{s:'The three lines of defense model uses business, compliance, and internal audit',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Regulation to Its Trigger',[['Glass-Steagall (1933)','The Great Depression and bank failures'],['Sarbanes-Oxley (2002)','Enron and WorldCom accounting frauds'],['Dodd-Frank (2010)','The 2008 financial crisis'],['Basel III (2017)','Global banking system fragility'],['CSRD (2024)','ESG disclosure and climate risk requirements']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Financial Regulation & Compliance',[{takeaway:'Every major regulation was written in the aftermath of a crisis',detail:'Glass-Steagall (Depression), SOX (Enron), Dodd-Frank (2008 crisis), Basel III (bank failures). Regulation is reactive, the cost of the crisis always exceeds the cost of the regulation that follows.'},{takeaway:'Compliance is an investment, not just a cost',detail:'JPMorgan\'s $12B/year tech and compliance spend enabled it to absorb First Republic during the 2023 crisis. Strong compliance creates competitive advantage through operational resilience and regulatory trust.'},{takeaway:'Personal liability changed corporate behavior',detail:'SOX\'s CEO/CFO criminal certification provisions fundamentally changed the incentive structure. When executives face prison for fraud, the quality of financial reporting improves dramatically.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Financial Regulation & Compliance','Startup Finance & Venture Funding','You\'ll learn how startups raise capital from angels to Series A to IPO, the venture capital ecosystem, term sheets, valuation methods, and why 90% of startups fail but the winners generate extraordinary returns.',35)],[(0,r.cq)('What triggered the Sarbanes-Oxley Act?',['The 2008 financial crisis','The Enron and WorldCom accounting scandals, SOX mandated CEO/CFO certification, independent audit committees, and internal controls testing to prevent future financial fraud','The dot-com bubble','The 1987 stock market crash'],1,'Enron ($63B) and WorldCom ($11B) were the largest accounting frauds in history at the time. SOX was the direct legislative response, making executives personally criminally liable for false financial statements.'),(0,r.cq)('Under the Volcker Rule, what are banks prohibited from doing?',['Making loans to consumers','Proprietary trading, trading securities for the bank\'s own profit rather than on behalf of clients','Paying dividends during recessions','Hiring new employees'],1,'The Volcker Rule separates client-facing market-making (allowed) from speculative proprietary trading (prohibited). This prevents banks from gambling with depositor money while receiving government backstop protection.'),(0,r.cq)('Basel III increased minimum CET1 capital ratios from 2% to 4.5%. Why does this matter?',['Banks make less profit','Banks now hold 2-3x more capital to absorb losses, making the banking system more resilient to economic shocks, demonstrated by banks surviving the 2020 COVID crisis with minimal systemic stress','It makes borrowing more expensive','It only affects small banks'],1,'In 2008, banks had thin capital buffers that were quickly overwhelmed. Basel III\'s higher requirements meant that during COVID (a potentially worse economic shock), banks had sufficient capital to absorb losses and continue lending.'),(0,r.cq)('What are the three lines of defense in compliance?',['Government, lawyers, and auditors','First line (business units own risk), Second line (compliance monitors and advises), Third line (internal audit independently verifies)','CEO, CFO, and board','Legal, accounting, and operations'],1,'This layered model ensures no single failure point in risk management. Each line has a distinct role: own risk, monitor risk, and independently verify that monitoring works.'),(0,r.cq)('Why is compliance increasingly viewed as a competitive advantage rather than just a cost?',['It isn\'t, compliance is only a cost','Companies with strong compliance can act faster (pre-approved frameworks), survive crises (capital cushions), and absorb weakened competitors, as JPMorgan demonstrated by acquiring First Republic during the 2023 banking crisis','Regulators reward compliant companies with subsidies','Compliance reduces taxes'],1,'JPMorgan\'s $12B/year compliance investment created operational resilience that became a strategic asset during crisis. While competitors failed, JPMorgan was positioned to acquire assets at distressed prices.')]),(0,r.L)(21,'Startup Finance & Venture Funding',45,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Startup Finance & Venture Funding','In 2004, Peter Thiel wrote a $500,000 check for 10.2% of a startup called Facebook. When Facebook IPO\'d in 2012, that stake was worth over $1 billion, a 2,000x return. But for every Facebook, there are a thousand startups that returned zero. Venture capital is the highest-risk, highest-reward corner of corporate finance, where $500K can become $1B or vanish entirely.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how startups raise capital through each stage, how VCs evaluate investments, term sheet mechanics, and why the venture model only works because a few massive winners pay for hundreds of losers.','Funding lifecycle: Pre-seed \u2192 Seed \u2192 Series A \u2192 Series B \u2192 Growth \u2192 IPO/Exit, with typical valuations at each stage',{type:'metric',title:'Thiel\'s Facebook Bet (2004)',value:'$500K \u2192 $1B+',detail:'10.2% stake delivered a 2,000x return',footer:'For every Facebook, a thousand startups return zero',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Venture investing gone wrong',detail:'SoftBank\'s Vision Fund invested $100 billion across hundreds of startups at peak valuations in 2019-2021, including $18.5B in WeWork (wrote down to near zero), $8B in Uber (sold at a loss), and $3B in Wirecard (total loss). The fund posted $27B in cumulative losses by 2023. Overpaying, even for great ideas, destroys returns.'},{label:'Venture investing done right',detail:'Sequoia Capital\'s early investments in Apple ($150K \u2192 billions), Google ($12.5M \u2192 $5B+), and WhatsApp ($60M \u2192 $3B return from Facebook acquisition) demonstrate the venture model: invest early, price disciplined, win big on the hits. Sequoia has returned 100x+ to its LPs over its lifetime.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'bars',title:'SoftBank Vision Fund Losses',items:[{label:'WeWork',value:100,displayValue:'$18.5B ~$0'},{label:'Uber',value:43,displayValue:'$8B loss'},{label:'Wirecard',value:16,displayValue:'$3B gone'}],footer:'$27B in cumulative losses, overpaying destroys returns',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'bars',title:'Sequoia: The Venture Model',items:[{label:'Apple',value:60,displayValue:'$150K \u2192 billions'},{label:'Google',value:80,displayValue:'$12.5M \u2192 $5B+'},{label:'WhatsApp',value:100,displayValue:'$60M \u2192 $3B'}],footer:'Invest early, price disciplined, win big, 100x+ to LPs',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE VENTURE LIFECYCLE','From Idea to IPO',['Pre-seed and Seed ($50K-$3M at $1-15M valuation): Founders build a minimum viable product (MVP) with personal savings, friends and family, angel investors, or accelerators like Y Combinator. At this stage, there\'s often no revenue, investors bet on the team, market size, and the insight behind the product. Y Combinator invests $500K for ~7% equity; its best investments (Airbnb, Dropbox, Stripe) returned thousands of multiples.','Series A-C ($5M-$200M at $15M-$2B+ valuation): Institutional VCs enter. Series A proves product-market fit, consistent user growth and early revenue. Series B scales the business, rapid expansion, hiring, market penetration. Series C and beyond fund market dominance, international expansion, acquisitions, preparing for IPO. Each round typically sells 15-25% of the company to new investors, diluting existing shareholders.','The "power law" governs venture returns: in a typical VC fund, 50% of investments return zero, 30% return 1-3x, and the top 5-10% generate 90%+ of total returns. A single investment like Facebook or Google can return the entire fund 3-5x. This is why VCs need to invest in high-potential companies and can\'t optimize for "safe" returns, the model only works with outlier outcomes.'],[{term:'Dilution',definition:'The reduction in ownership percentage that existing shareholders experience when new shares are issued to new investors.',example:'A founder who owns 100% raises a Series A selling 20% to VCs. The founder now owns 80%. After Series B (another 20%), they own 64% (80% \xd7 0.80).'},{term:'Valuation (Pre-Money/Post-Money)',definition:'Pre-money valuation is the company\'s worth before new investment. Post-money = Pre-money + New investment.',example:'$10M pre-money + $5M investment = $15M post-money. The investor owns $5M/$15M = 33.3% of the company.'},{term:'Power Law',definition:'The statistical distribution where a small number of extreme winners generate the vast majority of returns. Fundamental to venture capital.',example:'In Sequoia\'s portfolio, investments in Apple, Google, and WhatsApp generated more returns than hundreds of other investments combined.'}],'Funding funnel: 1,000 startups \u2192 100 get seed funding \u2192 20 get Series A \u2192 5 reach Series C \u2192 1-2 achieve IPO/major exit',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The Funding Funnel',nodes:[{label:'1,000 startups'},{label:'100 seed funded'},{label:'20 Series A'},{label:'5 Series C'},{label:'1-2 IPO/exit'}],footer:'The power law: a few winners pay for hundreds of losers',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A founder owns 100% of a startup. They raise a Seed round selling 15%, then Series A selling 20%, then Series B selling 20%. What percentage does the founder own after all three rounds?',['45%','54.4%','65%','50%'],1,'Correct! After Seed: 100% \xd7 0.85 = 85%. After Series A: 85% \xd7 0.80 = 68%. After Series B: 68% \xd7 0.80 = 54.4%. Each round multiplicatively dilutes previous shareholders. Founders must balance raising capital with preserving enough ownership to stay motivated.','Dilution is multiplicative: 100% \xd7 (1-0.15) \xd7 (1-0.20) \xd7 (1-0.20) = 100% \xd7 0.85 \xd7 0.80 \xd7 0.80 = 54.4%.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('VENTURE RETURNS','The Power Law in Action','comparison','Adjust the portfolio size and hit rate to see how the power law drives venture fund returns.',['A typical VC fund invests in 20-30 companies over 3-4 years. The expected return distribution: ~50% return 0-1x (losses), ~30% return 1-5x (modest wins), ~15% return 5-20x (good wins), and ~5% return 20x+ (home runs). A single 50x return in a 20-company portfolio delivers 2.5x on the entire fund, even if everything else goes to zero.','Term sheets define the economic and control terms of a VC investment. Key economic terms: liquidation preference (VCs get their money back first before common shareholders), anti-dilution protection (VCs get additional shares if the company raises money at a lower valuation), and participation rights (double-dipping: get liquidation preference PLUS pro-rata share of remaining proceeds). Key control terms: board seats, protective provisions (veto rights), and drag-along rights.','The venture exit landscape: IPO (historically 5-10% of VC-backed companies), M&A (30-40%, most common successful exit), secondary sales (investors sell to other investors), and failure/shutdown (50-60%). The average time to exit is 7-10 years. VCs need to return their fund within 10-12 years, creating pressure for exits, sometimes at suboptimal timing.'],[{term:'Liquidation Preference',definition:'VC investors\' right to receive their investment back (1-2x) before common shareholders receive anything in a sale or liquidation.',example:'A VC invests $10M with 1x liquidation preference. If the company sells for $8M, the VC gets all $8M. The founders get zero.'},{term:'Anti-Dilution',definition:'Protection that gives VCs additional shares if the company raises money at a lower valuation (a "down round"), reducing the impact of the price drop on the VC\'s ownership.',example:'A VC invested at $10/share. A down round prices shares at $5. Anti-dilution provisions might adjust the VC\'s effective price to $7/share through additional share issuance.'}],['50% of VC-backed startups return zero. The top 5% of investments generate 90%+ of fund returns.','Average time from first VC investment to exit: 7-10 years.','Y Combinator has funded 4,000+ startups with combined valuation exceeding $600B.'],[{label:'Portfolio Companies',min:10,max:50,default:25,unit:''},{label:'Home Run Multiple (x)',min:20,max:200,default:50,unit:'x'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('TERM SHEETS & NEGOTIATION','The Economics Behind Startup Deals',['Valuation negotiation is the most visible but not the most important term. A higher valuation means less dilution for founders, but aggressive terms (2x participating liquidation preference, full ratchet anti-dilution, board control) can make a high valuation worthless in practice. Experienced founders focus on the full package, not just the headline number.','Convertible notes and SAFEs (Simple Agreement for Future Equity) dominate seed-stage funding. Instead of setting a valuation immediately, the investment converts to equity at the next priced round with a discount (typically 20%) and valuation cap. This defers the difficult valuation question to Series A when more data exists. Y Combinator popularized SAFEs, which have become the standard for seed investing.','Down rounds, raising money at a lower valuation than the previous round, trigger anti-dilution protections and signal distress. In 2022-2023, the "Zero Interest Rate Phenomenon" (ZIRP) hangover caused widespread down rounds as startups valued at 50-100x revenue in 2021 were forced to raise at 10-20x. Instacart\'s valuation dropped from $39B to $13B. Stripe dropped from $95B to $50B. Down rounds are painful but sometimes necessary for survival.'],[{term:'SAFE',definition:'Simple Agreement for Future Equity, a seed-stage investment instrument that converts to equity at the next priced round, with a valuation cap and/or discount.',example:'An investor puts in $500K via SAFE with a $10M cap. At Series A valued at $20M, the SAFE converts at the $10M cap, giving the investor 2x the shares they\'d get at Series A price.'},{term:'Down Round',definition:'A funding round at a lower valuation than the previous round, triggering anti-dilution protections and signaling company distress.',example:'Instacart raised at $39B in 2021, then was valued at $13B in 2023, a 67% down round that massively diluted earlier investors.'}],'Term sheet anatomy: Valuation (headline) \u2192 Liquidation Preference (who gets paid first) \u2192 Anti-Dilution (downside protection) \u2192 Board Composition (who controls decisions) \u2192 Vesting (founder commitment)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'Term Sheet Anatomy',nodes:[{label:'Valuation',value:'Headline number'},{label:'Liquidation Preference',value:'Who gets paid first'},{label:'Anti-Dilution',value:'Downside protection'},{label:'Board Composition',value:'Who controls'}],footer:'Terms matter more than the headline valuation',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('In venture capital, the distribution where a few massive winners generate most returns is called the ___ law.',['power','Pareto','normal','exponential'],0),(0,r.q)('A VC invests $10M at a $40M post-money valuation with a 1x participating liquidation preference. The company later sells for $30M. How much does the VC receive?',['$7.5M (their 25% share)','$10M liquidation preference first, then 25% of remaining $20M = $10M + $5M = $15M','$10M (just their money back)','$30M (everything)'],1,'Participating preference means "double-dipping": (1) Get $10M back first (liquidation preference). (2) Then participate pro-rata in the remaining $20M: 25% \xd7 $20M = $5M. Total: $15M on a $30M exit, the VC gets 50% of proceeds despite owning only 25%. This is why term sheet details matter more than valuation.'),(0,r.ds)('Order the startup funding stages:',['Pre-seed: Founders, friends, and family ($50-500K)','Seed: Angel investors and accelerators ($500K-$3M)','Series A: First institutional VC round ($5-15M)','Series B: Scaling the business ($15-50M)','Series C+: Market dominance and pre-IPO ($50-200M+)','IPO or M&A exit']),(0,r.sc)('You\'re a startup founder with two term sheets. VC A offers $5M at $20M pre-money (20% dilution) with a 1x non-participating liquidation preference. VC B offers $5M at $30M pre-money (14.3% dilution) with a 2x participating liquidation preference and full ratchet anti-dilution. Which is better?',[{label:'VC B, higher valuation means less dilution',outcome:'The higher valuation is a trap. The 2x participating liquidation preference means VC B gets $10M back before founders see anything, THEN participates in remaining proceeds. Full ratchet anti-dilution means any down round massively dilutes founders. In most exit scenarios below $100M, VC B\'s terms give founders LESS than VC A\'s "lower" valuation.',correct:!1},{label:'VC A, the cleaner terms (1x non-participating) make the lower valuation actually better for founders in most exit scenarios',outcome:'Excellent analysis. With 1x non-participating preference, VC A chooses the greater of: $5M (liquidation preference) or 20% of exit value. At a $50M exit: VC A gets $10M (20%), founders get $40M. Under VC B at $50M: VC B gets $10M (2x pref) + 14.3% \xd7 $40M remaining ($5.7M) = $15.7M, founders get $34.3M. Cleaner terms win.',correct:!0},{label:'Neither, negotiate a third option',outcome:'Not wrong in spirit, but in practice founders don\'t always have unlimited options. Evaluating the terms you have is essential, and VC A is clearly superior despite the lower headline valuation.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'The power law means most VC returns come from a small number of huge winners',a:!0},{s:'A SAFE is a loan that must be repaid with interest',a:!1},{s:'Dilution from fundraising is multiplicative, each round dilutes ALL previous shareholders',a:!0},{s:'Liquidation preferences only benefit common shareholders',a:!1},{s:'Most VC-backed startups eventually IPO',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Venture Term',[['Seed Round','First external funding to build an MVP'],['Series A','First institutional VC round for product-market fit'],['Liquidation Preference','VCs get their money back first in an exit'],['Power Law','A few winners generate most fund returns'],['Down Round','Funding at a lower valuation than the previous round']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Startup Finance & Venture Funding',[{takeaway:'The power law governs venture returns, a few winners pay for everything',detail:'50% of VC investments return zero. The top 5% generate 90%+ of returns. A single Facebook or Google can return an entire fund 3-5x. This is why VCs chase massive outcomes, not modest returns.'},{takeaway:'Term sheet details matter more than headline valuation',detail:'Liquidation preferences, anti-dilution provisions, and participation rights can make a "high" valuation worthless in practice. Founders must evaluate the full economic package, not just the number on the press release.'},{takeaway:'Each funding round multiplicatively dilutes all previous shareholders',detail:'Three rounds of 20% dilution doesn\'t leave you with 40%, it leaves you with 51.2% (80%^3). Founders must balance raising enough capital to grow with preserving enough equity to stay motivated through the 7-10 year journey to exit.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Startup Finance & Venture Funding','Executive Compensation & Incentives','You\'ll learn how companies design pay packages to align executive behavior with shareholder value, stock options, performance shares, golden parachutes, and why CEO pay has become the most debated topic in corporate governance.',35)],[(0,r.cq)('Peter Thiel invested $500K in Facebook for 10.2% and made $1B+. This is an example of:',['Lucky gambling','The venture capital power law, a single extreme winner generating returns that dwarf everything else in the portfolio','Standard stock market investing','Private equity'],1,'Thiel\'s Facebook investment returned 2,000x. This single outcome was worth more than most entire VC funds. The power law means a few extreme winners drive virtually all venture returns.'),(0,r.cq)('A founder owns 100% and raises three rounds, each selling 20%. What does the founder own afterward?',['40%','54.4%','51.2%','60%'],2,'Dilution is multiplicative: 100% \xd7 0.80 \xd7 0.80 \xd7 0.80 = 51.2%. Each round dilutes ALL prior shareholders, not just the founder, this is why early investors also push for anti-dilution protection.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a liquidation preference and why should founders care?',['A tax benefit for investors','The right of VC investors to receive their investment back (1-2x) before common shareholders get anything in a sale, meaning founders may get zero in a small exit even if they own the majority of the company','A type of stock option','A government regulation'],1,'With a 1x liquidation preference on $10M invested, the VC gets $10M back before anyone else in any exit. If the company sells for $8M, the VC gets all $8M and founders get $0, despite the VC owning only 25%.'),(0,r.cq)('Why do 50% of VC-backed startups return zero but VC funds can still generate strong returns?',['They invest in hundreds of companies','The power law, one company returning 50-100x can pay for all the failures and still deliver 3-5x fund returns. VCs need hits, not batting averages.','Government subsidizes the losses','VCs only invest in guaranteed winners'],1,'In a 25-company portfolio, one 50x return = 2x the entire fund by itself. Even if the other 24 companies all go to zero, that single winner delivers 2x. This is why VCs optimize for upside potential, not downside protection.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a SAFE and why is it popular for seed-stage investing?',['A type of bank account','A Simple Agreement for Future Equity that converts to shares at the next priced round, popular because it defers the difficult valuation question and is simpler/cheaper than a priced round','A government bond for startups','A safety deposit box for investor funds'],1,'SAFEs avoid the cost and complexity of a full priced round at the seed stage. The conversion happens automatically at Series A, using a valuation cap and/or discount that rewards the seed investor for taking earlier risk.')]),(0,r.L)(22,'Executive Compensation & Incentives',45,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Executive Compensation & Incentives','In 2021, Elon Musk earned $0 in salary from Tesla, yet his compensation was worth $56 billion through stock options tied to Tesla reaching specific market cap and revenue milestones. Meanwhile, the average S&P 500 CEO earned $16.7 million, 340 times the median worker\'s pay. Executive compensation is the most visible, controversial, and financially consequential governance issue in corporate finance.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how executive pay packages are structured, the economics of stock options and performance shares, the principal-agent problem, and how to evaluate whether CEO pay creates or destroys shareholder value.','Pay structure breakdown: Base Salary (10-15%) \u2192 Annual Bonus (15-25%) \u2192 Stock Options (25-35%) \u2192 Performance Shares (25-35%)',{type:'metric',title:'Musk\'s Tesla Pay (2021)',value:'$56B / $0 salary',detail:'Entirely tied to market cap and revenue milestones',footer:'Average S&P 500 CEO: $16.7M, 340x the median worker',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Misaligned compensation',detail:'Lehman Brothers\' CEO Dick Fuld earned $484 million in salary and bonuses from 2000-2007, incentivizing short-term risk-taking. When Lehman collapsed in 2008, shareholders lost everything, but Fuld kept his hundreds of millions. The compensation structure rewarded risk-taking with no downside for executives.'},{label:'Well-aligned compensation',detail:'Elon Musk\'s Tesla compensation plan paid $0 unless Tesla\'s market cap grew from $50B to $650B+ AND revenue/profit targets were met. Musk earned $56B because Tesla grew from $50B to $1.2T, shareholders got a 24x return alongside the CEO. The incentive alignment was nearly perfect.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Lehman: Misaligned Pay',value:'$484M to CEO',detail:'Rewarded short-term risk-taking with no downside',footer:'Shareholders lost everything; the CEO kept his millions',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Musk\'s Tesla Plan: Aligned',points:[{label:'Start',value:'$50B'},{label:'Milestones',value:'$650B+'},{label:'Result',value:'$1.2T'}],trend:'up',footer:'Pay $0 unless shareholders won, they got a 24x return',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE PAY PACKAGE','How Executive Compensation Is Structured',['Modern CEO compensation has four components: (1) Base salary ($1-2M, typically 10-15% of total pay). (2) Annual bonus (0-200% of salary, tied to annual financial targets like revenue, EPS, or operating income). (3) Stock options (right to buy shares at today\'s price, valuable only if the stock rises). (4) Performance shares/RSUs (shares granted over 3-5 years, often contingent on meeting performance targets like TSR or ROIC).','The principal-agent problem is the fundamental tension: shareholders (principals) want long-term value creation, but managers (agents) might prefer short-term gains, perks, empire-building, or risk avoidance. Compensation design attempts to align these interests by tying the majority of CEO pay to stock price performance. The question is whether the alignment is real or cosmetic.','The compensation committee, a board subcommittee of independent directors, sets executive pay. They use peer group benchmarking (comparing pay to similar-sized companies), performance metrics, and shareholder feedback. Critics argue this creates a "Lake Wobegon" effect: every company wants to pay above-median, ratcheting pay upward. Supporters argue competitive pay attracts talent.'],[{term:'Stock Options',definition:'The right to purchase company stock at a predetermined "strike" price, typically today\'s market price. Valuable only if the stock price rises above the strike.',example:'A CEO receives options to buy 1M shares at $100 (today\'s price). If the stock rises to $150, each option is worth $50, total: $50M.'},{term:'RSUs (Restricted Stock Units)',definition:'Shares granted to executives that vest over time (typically 3-4 years), sometimes contingent on performance targets.',example:'A CEO receives 100,000 RSUs vesting over 4 years. At $200/share, the grant is worth $20M if the executive stays and meets performance conditions.'},{term:'Principal-Agent Problem',definition:'The conflict of interest between shareholders (principals) who want long-term value and managers (agents) who may pursue personal interests.',example:'A CEO might resist a beneficial acquisition because it risks their job, even though shareholders would benefit from the deal.'},{term:'Golden Parachute',definition:'A large severance package triggered by termination following a change of control (acquisition). Designed to reduce CEO resistance to beneficial takeovers.',example:'A golden parachute might pay the CEO 3x salary + bonus ($15-50M) if the company is acquired and they are terminated.'}],'Alignment spectrum: Salary-heavy (low alignment) \u2192 Bonus-heavy (short-term alignment) \u2192 Equity-heavy (long-term alignment) \u2192 Performance-equity-heavy (strongest alignment)',void 0,{type:'stack',title:'The CEO Pay Package',total:'100% of Pay',segments:[{label:'Base Salary',value:'10-15%',percent:13,color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Annual Bonus',value:'15-25%',percent:22,color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Stock Options',value:'25-35%',percent:30,color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Performance Shares',value:'25-35%',percent:35,color:'#10B981'}],footer:'Equity-heavy pay creates the strongest alignment',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A CEO has options to buy 2M shares at $80 (the strike price). The stock is now $120. What is the value of the options?',['$240M','$160M','$80M','$40M'],2,'Correct! Option value = (Market price \u2212 Strike price) \xd7 Number of options = ($120 \u2212 $80) \xd7 2M = $40 per option \xd7 2M = $80M. Options only have value if the stock price exceeds the strike price.','Each option is worth: $120 (current) \u2212 $80 (strike) = $40. Total: $40 \xd7 2M options = $80M. If the stock were below $80, the options would be worthless ("underwater").'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('PAY FOR PERFORMANCE','Does CEO Compensation Actually Align with Shareholder Returns?','comparison','Adjust the stock performance and CEO pay levels to see how different pay structures affect alignment between executives and shareholders.',['The "Say on Pay" provision in Dodd-Frank gives shareholders a non-binding vote on executive compensation. While advisory only, companies that receive less than 70% support typically make significant changes. In 2024, 97% of S&P 500 companies received majority support, but the 3% that didn\'t faced intense pressure and board-level changes.','Clawback provisions allow companies to recover compensation if financial results are later restated. The SEC\'s 2023 clawback rules mandate recovery of excess incentive pay from executives following a restatement, regardless of fault. This addresses the Lehman problem: executives who earned bonuses based on subsequently revealed bad results must return the money.','The debate over CEO pay ratios: the SEC requires disclosure of the CEO-to-median-employee pay ratio. S&P 500 average: 340:1. Critics argue this reflects market failure and governance capture. Defenders argue CEO pay reflects market pricing of scarce talent whose decisions affect billions in enterprise value. The truth likely lies between: pay should be high to attract talent, but the ratcheting effect of peer benchmarking inflates pay beyond market-clearing levels.'],[{term:'Say on Pay',definition:'A non-binding shareholder vote on executive compensation required by Dodd-Frank. Below 70% support typically triggers changes.',example:'When GE received only 58% Say on Pay support in 2022, it overhauled its CEO compensation structure within months.'},{term:'Clawback',definition:'A provision requiring executives to return compensation if financial results are later restated or misconduct is discovered.',example:'After Wells Fargo\'s fake accounts scandal, the board clawed back $75M from former CEO John Stumpf.'}],['S&P 500 median CEO total compensation: $16.7M (2023). Average CEO-to-worker ratio: 340:1.','Equity-based compensation (options + RSUs) represents 60-75% of total CEO pay at large companies.','Elon Musk\'s $56B Tesla pay package was the largest in history, but required Tesla to grow from $50B to $650B+ market cap.'],[{label:'Stock Price Change (%)',min:-50,max:100,default:20,unit:'%'},{label:'CEO Pay ($M)',min:5,max:100,default:20,unit:'$M'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('COMPENSATION DESIGN','Building Pay Packages That Actually Work',['Effective compensation design follows three principles: (1) Pay for performance, the majority of pay should vary with measurable outcomes. (2) Long-term orientation, vesting periods of 3-5 years discourage short-term manipulation. (3) Downside exposure, executives should face real losses if performance is poor (not just reduced upside). The best structures use performance shares with relative TSR (Total Shareholder Return) hurdles that reward outperformance versus peers.','Perverse incentives are the dark side of compensation design. Stock options encourage risk-taking because options are worth more when volatility is higher (option pricing theory). Annual EPS bonuses incentivize share buybacks over R&D investment. Revenue growth targets can incentivize unprofitable expansion. Every metric can be gamed, which is why the best packages use multiple metrics with balancing checks.','Beyond the C-suite: employee stock ownership programs (ESOPs), broad-based equity grants, and profit-sharing plans extend the alignment logic to all employees. Companies with broad equity ownership (like Microsoft, where thousands of employees became millionaires) often outperform because every employee thinks like an owner. The challenge is designing plans that are meaningful without excessive dilution.'],[{term:'Total Shareholder Return (TSR)',definition:'Stock price appreciation plus dividends, the total return shareholders receive. Often used as a performance metric relative to a peer group.',example:'A CEO\'s performance shares vest only if the company\'s 3-year TSR is in the top quartile versus its peer group, rewarding relative outperformance.'},{term:'ESOP',definition:'Employee Stock Ownership Plan, a retirement plan that invests primarily in company stock, giving all employees an ownership stake.',example:'Publix Super Markets is 100% employee-owned through its ESOP, with employees collectively owning $40B+ in company stock.'}],'Compensation matrix: Metric (Revenue, EPS, TSR, ROIC) \xd7 Time Horizon (1-year bonus, 3-year vesting, 5-year performance) \u2192 Balanced alignment',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Compensation Design Principles',items:[{label:'Pay for Performance',value:'Pay varies with outcomes',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Long-Term Orientation',value:'3-5 year vesting',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Downside Exposure',value:'Real losses if poor',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'The best packages use multiple balanced metrics',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The governance mechanism giving shareholders a non-binding vote on executive pay is called "Say on ___."',['Pay','Compensation','Performance','Stock'],0),(0,r.q)('Dick Fuld earned $484M from Lehman while shareholders lost everything. Elon Musk earned $56B from Tesla while shareholders earned 24x. What\'s the key design difference?',['Fuld was paid more','Musk was paid entirely in performance-based equity that was worthless unless shareholders earned massive returns. Fuld was paid in salary, bonuses, and time-vested equity regardless of long-term outcomes.','Musk worked harder','The companies were in different industries'],1,'Musk\'s compensation was all-or-nothing: $0 unless Tesla hit market cap and operational milestones. Fuld\'s was heavily weighted toward annual cash and bonuses that rewarded short-term metrics. The design determined the alignment.'),(0,r.tr)('Executive Compensation Essentials',[['What are the four components of CEO pay?','Base salary (10-15%), annual bonus (15-25%), stock options (25-35%), and performance shares/RSUs (25-35%).'],['What is the principal-agent problem?','Shareholders want long-term value; managers might prefer short-term gains, perks, or risk avoidance. Compensation tries to align these interests.'],['What is a clawback provision?','A requirement for executives to return compensation if financial results are restated. The SEC mandated universal clawbacks in 2023.'],['Why is the CEO-to-worker ratio controversial?','At 340:1, critics say it reflects governance capture. Defenders say it reflects scarce talent pricing. Both have merit.']]),(0,r.sc)('The compensation committee is designing a new CEO pay package. Option A: $2M salary + $5M cash bonus tied to annual EPS + $10M in time-vested RSUs. Option B: $1M salary + $2M cash bonus + $15M in performance shares vesting only if 3-year TSR exceeds the peer median. Which is better?',[{label:'Option A, more cash provides security and the RSUs provide equity alignment',outcome:'Weak alignment. The $5M EPS bonus incentivizes buybacks over investment. Time-vested RSUs reward tenure, not performance, the CEO gets $10M just for staying, even if the stock drops 50%. Total pay has minimal connection to shareholder outcomes.',correct:!1},{label:'Option B, the performance shares create strong alignment because the CEO only earns the big payout if shareholders earn above-median returns over 3 years',outcome:'Excellent. 83% of total pay ($15M/$18M) is contingent on 3-year relative performance. The CEO is incentivized to outperform peers, not just meet absolute targets that could be achieved in a rising market. The lower cash base also creates urgency.',correct:!0},{label:'Neither, just pay a high salary and avoid the complexity',outcome:'High fixed salary creates zero incentive for performance. This is the worst possible compensation structure from a shareholder alignment perspective.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'Stock options are valuable even if the stock price stays flat or declines',a:!1},{s:'The principal-agent problem describes the conflict between shareholders and managers',a:!0},{s:'Say on Pay votes are legally binding on the board',a:!1},{s:'Clawback provisions allow recovery of executive compensation after financial restatements',a:!0},{s:'CEO pay in the S&P 500 averages about 5x the median worker pay',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Compensation Term',[['Stock Options','Right to buy shares at a preset strike price'],['RSUs','Shares that vest over time, sometimes with performance conditions'],['Golden Parachute','Large severance triggered by acquisition and termination'],['Say on Pay','Shareholder vote on executive compensation'],['Clawback','Recovery of pay after financial restatements']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Executive Compensation & Incentives',[{takeaway:'Compensation design determines executive behavior',detail:'Lehman\'s cash-heavy pay rewarded short-term risk-taking. Tesla\'s all-equity milestone-based plan rewarded long-term shareholder value creation. The design IS the incentive.'},{takeaway:'The principal-agent problem is the central challenge',detail:'Shareholders want long-term value; managers may pursue short-term gains. Well-designed compensation, performance shares, relative TSR hurdles, clawbacks, bridges this gap.'},{takeaway:'Every compensation metric can be gamed',detail:'EPS targets incentivize buybacks. Revenue targets incentivize unprofitable growth. The best packages use multiple metrics (financial, operational, relative) with balancing checks and 3-5 year vesting.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Executive Compensation & Incentives','Corporate Tax Strategy','You\'ll learn how corporate tax strategy affects every aspect of finance, from capital structure decisions to international operations, and why understanding tax is essential for evaluating any corporate finance decision.',40)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the principal-agent problem in executive compensation?',['CEOs are paid too much','The conflict between shareholders (who want long-term value) and managers (who may prefer short-term gains, perks, or risk avoidance), compensation design tries to align these interests','Agents don\'t understand finance','Boards are too small'],1,'The principal-agent problem is the fundamental tension compensation tries to resolve. Without alignment mechanisms, executives might build empires, avoid risk, or focus on short-term metrics that boost their bonuses at the expense of long-term value.'),(0,r.cq)('A CEO has 1M stock options with a $50 strike price. The stock is at $75. What are the options worth?',['$75M','$50M','$25M','$125M'],2,'Value = (Market - Strike) \xd7 Options = ($75 - $50) \xd7 1M = $25M. Options only have value when the stock exceeds the strike price.'),(0,r.cq)('Why was Elon Musk\'s Tesla compensation plan considered well-aligned with shareholders?',['He was paid the most in history','The plan paid $0 unless Tesla\'s market cap grew from $50B to $650B+ AND revenue/profit targets were met, shareholders earned 24x returns alongside the CEO\'s payout','He donated his salary','The board forced the plan on him'],1,'Musk\'s entire $56B payout was contingent on shareholder outcomes. No shareholder returns = no CEO pay. This near-perfect alignment is rare.'),(0,r.cq)('What is a clawback provision and why does it matter?',['A tax deduction for executive pay','A requirement for executives to return compensation if financial results are restated, preventing executives from profiting from inflated or fraudulent results','A bonus for good performance','A type of stock option'],1,'Clawbacks address the Lehman problem: executives earned billions in bonuses based on results that later proved unsustainable or fraudulent. The 2023 SEC rules mandate universal clawbacks.'),(0,r.cq)('The S&P 500 average CEO-to-worker pay ratio is approximately:',['10:1','50:1','340:1','1000:1'],2,'The 340:1 ratio is disclosed per SEC requirements. Whether this reflects efficient market pricing of CEO talent or governance failure is debated, but the number itself is a fact that every corporate finance student should know.')]),(0,r.L)(23,'Corporate Tax Strategy',45,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Corporate Tax Strategy','In 2020, Amazon reported $21.3 billion in pre-tax US income but paid an effective federal tax rate of just 9.4%, well below the statutory 21% rate. Apple has held over $250 billion offshore, routed through Irish subsidiaries that at one point paid an effective tax rate of 0.005%. Corporate tax strategy is not about illegal evasion, it\'s about understanding the legal framework that determines how much companies actually pay, and why the gap between statutory and effective rates matters for every valuation.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand how tax strategy affects corporate finance decisions, the difference between tax avoidance and evasion, international tax structures, and how taxes influence capital structure, location, and investment choices.','Comparison chart: Statutory Tax Rate (21%) vs. Effective Tax Rates for major tech companies (7-15%)',{type:'comparison',title:'Statutory vs. Effective Tax',rows:[{label:'Statutory rate',value:'21%',percent:100},{label:'Amazon effective (2020)',value:'9.4%',percent:45}],footer:'The gap is the value of legal tax strategy',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Tax strategy failure',detail:'In 2016, the EU ruled that Ireland gave Apple illegal tax benefits, ordering Apple to pay \u20ac13 billion in back taxes. The case highlighted the reputational and financial risks of aggressive tax planning, even if technically legal at the time. Apple\'s "Double Irish" structure became politically toxic, forcing restructuring of its entire international tax approach.'},{label:'Tax-efficient finance',detail:'Amazon\'s tax strategy is entirely legal: accelerated depreciation on massive warehouse investments, R&D tax credits on $73B in cumulative R&D spending, stock-based compensation deductions, and strategic loss carryforwards from its unprofitable early years. The result: Amazon reinvested billions that would have gone to taxes into growth, funding the AWS and logistics empire.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'Apple: Tax Strategy Backfired',value:'\u20ac13B back taxes',detail:'The EU ruled the "Double Irish" structure illegal',footer:'Aggressive planning created reputational and financial risk',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'grid',title:'Amazon: Legal Tax Efficiency',items:[{label:'Accelerated Depreciation',color:'#10B981'},{label:'R&D Tax Credits',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Stock-Comp Deductions',color:'#10B981'},{label:'Loss Carryforwards',color:'#10B981'}],footer:'Reinvested tax savings funded the AWS empire',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('TAX FUNDAMENTALS','How Corporate Taxes Actually Work',['The US federal corporate tax rate is 21% (reduced from 35% in the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act). But the effective tax rate, what companies actually pay, is typically 12-18% for large companies due to legal deductions, credits, and deferrals. The gap between statutory and effective rates represents the value of tax strategy.','Key tax shields: (1) Interest deductibility, interest on debt is tax-deductible, reducing the effective cost of debt (the foundation of the WACC tax shield). (2) Depreciation, spreading the cost of assets over their useful life creates a non-cash deduction. Accelerated depreciation (front-loading deductions) is more valuable due to time value of money. (3) R&D tax credits, direct credits for qualified research spending. (4) Loss carryforwards, past losses offset future profits, reducing taxes for years.','Tax avoidance vs. tax evasion: Avoidance is legal, structuring transactions to minimize tax within the law (using deductions, credits, entity structures). Evasion is illegal, hiding income, fake deductions, offshore accounts. The line between aggressive avoidance and evasion is sometimes blurry, which is why companies employ armies of tax lawyers and CPAs to stay on the right side.'],[{term:'Effective Tax Rate',definition:'Taxes actually paid \xf7 Pre-tax income. Usually lower than the statutory rate due to legal deductions, credits, and deferrals.',example:'A company with $1B pre-tax income pays $140M in taxes: effective rate = 14%, versus the 21% statutory rate. The 7% gap reflects tax strategy value.'},{term:'Tax Shield',definition:'A reduction in taxable income from deductible expenses, most commonly interest payments and depreciation.',example:'A company with $500M in interest expense at a 21% tax rate saves $105M in taxes, the interest tax shield that makes debt cheaper than equity.'},{term:'Loss Carryforward',definition:'The ability to use past operating losses to offset future taxable income, reducing future tax payments.',example:'Amazon accumulated $3B+ in net operating losses in its early unprofitable years and used them to reduce taxes for years after becoming profitable.'},{term:'Accelerated Depreciation',definition:'Front-loading depreciation deductions to reduce taxable income in earlier years, creating a time-value benefit compared to straight-line depreciation.',example:'Bonus depreciation allows 100% deduction of equipment costs in the first year instead of spreading over 5-7 years, immediate tax savings.'}],'Tax funnel: Gross Revenue \u2192 minus COGS \u2192 minus Operating Expenses \u2192 minus Tax Shields (Interest, Depreciation, Credits) \u2192 Taxable Income \xd7 Rate = Taxes Owed',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The Tax Funnel',nodes:[{label:'Gross Revenue'},{label:'\u2212 COGS & OpEx'},{label:'\u2212 Tax Shields'},{label:'Taxable Income \xd7 Rate'},{label:'Taxes Owed'}],footer:'Interest, depreciation, and credits shrink the bill',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A company has $1B pre-tax income, $200M in interest expense, $150M in depreciation, and $50M in R&D credits. The tax rate is 21%. What is the approximate tax savings from these shields?',['$400M','$73.5M from deductions + $50M credit = $123.5M total','$84M','$50M'],1,'Correct! Interest + Depreciation deductions: ($200M + $150M) \xd7 21% = $73.5M in tax savings. Plus $50M R&D tax credit (dollar-for-dollar). Total savings: $123.5M. The effective tax on $1B changes from $210M (21%) to $86.5M (8.7% effective rate).','Deduction value: ($200M + $150M) \xd7 0.21 = $73.5M. R&D credit: $50M. Total: $123.5M. This reduces the effective tax rate from 21% to ($210M - $123.5M) \xf7 $1B = 8.65%.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('TAX STRATEGY IMPACT','How Tax Decisions Affect Corporate Value','comparison','Adjust the debt level and depreciation method to see how tax strategy impacts after-tax cash flows and company value.',['Capital structure and taxes are deeply intertwined. The Modigliani-Miller theorem with taxes shows that company value increases with debt because of the interest tax shield: Value of tax shield = Tax rate \xd7 Debt. With 21% tax rate and $10B in debt, the tax shield is worth $2.1B. This is a primary reason companies maintain significant debt even when they have plenty of cash.','International tax planning: the OECD\'s global minimum tax of 15% is reshaping how multinationals structure operations. Before 2024, companies could route profits through tax havens (Ireland at 12.5%, Bermuda at 0%, Netherlands holding companies). The minimum tax reduces but doesn\'t eliminate the benefit of low-tax jurisdictions, the spread between 15% and 21% (US) still creates incentive for international planning.','Tax considerations in M&A: deal structure (asset purchase vs. stock purchase) has massive tax implications. In an asset purchase, the buyer gets a stepped-up tax basis (higher depreciation deductions); in a stock purchase, the buyer inherits the target\'s existing tax basis. The tax difference can be worth 5-10% of deal value, $500M-$1B on a large acquisition. Tax structuring is not an afterthought; it\'s integral to deal economics.'],[{term:'Stepped-Up Basis',definition:'In an asset purchase, the buyer revalues acquired assets to fair market value, creating higher depreciation deductions and future tax savings.',example:'A buyer pays $5B for a company with $1B book value assets. In an asset deal, the $4B "step-up" creates ~$840M in future tax savings (at 21%).'},{term:'Section 1031 Exchange',definition:'A tax-deferral mechanism allowing sale of an investment property and reinvestment in a "like-kind" property without immediate capital gains tax.',example:'A company sells an office building for $50M (original cost $20M) and reinvests in a larger building, deferring the $6.3M capital gains tax.'}],['The interest tax shield on US corporate debt is worth approximately $300B+ annually to corporate America.','The effective tax rate for S&P 500 companies averages 15-18%, vs. the 21% statutory rate.','The OECD global minimum tax of 15% affects multinationals with \u20ac750M+ in revenue.'],[{label:'Debt Level ($B)',min:0,max:20,default:5,unit:'$B'},{label:'Tax Rate (%)',min:10,max:30,default:21,unit:'%'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('TAXES IN PRACTICE','Strategic Tax Decisions Every CFO Makes',['Location decisions are heavily tax-influenced: state corporate tax rates range from 0% (Wyoming, Nevada, South Dakota) to 11.5% (New Jersey). Ireland\'s 12.5% rate attracted hundreds of US tech companies. The TCJA\'s territorial system (2017) changed the calculus for US multinationals, but tax-motivated structuring remains a key CFO function.','Stock-based compensation provides a tax shield that most people don\'t understand: when employees exercise stock options or RSUs vest, the company gets a tax deduction equal to the market value of the shares minus what the employee paid. Amazon\'s massive stock-based compensation deductions ($10B+ annually) are a primary reason its effective tax rate is well below 21%.','Tax reform risk is real and material: the 2017 TCJA reduced the corporate rate from 35% to 21%, increasing after-tax earnings by ~20% for most companies. Any reversal (which is perpetually debated in Congress) would reduce after-tax cash flows and company values significantly. CFOs must scenario-plan for potential tax rate changes and their impact on capital structure, location, and investment decisions.'],[{term:'TCJA',definition:'Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (2017), reduced the US federal corporate tax rate from 35% to 21% and moved to a territorial system for international taxation.',example:'The TCJA increased S&P 500 after-tax earnings by approximately 20%, contributing to the stock market rally from 2018-2019.'},{term:'Territorial System',definition:'A tax system where a country taxes only domestic income, not foreign income. Replaced the US worldwide system under the TCJA.',example:'Under the old system, Apple owed US taxes on all global profits. Under the territorial system, only US-sourced profits are taxed at the US rate.'}],'Decision matrix: Tax Factor \xd7 Business Decision = Capital Structure (debt vs. equity) \xd7 Location (state/country) \xd7 Investment (accelerated depreciation) \xd7 M&A (asset vs. stock deal)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'Strategic Tax Decisions',items:[{label:'Capital Structure',value:'Interest deductibility',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Location',value:'State & country rates',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'M&A Structure',value:'Asset vs. stock deal',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Stock Comp',value:'Deduction at vesting',color:'#A451E6'}],footer:'Taxes influence every major corporate finance decision',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The 2017 law that reduced the US corporate tax rate from 35% to 21% is called the Tax Cuts and ___ Act.',['Reform','Jobs','Recovery','Growth'],1),(0,r.q)('Amazon reports $21.3B pre-tax income but pays only 9.4% effective tax. Is this legal?',['No, Amazon is evading taxes','Yes, Amazon uses legal strategies: accelerated depreciation on warehouses, R&D credits, stock compensation deductions, and loss carryforwards from its unprofitable years','Amazon doesn\'t pay any taxes','Only the US portion is legal'],1,'Every element of Amazon\'s tax strategy uses provisions Congress specifically created to incentivize investment, R&D, and employee equity compensation. Whether these incentives are good policy is debatable; their legality is not.'),(0,r.pc)('Corporate Tax Shield Calculation',[{label:'Pre-tax income',formula:'Revenue \u2212 Expenses',result:'$1,000M'},{label:'Interest deduction',formula:'$200M debt payments',result:'\u2212$200M'},{label:'Depreciation deduction',formula:'Accelerated method',result:'\u2212$150M'},{label:'Taxable income',formula:'$1B \u2212 $200M \u2212 $150M',result:'$650M'},{label:'Tax at 21%',formula:'$650M \xd7 0.21',result:'$136.5M'},{label:'R&D credit',formula:'Dollar-for-dollar',result:'\u2212$50M'},{label:'Total tax owed',formula:'$136.5M \u2212 $50M',result:'$86.5M'},{label:'Effective rate',formula:'$86.5M \xf7 $1,000M',result:'8.65%'}]),(0,r.sc)('Your company is acquiring a target for $2B. You can structure it as an asset purchase (stepped-up basis: $4B in depreciation deductions over 15 years) or a stock purchase (target\'s $500M existing basis continues). Tax rate is 21%. Which structure is better from a tax perspective?',[{label:'Stock purchase, simpler and faster to close',outcome:'Simpler, yes. But you\'re leaving massive tax value on the table. The asset purchase creates $3.5B more in depreciation deductions ($4B \u2212 $0.5B), saving $735M in taxes over 15 years. On a PV basis at 8% discount rate, that\'s ~$450M in value. Tax structuring matters.',correct:!1},{label:'Asset purchase, the $3.5B additional depreciation creates ~$735M in tax savings ($3.5B \xd7 21%), worth ~$450M in present value, a significant portion of the deal economics',outcome:'Excellent tax analysis. The $450M PV of tax savings is 22.5% of the $2B deal price. Many deals that look marginal on pre-tax economics become value-creating after proper tax structuring. This is why tax advisors sit at the deal table.',correct:!0},{label:'It doesn\'t matter, taxes are an afterthought in M&A',outcome:'Taxes are NEVER an afterthought in M&A. Tax structure differences routinely represent 5-10% of deal value. Ignoring tax optimization in a $2B deal means potentially forfeiting $450M.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'The US statutory corporate tax rate is 21%',a:!0},{s:'Interest on corporate debt is not tax-deductible',a:!1},{s:'Tax avoidance and tax evasion are the same thing',a:!1},{s:'Accelerated depreciation provides greater tax benefits than straight-line in early years',a:!0},{s:'The OECD global minimum tax is 21%',a:!1}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Tax Strategy',[['Interest Tax Shield','Deducting debt interest payments to reduce taxable income'],['R&D Tax Credit','Dollar-for-dollar tax reduction for qualified research spending'],['Loss Carryforward','Using past losses to offset future taxable income'],['Stepped-Up Basis','Revaluing acquired assets for higher depreciation deductions'],['Accelerated Depreciation','Front-loading asset deductions for earlier tax savings']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Corporate Tax Strategy',[{takeaway:'The gap between statutory and effective tax rates represents tax strategy value',detail:'The US statutory rate is 21%, but S&P 500 effective rates average 15-18%. Interest deductions, depreciation, R&D credits, and loss carryforwards account for the difference, all legal, all material to valuation.'},{takeaway:'Taxes influence every major corporate finance decision',detail:'Capital structure (interest deductibility favors debt), location (state and international rate differences), M&A (asset vs. stock purchase), and investment timing (accelerated depreciation) are all tax-influenced.'},{takeaway:'International tax is being transformed by the global minimum tax',detail:'The OECD\'s 15% floor reduces but doesn\'t eliminate benefits of low-tax jurisdictions. Companies are shifting from pure profit-shifting to substance-based planning.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Corporate Tax Strategy','Financial Engineering & Structured Products','You\'ll learn how financial engineers create complex instruments, securitization, derivatives, and structured products, that can either manage risk brilliantly or, when misused, trigger global financial crises.',40)],[(0,r.cq)('Why is Amazon\'s effective tax rate significantly below the 21% statutory rate?',['Amazon illegally avoids taxes','Amazon legally uses accelerated depreciation, R&D credits, stock compensation deductions, and loss carryforwards, all provisions Congress created to incentivize investment and innovation','Amazon doesn\'t earn any profits','The 21% rate doesn\'t apply to tech companies'],1,'Every element of Amazon\'s tax strategy uses legislated incentives. Accelerated depreciation encourages capital investment. R&D credits encourage innovation. These are policy tools, not loopholes.'),(0,r.cq)('A company has $500M in debt at 6% interest and a 21% tax rate. What is the annual interest tax shield?',['$30M','$6.3M','$105M','$500M'],0,'Interest expense: $500M \xd7 6% = $30M. Tax shield: $30M \xd7 21% = $6.3M. Wait, the question asks for the tax shield: $30M \xd7 0.21 = $6.3M annually. The $6.3M in tax savings is why debt is cheaper than equity after taxes.'),(0,r.cq)('What is the difference between tax avoidance and tax evasion?',['They are the same thing','Avoidance is legal (structuring transactions to minimize tax within the law); evasion is illegal (hiding income, fake deductions, fraud)','Avoidance is only for large companies','Evasion is when you pay more than required'],1,'Tax avoidance = using legal provisions (deductions, credits, entity structures) to minimize tax. Tax evasion = hiding income or fabricating deductions. The line can be blurry, which is why tax law is a massive industry.'),(0,r.cq)('In M&A, why does an asset purchase often provide better tax economics than a stock purchase?',['Asset purchases are faster','An asset purchase gives the buyer a stepped-up tax basis, creating higher depreciation deductions over time, potentially worth 5-10% of deal value in tax savings','Stock purchases are illegal','There is no tax difference'],1,'The stepped-up basis allows the buyer to depreciate assets at fair market value rather than historical cost. On a $2B acquisition, this can create $500M-$1B in additional deductions, saving $100-200M in taxes.'),(0,r.cq)('The TCJA (2017) reduced the US corporate rate from 35% to 21%. What was the approximate impact on after-tax earnings?',['No impact','Approximately 20% increase in after-tax earnings for most companies','50% increase','Earnings decreased'],1,'At 35%: $100 pre-tax \u2192 $65 after-tax. At 21%: $100 pre-tax \u2192 $79 after-tax. Increase: ($79-$65)/$65 = 21.5%. This ~20% boost to after-tax earnings was a primary driver of the 2018-2019 stock market rally.')]),(0,r.L)(24,'Financial Engineering & Structured Products',45,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Financial Engineering & Structured Products','In 2005, a team at JPMorgan invented the credit default swap (CDS), a derivative that let investors buy insurance against bond defaults. By 2007, the CDS market had grown to $62 trillion, larger than the entire world\'s GDP. When the housing market collapsed, these instruments amplified losses across the global financial system, turning a US housing problem into a worldwide crisis that destroyed $10 trillion in wealth. Financial engineering is the most powerful, and most dangerous, tool in finance.','By the end of this lesson, you\'ll understand securitization, derivatives engineering, structured products, and how these tools can either manage risk brilliantly or create systemic catastrophe when misused.','Securitization flow: Individual Mortgages \u2192 Pool \u2192 Tranches (AAA/AA/BBB/Equity) \u2192 Investors',{type:'metric',title:'CDS Market by 2007',value:'$62 trillion',detail:'Larger than the entire world\'s GDP',footer:'Financial engineering amplified a US housing problem globally',sentiment:'negative'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Financial engineering misused',detail:'Collateralized Debt Obligations (CDOs) packaged subprime mortgages into securities rated AAA by rating agencies. When housing prices fell 30%, the "safe" senior tranches suffered massive losses. CDOs amplified the housing bust into a global financial crisis, the most destructive example of financial engineering in history.'},{label:'Financial engineering used well',detail:'Mortgage-backed securities (MBS), when properly structured, allow banks to originate loans, sell them to investors, and recycle capital for more lending. This securitization model funded the expansion of homeownership from 40% to 65% of American families over 50 years. The tool works, the abuse doesn\'t.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'metric',title:'CDOs Misused',value:'AAA \u2192 massive loss',detail:'Subprime mortgages packaged as "safe" senior tranches',footer:'CDOs amplified the housing bust into a global crisis',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'MBS: Engineering Used Well',points:[{label:'Past',value:'40% owned'},{label:'Today',value:'65% owned'}],trend:'up',footer:'Securitization funded 50 years of expanding homeownership',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('SECURITIZATION','Turning Illiquid Assets into Tradeable Securities',['Securitization is the process of pooling individual financial assets (mortgages, auto loans, credit card receivables) and issuing securities backed by those pools. The bank originates loans, sells them to a Special Purpose Vehicle (SPV), which issues bonds to investors. The bank gets cash to make more loans; investors get securities backed by the loan payments.','Tranching is the innovation that makes securitization powerful, and dangerous. A pool of mortgages is divided into tranches (slices) with different risk levels: Senior tranches (AAA-rated) absorb losses last and receive the lowest yield. Mezzanine tranches (A to BBB) absorb losses next. Equity/first-loss tranches absorb losses first but receive the highest yield. This structure allows risk-averse investors (pension funds) and risk-seeking investors (hedge funds) to participate in the same pool.','The "originate to distribute" model created moral hazard: banks that sell loans have less incentive to ensure borrower quality than banks that hold loans. In 2005-2007, mortgage originators approved loans to borrowers who clearly couldn\'t pay (NINJA loans, No Income, No Job, No Assets) because they would sell the loans immediately. This breakdown in underwriting standards was the root cause of the 2008 crisis.'],[{term:'Securitization',definition:'Pooling financial assets (loans) and issuing securities backed by the pool\'s cash flows, allowing banks to transfer risk and recycle capital.',example:'A bank pools 10,000 mortgages ($3B) into a mortgage-backed security and sells bonds to investors, receiving $3B in cash to make new loans.'},{term:'Tranching',definition:'Dividing a securitized pool into layers with different risk/return profiles. Senior tranches are safest (lowest yield); equity tranches absorb first losses (highest yield).',example:'A $1B MBS: $700M AAA-rated senior (3% yield), $200M BBB mezzanine (6% yield), $100M equity tranche (15% yield, absorbs first 10% of losses).'},{term:'SPV (Special Purpose Vehicle)',definition:'A legally separate entity created to hold securitized assets, isolating them from the originator\'s bankruptcy risk.',example:'JPMorgan creates "JPM Mortgage Trust 2024-1" to hold pooled mortgages, if JPMorgan fails, the trust\'s assets are protected.'},{term:'Moral Hazard',definition:'When one party takes excessive risk because another party bears the consequences. In securitization, originators who sell loans don\'t bear default risk.',example:'Countrywide approved NINJA loans because it sold them immediately, the default risk was transferred to MBS investors, not Countrywide.'}],'Securitization chain: Borrowers \u2192 Originator (bank) \u2192 SPV \u2192 Tranches (Senior AAA / Mezzanine BBB / Equity) \u2192 Investors',void 0,{type:'stack',title:'Tranching a $1B Mortgage Pool',total:'$1B Pool',segments:[{label:'Senior (AAA)',value:'$700M',percent:70,color:'#10B981'},{label:'Mezzanine (BBB)',value:'$200M',percent:20,color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Equity (first loss)',value:'$100M',percent:10,color:'#EF4444'}],footer:'Equity absorbs losses first; senior is paid last',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('A $1B mortgage pool is structured into three tranches: $700M senior (AAA), $200M mezzanine (BBB), $100M equity. If 8% of mortgages default ($80M in losses), who absorbs it?',['Everyone loses proportionally','The equity tranche absorbs all $80M, senior and mezzanine are protected','Only the senior tranche is affected','The bank that originated the mortgages'],1,'Correct! The equity tranche absorbs the first $100M of losses. At $80M in defaults, the equity tranche loses 80% of its value ($80M/$100M), but senior ($700M) and mezzanine ($200M) are completely unscathed. This is why senior tranches earn AAA ratings.','Loss waterfall: Equity absorbs first $100M \u2192 Mezzanine absorbs next $200M \u2192 Senior absorbs last. At $80M total losses, only equity is hit (80% loss). If losses reached $150M, equity is wiped out and mezzanine loses $50M.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('THE CDO CRISIS','How Structured Products Amplified the Housing Bust','comparison','Adjust the default rate and recovery rate to see how different tranche holders are affected, and how quickly "safe" senior tranches can suffer losses.',['CDOs (Collateralized Debt Obligations) took securitization to the next level, and over the cliff. CDOs pooled tranches from MULTIPLE mortgage-backed securities, creating "CDOs of MBS." Then CDO-squared products pooled CDO tranches. This layering multiplied the leverage and correlation risk: when housing prices fell broadly, the "diversification" assumed in the models evaporated.','The rating agency failure was critical: Moody\'s and S&P rated 83% of CDO tranches AAA, the same rating as US government bonds. Their models assumed housing prices would never decline nationally. When they did, $300B+ in "AAA" securities suffered losses. The ratings created false confidence, allowing pension funds and sovereign wealth funds to buy what were actually high-risk instruments.','Credit Default Swaps (CDS) amplified the crisis further: investors could bet against mortgage securities without owning them. AIG sold $440 billion in CDS "insurance" on mortgage securities without holding capital reserves. When defaults spiked, AIG couldn\'t pay claims, requiring a $182 billion government bailout. Michael Burry and Steve Eisman (of "The Big Short") bought CDS to bet against subprime mortgages and earned billions.'],[{term:'CDO',definition:'Collateralized Debt Obligation, a structured product that pools and tranches securities (often MBS tranches), creating leveraged exposure to the underlying assets.',example:'A CDO might pool the mezzanine tranches from 100 different MBS deals, then tranche that pool into its own AAA/BBB/equity slices.'},{term:'Credit Default Swap (CDS)',definition:'A derivative contract where one party pays a premium to receive protection against a bond default, essentially insurance on bonds.',example:'Michael Burry paid 2%/year in CDS premiums to insure $1.3B in subprime MBS. When defaults spiked, his CDS paid out $800M in profit.'}],['$300B+ in AAA-rated CDO tranches suffered losses during the crisis, these were supposed to be "as safe as government bonds."','AIG sold $440B in CDS protection without adequate reserves, requiring a $182B government bailout.','The Big Short: Michael Burry earned $800M by buying CDS against subprime mortgages, betting that financial engineering had created a house of cards.'],[{label:'Mortgage Default Rate (%)',min:1,max:40,default:5,unit:'%'},{label:'Recovery Rate (\xa2/$1)',min:20,max:80,default:50,unit:'\xa2'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('MODERN FINANCIAL ENGINEERING','Tools That Shape Finance Today',['Post-crisis financial engineering has evolved: simpler structures, more transparency, and better regulation. CLOs (Collateralized Loan Obligations), the corporate loan equivalent of MBS, have become the largest structured product market at $1T+. CLOs performed well during COVID partly because they incorporated lessons from the MBS crisis: better underwriting, simpler structures, and more conservative models.','Derivatives for corporate risk management remain essential: interest rate swaps (exchanging fixed for floating rates, $400T+ notional outstanding), currency forwards (locking exchange rates for international transactions), and commodity futures (hedging input costs). These instruments, when used for hedging rather than speculation, reduce corporate risk and enable more confident capital allocation.','The frontier of financial engineering: tokenization (representing traditional assets on blockchain), parametric insurance (automatic payouts triggered by measurable events like earthquake magnitude), and AI-driven structured products. Each carries the same dual nature, tremendous potential for risk management and efficiency, but also potential for complexity and abuse if not properly regulated and understood.'],[{term:'CLO',definition:'Collateralized Loan Obligation, a structured product that pools corporate loans and issues tranches, similar to MBS but with corporate rather than mortgage loans.',example:'A CLO pools 200 leveraged loans from PE-backed companies, issues AAA-rated senior tranches yielding 5% and equity tranches yielding 15-20%.'},{term:'Interest Rate Swap',definition:'A derivative where two parties exchange interest rate cash flows, typically fixed for floating, to manage rate risk.',example:'A company with floating-rate debt swaps to fixed rate: it pays 4% fixed and receives SOFR floating, locking in its interest cost.'}],'Evolution: Simple Bonds \u2192 MBS/ABS (1980s) \u2192 CDOs (1990s) \u2192 Crisis (2008) \u2192 Simpler CLOs, Better Regulation (2010s) \u2192 Tokenization/AI (2020s)',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'Evolution of Financial Engineering',nodes:[{label:'Simple Bonds'},{label:'MBS/ABS (1980s)'},{label:'CDOs (1990s)'},{label:'Crisis (2008)'},{label:'Simpler CLOs (2010s)'},{label:'Tokenization/AI (2020s)'}],footer:'The tool is neutral, impact depends on how it\'s used',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.fb)('The process of pooling loans and issuing securities backed by those loan payments is called ___.',['securitization','consolidation','aggregation','syndication'],0),(0,r.q)('AIG sold $440B in Credit Default Swaps without adequate capital reserves. When mortgage defaults spiked, what happened?',['AIG earned record profits','AIG couldn\'t pay CDS claims and required a $182B government bailout, the largest corporate rescue in US history, because AIG\'s failure would have cascaded through the entire financial system','AIG restructured its debt','Nothing, CDS are risk-free'],1,'AIG treated CDS premiums as free income, never expecting to pay claims. When the housing market collapsed, AIG owed far more than it could pay. The government stepped in because AIG\'s counterparties (every major bank) would have faced catastrophic losses if AIG defaulted.'),(0,r.tr)('Financial Engineering Essentials',[['What is securitization?','Pooling financial assets and issuing securities backed by the pool\'s cash flows. Banks transfer risk and recycle capital; investors access diversified asset-backed securities.'],['How does tranching work?','A pool is divided into layers: senior (safest, lowest yield, last to absorb losses), mezzanine (moderate), and equity (riskiest, highest yield, first to absorb losses).'],['What caused the 2008 CDO crisis?','CDOs of subprime MBS were rated AAA by agencies using flawed models. When housing prices fell broadly, "safe" tranches suffered massive losses, $300B+ in AAA securities.'],['What is a Credit Default Swap?','Insurance on bonds, one party pays premiums, the other pays if the bond defaults. AIG sold $440B in CDS without reserves, requiring a $182B bailout.']]),(0,r.sc)('A bank proposes creating a CDO from your company\'s corporate loans. They\'ll package 500 loans, tranche them, and sell the tranches to investors. This frees up $2B in capital. But you\'d lose the ongoing relationship with borrowers. What do you recommend?',[{label:'Securitize everything, $2B in freed capital is too valuable to pass up',outcome:'The 2008 crisis started with exactly this reasoning. When originators sell all loans, they lose incentive to maintain underwriting standards (moral hazard). Freed capital is only valuable if the originate-to-distribute model maintains credit quality. Full securitization without retention is dangerous.',correct:!1},{label:'Securitize 60-70% but retain the equity tranche (first-loss position), this aligns incentives by keeping the bank exposed to credit quality while still freeing significant capital',outcome:'This is the post-crisis best practice, now mandated by Dodd-Frank "risk retention" rules. By keeping the equity tranche, the bank retains skin in the game, ensuring it only securitizes loans it believes in. You free $1.2-1.4B while maintaining credit discipline.',correct:!0},{label:'Don\'t securitize, keep all loans on the balance sheet',outcome:'Overly conservative. Securitization is a valuable tool when done responsibly. Retaining 100% of loans ties up capital that could fund new lending. The answer isn\'t avoidance, it\'s disciplined use with proper risk retention.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'Senior tranches absorb losses first in a securitized structure',a:!1},{s:'CDOs were a major contributor to the 2008 financial crisis',a:!0},{s:'Moral hazard occurs when loan originators bear no default risk',a:!0},{s:'Credit Default Swaps are always speculative instruments',a:!1},{s:'The CLO market has grown to over $1 trillion post-crisis',a:!0}]),(0,r.m)('Match the Structured Product',[['MBS','Securities backed by pools of mortgages'],['CDO','Securities backed by other securities, layered risk'],['CLO','Securities backed by pools of corporate loans'],['CDS','Insurance-like contracts against bond defaults'],['SPV','Legal entity created to isolate securitized assets']]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Financial Engineering & Structured Products',[{takeaway:'Securitization is powerful but requires aligned incentives',detail:'When originators retain skin in the game (equity tranche), securitization efficiently distributes risk. When they don\'t (originate-to-distribute), moral hazard leads to deteriorating quality, as the 2008 crisis proved.'},{takeaway:'Tranching creates different risk/return profiles from the same pool',detail:'Senior tranches are protected by subordination. Equity tranches absorb first losses for higher yield. The structure works until losses exceed models, which happened catastrophically with subprime CDOs.'},{takeaway:'Financial engineering is a tool, its impact depends on how it\'s used',detail:'MBS funded American homeownership. Interest rate swaps manage $400T+ in risk. But CDOs and unregulated CDS amplified a housing downturn into a global crisis. Understanding the tools and their limitations is essential.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Financial Engineering & Structured Products','Corporate Finance Advanced Capstone','You\'ll synthesize everything you\'ve learned across all 24 lessons into a comprehensive capstone that tests your ability to analyze, value, and make strategic corporate finance decisions.',40)],[(0,r.cq)('What is securitization and why is it useful?',['A type of stock offering','Pooling financial assets (loans) and issuing securities backed by those pools, it allows banks to transfer risk, recycle capital for new lending, and gives investors access to diversified credit exposure','A way to hide bad loans','Government-mandated loan restructuring'],1,'Securitization efficiently connects borrowers (who need loans) with investors (who want yield). Banks originate, package, and distribute, recycling capital to originate more. The model works when incentives are aligned.'),(0,r.cq)('In a $1B MBS with 70% senior, 20% mezzanine, and 10% equity tranches, what happens if defaults cause $120M in losses?',['Everyone loses 12%','Equity tranche is wiped out ($100M loss), mezzanine loses $20M (10% of its $200M value), senior tranche is untouched','Only senior is affected','The bank absorbs all losses'],1,'Loss waterfall: Equity absorbs first $100M (wiped out at 100% loss). Remaining $20M hits mezzanine ($20M/$200M = 10% loss). Senior ($700M) is untouched because total losses haven\'t breached the $300M subordination buffer.'),(0,r.cq)('What was the fundamental flaw in pre-crisis CDO ratings?',['Rating agencies didn\'t exist','Models assumed housing prices would never decline nationally, when they did, the assumed diversification evaporated and $300B+ in "AAA" securities suffered massive losses','CDOs were never rated','The ratings were correct'],1,'Rating models assumed low correlation between regional housing markets. When a national decline hit, ALL underlying mortgages deteriorated simultaneously, the diversification that justified AAA ratings was illusory.'),(0,r.cq)('Why did AIG\'s CDS exposure require a $182B government bailout?',['AIG had bad management','AIG sold $440B in Credit Default Swap protection without adequate capital reserves. When defaults spiked, AIG couldn\'t pay claims. A default would have cascaded to every major bank holding AIG CDS contracts.','AIG invested in the stock market','AIG\'s insurance business failed'],1,'AIG treated CDS premiums as revenue without reserving for potential claims. Its counterparties (Goldman, Deutsche Bank, etc.) depended on AIG to pay if bonds defaulted. AIG\'s failure would have created a chain reaction through the global financial system.'),(0,r.cq)('What is "risk retention" in post-crisis securitization regulation?',['A tax on securitized products','Dodd-Frank requires securitization originators to retain at least 5% of the credit risk (typically the equity tranche), ensuring they have skin in the game and maintain underwriting standards','Banks must retain all loans','Investors must hold securities for 5 years'],1,'Risk retention addresses the moral hazard that caused the crisis: when originators had zero exposure to default, they approved terrible loans. Requiring 5% retention (especially the first-loss equity tranche) aligns incentives.')]),(0,r.L)(25,'Corporate Finance Advanced Capstone',50,[(0,r.hookOpener)('Corporate Finance Advanced Capstone','You\'ve now studied every major domain of corporate finance, from financial statements and time value of money to securitization and international tax strategy. In this capstone, you\'ll synthesize all 24 previous lessons into an integrated framework. Real corporate finance decisions don\'t come neatly labeled "capital structure" or "valuation", they require pulling from every tool simultaneously. This lesson tests whether you can think like a CFO.','By the end of this capstone, you\'ll demonstrate mastery of integrated corporate finance analysis, combining valuation, capital structure, risk management, governance, tax, and strategic decision-making into cohesive recommendations.','Integrated framework diagram: All 24 lesson topics flowing into the CFO\'s decision-making nexus',{type:'grid',title:'The Integrated CFO Framework',items:[{label:'Valuation',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Capital Structure',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Risk Management',color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Tax & Governance',color:'#10B981'}],footer:'Real decisions pull from every tool simultaneously',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.stakesCard)({label:'Siloed corporate finance thinking',detail:'GE under Jack Welch became a case study in fragmented finance: aggressive M&A without integration planning, financial engineering that masked operational weakness, tax strategies that created complexity, and compensation that rewarded quarterly EPS above all else. By 2018, GE had lost $200B+ in market value as decades of disconnected financial decisions unraveled.'},{label:'Integrated corporate finance mastery',detail:'Satya Nadella\'s Microsoft combined every corporate finance lever: strategic M&A ($69B Activision, $26B LinkedIn), optimal capital structure ($77B debt at record-low rates), massive shareholder returns ($100B+ in buybacks), tax-efficient international operations, and compensation aligned with cloud transformation. Market cap grew from $300B to $3T, a 10x return in 10 years.'},{withoutVisual:{type:'line',title:'GE: Siloed Finance Thinking',points:[{label:'2000 peak',value:'$500B'},{label:'2008 crisis',value:'$200B'},{label:'2018',value:'$80B'}],trend:'down',footer:'Decades of disconnected decisions lost $200B+',sentiment:'negative'},withVisual:{type:'line',title:'Microsoft: Integrated Mastery',points:[{label:'2014',value:'$300B'},{label:'2019',value:'$1T'},{label:'2024',value:'$3T'}],trend:'up',footer:'Every corporate finance lever pulled together, 10x in 10 years',sentiment:'positive'}}),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE INTEGRATED FRAMEWORK','Connecting All the Pieces',['A CFO faces simultaneous, interconnected decisions: How much to invest? (Capital budgeting, NPV, WACC hurdle rate). How to finance it? (Capital structure, debt vs. equity, bond markets, tax shields). How much to return to shareholders? (Dividends, buybacks, when stock is undervalued). How to manage risk? (Hedging, treasury management, ESG). How to comply and govern? (SOX, board oversight, executive compensation). These are not separate topics, they are one integrated system.','The capital allocation framework prioritizes: (1) Invest in all positive-NPV projects (earn above WACC). (2) Maintain optimal capital structure (minimize WACC through the right debt/equity mix). (3) Manage risk (hedge material exposures, maintain liquidity). (4) Return excess cash to shareholders (buybacks if undervalued, dividends if consistent). Every dollar of cash flow should flow through this hierarchy.','Valuation connects everything: company value equals future free cash flows discounted at WACC. FCF depends on investment decisions and tax strategy. WACC depends on capital structure and risk. Growth depends on M&A strategy and capital budgeting. Governance ensures decisions serve shareholders. Every topic in this course directly feeds into the valuation equation.'],[{term:'Capital Allocation',definition:'The strategic process of deciding how to deploy a company\'s financial resources across investment, financing, risk management, and shareholder returns.',example:'Microsoft allocates: 30% to R&D/CapEx, 25% to M&A, 15% to debt service, 30% to buybacks and dividends, optimized for growth and shareholder returns.'},{term:'Integrated Financial Strategy',definition:'Coordinating all financial decisions (investment, financing, risk, return) as an interconnected system rather than isolated choices.',example:'Apple\'s strategy: invest in R&D (iPhone, chips), finance with cheap debt (AA+ rated, 2.4% after-tax), hedge international revenue, return $600B+ to shareholders through buybacks and dividends.'}],'The CFO Nexus: Capital Budgeting + Capital Structure + Working Capital + Risk Management + Tax Strategy + Governance + Shareholder Returns = Enterprise Value',void 0,{type:'flow',title:'The CFO Nexus',nodes:[{label:'Capital Budgeting'},{label:'Capital Structure'},{label:'Working Capital'},{label:'Risk & Tax'},{label:'Governance & Returns'},{label:'Enterprise Value'}],footer:'These are not separate topics, they are one system',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.microCheck)('Microsoft acquired Activision for $69B. Which corporate finance concepts are directly relevant to this decision?',['Only valuation','Only capital structure','Valuation (DCF, comps, precedent transactions), capital structure (how to finance $69B), tax strategy (deal structuring), risk management (integration risk), governance (board approval, regulatory clearance), and working capital (cash flow impact)','Only M&A'],2,'Correct! A single major decision touches every domain: valuation (was $69B fair?), financing (cash vs. debt vs. equity), tax (asset vs. stock purchase, international implications), risk (regulatory approval, integration), governance (board fiduciary duty), and ongoing operations (working capital, treasury impact). Corporate finance is integrated.','A $69B acquisition is not just a "valuation" question or "capital structure" question, it requires simultaneous analysis across every corporate finance domain.'),(0,r.interactiveGraph)('CFO DECISION SIMULATOR','Integrating Multiple Finance Disciplines','comparison','Adjust multiple variables simultaneously to see how interconnected corporate finance decisions affect enterprise value.',['The best CFOs think in systems, not silos. When Apple decided to issue $17B in bonds despite having $145B in cash, it was simultaneously a capital structure decision (adding debt), a tax decision (avoiding 35% repatriation tax), a shareholder return decision (using proceeds for buybacks), and a market timing decision (locking in historically low rates). Each dimension reinforced the others.','The anti-pattern is "analysis paralysis" or siloed thinking. A company that optimizes tax structure without considering the impact on its credit rating, or that maximizes buybacks without maintaining an adequate liquidity cushion, or that pursues growth acquisitions without considering integration cost and cultural fit, these fragmented approaches destroy value even when each individual decision seems rational.','The capstone skill is the ability to identify trade-offs and second-order effects. Adding debt reduces WACC (good) but increases bankruptcy risk (bad) and may restrict operational flexibility through covenants (bad). An acquisition creates synergies (good) but requires integration spending (bad) and may distract management (bad). The CFO\'s job is to weigh all dimensions simultaneously and find the net-positive path.'],[{term:'Second-Order Effects',definition:'The indirect consequences of a financial decision that may not be obvious from first-principles analysis.',example:'Aggressive cost-cutting improves margins (first-order) but may degrade product quality, losing customers over 2-3 years (second-order).'}],['Apple\'s integrated strategy: $145B cash + $17B bonds + $600B buybacks + international tax optimization = $300B to $3T market cap growth.','Microsoft under Nadella: Cloud pivot + Activision M&A + optimal capital structure + aligned compensation = $300B to $3T in 10 years.','GE\'s siloed approach: Aggressive M&A + financial engineering + earnings management + short-term compensation = $500B to $80B in 20 years.'],[{label:'WACC (%)',min:5,max:15,default:9,unit:'%'},{label:'Revenue Growth (%)',min:0,max:30,default:10,unit:'%'}]),(0,r.teachingSlide)('THE CFO MINDSET','Thinking Like a Corporate Finance Leader',['The CFO is the bridge between operations and financial markets. Every operational decision has financial implications (building a factory affects CapEx, depreciation, and FCF). Every financial decision has operational implications (taking on debt restricts flexibility through covenants). The best CFOs understand both worlds and translate between them.','Common pitfalls that destroy value: (1) Optimizing for a single metric (EPS) while ignoring balance sheet health. (2) Ignoring risk until a crisis forces attention. (3) Treating tax, treasury, and risk management as back-office functions rather than strategic capabilities. (4) Allowing short-term compensation incentives to drive long-term strategic decisions. (5) Failing to maintain excess financial capacity (cash, credit lines) for opportunistic acquisitions during downturns.','The ultimate test of corporate finance mastery: can you evaluate a complex situation, like a potential acquisition during an economic downturn funded by junk bond issuance in a foreign currency, and identify all relevant dimensions (valuation, financing, risk, tax, governance, regulatory), assess their interactions, and make a recommendation that maximizes long-term shareholder value? That is what this course has prepared you to do.'],[{term:'Financial Flexibility',definition:'Maintaining unused debt capacity, cash reserves, and credit facilities to capitalize on opportunities (discounted acquisitions, investment in downturns) when competitors are constrained.',example:'JPMorgan\'s $50B+ excess capital allowed it to acquire First Republic during the 2023 banking crisis, an opportunity available only to the financially prepared.'}],'The Integrated CFO: Strategist (what to invest in) + Architect (how to finance it) + Guardian (how to protect it) + Communicator (how to explain it to markets)',void 0,{type:'grid',title:'The CFO Mindset',items:[{label:'Strategist',value:'What to invest in',color:'#A451E6'},{label:'Architect',value:'How to finance it',color:'#3B82F6'},{label:'Guardian',value:'How to protect it',color:'#F59E0B'},{label:'Communicator',value:'How to explain it',color:'#10B981'}],footer:'Financial flexibility lets you act when competitors can\'t',sentiment:'positive'}),(0,r.simulationFinale)('Chief Financial Officer','You\'re the newly appointed CFO of a $5B revenue technology company. The company has $1B in cash, $2B in debt (at 5%), growing revenue at 15%/year, and a stock price of $100. The board presents three strategic priorities requiring your integrated analysis.',[{prompt:'Priority 1: A competitor is available for acquisition at $3B (8x EBITDA). Your DCF says fair value is $2.5B. Comparable transactions average 9x. The deal would add $500M in revenue with $80M in synergies. How do you evaluate this?',options:[{label:'Bid $3B immediately, the synergies make it worthwhile and a competitor might outbid us',consequence:'Overpaying by $500M above intrinsic value to capture $80M/year in synergies doesn\'t create enough value. Even at $80M perpetual synergies discounted at 10%, synergy value is $800M. Fair price with synergies: $2.5B + $800M = $3.3B max. $3B is defensible but you should negotiate, not accept immediately.',score:1},{label:'Start negotiations at $2.5B (your DCF value), acknowledging you\'d pay up to $3.3B (DCF + discounted synergies), and structure as an asset deal for tax benefits',consequence:'Excellent integrated analysis. You anchored to intrinsic value ($2.5B), quantified synergies ($800M NPV), set a maximum ($3.3B), and considered tax structuring (asset deal for stepped-up basis, potentially worth $200M+ in tax savings). This is how CFOs negotiate acquisitions.',score:3},{label:'Pass entirely, $3B is above your DCF value',consequence:'Too rigid. Synergies are real ($80M/year = $800M NPV) and the strategic value of eliminating a competitor is meaningful. A price between $2.5B-$3.3B can create shareholder value. Walking away from every deal above DCF means missing strategic opportunities.',score:1}]},{prompt:'Priority 2: To fund the acquisition and growth, you need $2B in capital. Your options: (A) Issue $2B in stock at $100/share, (B) Issue $2B in bonds at 6%, (C) Issue $1B in convertible bonds at 4% and $1B in stock. Current WACC is 9%. Your stock seems fairly valued.',options:[{label:'Issue $2B in stock, no added debt risk',consequence:'At $100/share, issuing 20M shares dilutes existing shareholders by 10-15%. If the stock is fairly valued, equity issuance doesn\'t leave money on the table, but the cost of equity (~12%) is more expensive than debt (6% \xd7 0.79 = 4.7% after-tax). Pure equity also signals that management thinks the stock may be overvalued.',score:1},{label:'Issue $1B convertible bonds (4%) + $1B stock, balanced approach that minimizes dilution, captures partial tax shield, and manages risk with moderate additional leverage',consequence:'Strong balanced approach. The convertible bonds at 4% are cheaper than straight bonds (6%) because of the equity conversion feature. This raises $2B with less dilution than pure equity, captures some tax shield, and keeps leverage manageable. Your debt/capital ratio moves from 40% to ~45%, within optimal range.',score:3},{label:'Issue $2B in bonds at 6%, maximize the tax shield and avoid dilution',consequence:'Aggressive. This takes debt from $2B to $4B (debt/capital ~57%). The interest tax shield saves ~$252M/year in taxes, but $240M in annual interest significantly reduces financial flexibility. If the acquisition underperforms or a recession hits, the high leverage becomes dangerous.',score:1}]},{prompt:'Priority 3: The board asks about capital return strategy. You have $1B in cash post-fundraising, $250M in annual FCF, and the stock trades at $100 with your DCF suggesting $115 intrinsic value. What do you recommend?',options:[{label:'Return all excess cash via special dividend, shareholders decide how to reinvest',consequence:'Dividends are tax-inefficient (taxed immediately) and set expectations (shareholders expect future dividends). With the stock 13% undervalued ($100 vs. $115 intrinsic), buybacks would create more value per dollar spent.',score:0},{label:'Announce a $500M buyback program, the stock is 13% undervalued, so each dollar of buyback acquires $1.13 of intrinsic value. Maintain $500M as a strategic reserve for opportunistic investments during any market downturn.',consequence:'Perfect integrated thinking. You recognized the stock is undervalued (buyback opportunity), sized the program to maintain financial flexibility ($500M reserve), and preserved optionality for future investments during downturns. This is CFO-level capital allocation.',score:3},{label:'Hold all cash, you might need it for future acquisitions',consequence:'Hoarding cash when the stock is undervalued wastes shareholder value. $1B earning 4% in money markets versus $500M buying stock at a 13% discount to intrinsic value. The buyback generates better returns. Keeping some reserve is wise; keeping everything is excessive.',score:1}]}],'Integrating valuation (DCF-anchored negotiation), capital structure (balanced convertible + equity), and capital allocation (buyback of undervalued stock + strategic reserve), scoring 9/9.','The capstone demonstrates that corporate finance mastery means integrating all disciplines simultaneously: valuation informs acquisition pricing, capital structure informs financing choices, tax strategy informs deal structuring, and capital allocation informs shareholder return decisions. No dimension operates in isolation, and the best CFOs see the full picture.'),(0,r.fb)('The process of deciding how to deploy a company\'s financial resources across investment, financing, risk, and shareholder returns is called capital ___.',['budgeting','allocation','structuring','raising'],1),(0,r.q)('GE destroyed $200B+ in value through siloed financial decisions. Microsoft created $2.7T under Nadella through integrated strategy. What is the key lesson?',['Microsoft was in a better industry','Integrated corporate finance strategy, where investment, financing, risk management, tax, governance, and compensation decisions reinforce each other, creates exponentially more value than optimizing any single dimension in isolation','Microsoft had better products','Nadella was a better CEO'],1,'GE optimized individual metrics (quarterly EPS through financial engineering) while ignoring system-level effects (balance sheet fragility, operational decline). Microsoft coordinated all financial dimensions toward a coherent strategic vision. The integration is the edge.'),(0,r.m)('Match the Corporate Finance Domain to Its Core Question',[['Capital Budgeting','Which projects create value above the cost of capital?'],['Capital Structure','What is the optimal mix of debt and equity?'],['Valuation','What is the company or asset truly worth?'],['Risk Management','How do we protect against financial uncertainty?'],['Shareholder Returns','How much cash should be returned to owners?']]),(0,r.ds)('Order the capital allocation hierarchy:',['Invest in all positive-NPV projects (earn above WACC)','Maintain optimal capital structure (minimize WACC)','Manage risk (hedge material exposures, maintain liquidity)','Build strategic reserves for opportunistic investments','Return excess cash to shareholders (buybacks if undervalued, dividends if not)']),(0,r.sc)('Economic conditions are deteriorating. Your company has $2B in cash, a strong balance sheet, and competitors are struggling. What is the integrated corporate finance response?',[{label:'Cut costs aggressively, halt all investments, hoard cash',outcome:'This preserves cash but forfeits the opportunity downturn provides. Competitors are weakened, acquisition targets are cheap, and financial strength is a competitive advantage. Pure defense is a waste of a strong balance sheet.',correct:!1},{label:'Maintain investments in core R&D, pursue discounted acquisitions of distressed competitors, continue moderate buybacks (stock is likely undervalued in a downturn), and keep 6 months of cash reserves for worst-case scenarios',outcome:'This is integrated CFO thinking at its finest. You play offense (cheap acquisitions, undervalued buybacks) and defense (cash reserves, continued investment) simultaneously. Financial strength in a downturn is the ultimate competitive advantage.',correct:!0},{label:'Increase leverage aggressively to fund acquisitions, rates are dropping',outcome:'Adding significant debt during a downturn is reckless. Revenue may decline, making debt service harder. The right approach uses existing financial strength (cash, credit lines) rather than creating new risk through aggressive leverage during uncertain times.',correct:!1}]),(0,r.tf)([{s:'The best CFOs think about financial decisions as an integrated system',a:!0},{s:'Optimizing a single financial metric always maximizes shareholder value',a:!1},{s:'Financial flexibility, unused debt capacity and cash reserves, is a strategic asset',a:!0},{s:'Every corporate finance topic in this course directly connects to enterprise valuation',a:!0},{s:'M&A decisions only require valuation analysis',a:!1}]),(0,r.summaryCards)('Corporate Finance Advanced Capstone',[{takeaway:'Corporate finance is an integrated system, not separate topics',detail:'Investment, financing, risk management, tax, governance, and shareholder return decisions are deeply interconnected. A decision in any one area affects all others. The best CFOs see and manage these connections.'},{takeaway:'Capital allocation is the CFO\'s highest-value function',detail:'Every dollar must flow through the hierarchy: positive-NPV investments first, then optimal capital structure, then risk management, then shareholder returns. Disciplined capital allocation is the difference between Microsoft\'s $3T and GE\'s decline.'},{takeaway:'Financial strength in a downturn is the ultimate competitive advantage',detail:'Companies with cash reserves, low leverage, and strong credit can acquire distressed competitors, invest when others cut, and buy back undervalued stock. Preparation during good times funds opportunity during bad times.'}]),(0,r.whatsNext)('Corporate Finance Advanced Capstone','Business Strategy','You\'ve mastered corporate finance, the quantitative engine of business decisions. Next, you\'ll learn Business Strategy: how companies build sustainable competitive advantages, choose which markets to enter, and create the value that corporate finance manages and allocates.',50)],[(0,r.cq)('What is the capital allocation hierarchy for a well-managed company?',['Return cash first, then invest whatever is left','Invest in all positive-NPV projects first, maintain optimal capital structure, manage risk, build strategic reserves, then return excess cash to shareholders','Invest in whatever the CEO prefers','Minimize all spending and maximize cash hoarding'],1,'This hierarchy ensures every dollar is deployed to its highest-value use. Positive-NPV projects earn above cost of capital (value-creating). Optimal capital structure minimizes WACC. Risk management protects value. Only truly excess cash should be returned.'),(0,r.cq)('Why did Microsoft\'s integrated strategy create $2.7T in value while GE\'s siloed approach destroyed $200B+?',['Microsoft was lucky','Microsoft coordinated all financial decisions, M&A, capital structure, tax, compensation, and shareholder returns, toward a coherent cloud transformation strategy. GE optimized individual metrics (EPS) while ignoring system-level effects (balance sheet fragility, operational decline).','Microsoft had more revenue','GE was in the wrong industry'],1,'Integration is the key. Microsoft\'s $69B Activision deal, debt issuance at record-low rates, $100B+ buybacks, and cloud-aligned compensation all reinforced each other. GE\'s earnings management, aggressive M&A, and short-term bonuses worked against each other.'),(0,r.cq)('A company has a $3T acquisition target with DCF value of $2.5B and $800M in synergy NPV. What is the maximum price that creates shareholder value?',['$2.5B','$3.3B, the DCF value ($2.5B) plus the synergy value ($800M) is the maximum before the deal destroys value','$3.5B','There is no maximum'],1,'Maximum value-creating price = intrinsic value + synergy value = $2.5B + $800M = $3.3B. Above $3.3B, the acquirer is paying more than the target is worth even including synergies, destroying shareholder value.'),(0,r.cq)('Your stock trades at $100 but your DCF says $115. What shareholder return strategy is optimal?',['Pay a special dividend','Buybacks, each dollar spent acquires $1.15 in intrinsic value, creating a 15% return for remaining shareholders. Buybacks are most value-creating when the stock is undervalued.','Hold cash and wait','Issue more stock'],1,'When stock is undervalued, buybacks are the highest-return use of capital. $100 spent buys $115 of intrinsic value, a 15% instant return for remaining shareholders. This is why Buffett buys back Berkshire stock when it\'s below intrinsic value.'),(0,r.cq)('What makes financial flexibility a "strategic asset" during economic downturns?',['Companies can pay higher dividends','Companies with cash reserves and unused debt capacity can acquire distressed competitors at discounts, invest when others cut back, and buy undervalued stock, turning financial preparation into competitive advantage','Flexibility means avoiding all risk','It allows companies to avoid bankruptcy'],1,'JPMorgan acquired First Republic during the 2023 crisis. Apple and Microsoft continued investing through COVID while competitors froze. Financial strength enables offensive action precisely when opportunities are greatest, during downturns when others are paralyzed.')])]},1866,[1877]);